Historical implementation log
=============================
The dated milestones below record earlier states, including gates and unfinished
work that later changed. For current fresh-session scope, implementation gates,
validation evidence and remaining live qualification, read README.5BSD and
INTEGRATION-GATES.5BSD.md. The live plan is tests/fresh-session-live-matrix.json;
its 22 cases remain unexecuted and do not authorize operating VMs or services.

HyperTrace experiment: import and daemon repair milestone
2026-10-13

Branch: experimental/hypertrace
Parent: dev at 1de2e37e9eff3a80d6605e546339f4ea4ebeb83f
Public upstream tip: 42466516653b1974ae8d8d3899e9d6972312322b
Last public commit: 2024-08-23 (committer timestamp).
No evidence of newer development on the imported public branch. This does
not establish whether private or other-branch development exists.

Completed
The complete upstream delta (1957 paths) is preserved, with two additional
license files, exact hashes, executable modes, and a binary-capable patch.
The vendor snapshot is an overlay against the pinned FreeBSD base; it does
not duplicate the entire operating system. upstream/ remains byte-identical
to the pinned revision. Local changes live in the ordered patches/ series.

0001 repairs connection close/destructor ownership, reference-underflow
checks, reversed reaper eligibility tests, unterminated peer identity strings,
partial writes, EINTR handling, SIGPIPE suppression and response status
semantics. Failed requests previously could send both NAK and ACK; the
patched dispatcher sends one result. Cleanup eligibility requires job cleanup
and zero references. This is not a proof of all event-loop lifetime safety.
The old __maybe_unused macro is renamed to avoid modern sys/cdefs.h collision.

0002 validates and bounds cleanup payloads before queuing work, parses the
received payload instead of reading from forwarding recipients, gives each
cleanup job ownership of its own entries, and frees those entries on teardown.
It releases SEND_INFO references, zeroes outgoing message headers, checks ELF
payload length and location termination, bounds input frames to 64 MiB, avoids
one-past-end vector indexing for header-only requests, and removes reads of an
uninitialized error variable in socket setup. Cleanup limits are 4096 names
of at most 4096 bytes each. These are explicit experimental resource limits;
the native-size_t protocol has NOT been made architecture-independent.

Validation
Run with a NEW, empty output directory under /usr/obj:
  python3.12 contrib/hypertrace/tests/run.py /usr/obj/hypertrace-check
The driver verifies vendor hashes, applies both patches to an isolated copy,
reproduces nine failing upstream connection cases, and runs eleven passing
patched connection checks. Actual socketpair I/O is used, with linker wrappers
injecting short writes/EINTR deterministically. The only substituted daemon
services are logging functions. No daemon or device is started.
It then compiles all eleven daemon translation units using all logical CPUs,
links dtraced, and runs cleanup-parser tests with address/undefined-behavior
sanitizers. Actual SEND_INFO and CLEANUP job ownership/reference lifetimes
are also exercised with sanitized connection/job code, linked to the real
daemon dependencies. The upstream Makefile's -Wno-gcc-compat allowance is retained for
Clang cleanup attributes; other build warnings are errors.
Successful results: /usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/qualified/
This is component qualification, not end-to-end daemon/VM qualification.

Remaining before a working 5BSD HyperTrace VM
1. Port the upstream libdtrace/DIF/ELF/type changes to the current compiler
   and retain existing providers, exporters and probe contracts. Build and
   run the upstream HyperTrace compiler tests against that port.
2. Integrate host/guest runtime hooks, tagged guest addresses, page-table
   walking and virtio transport with the current DTrace and bhyve interfaces.
   Review all guest lengths, types, addresses, quotas and ownership boundaries.
3. Implement guest reconnect/replay using explicit session generations and
   acknowledged program state. Retry must be idempotent; revoked programs
   must never replay. Test crash before/after acknowledgment, repeated reconnect,
   partial transfer, host daemon restart and simultaneous guests.
4. Integrate save/restore/reset: stop or drain tracing before snapshot, create
   a new restore generation, rebuild probe maps and clock anchors, and reject
   stale callbacks. Test snapshots with outstanding transport messages and
   actions, repeated restore, clone, teardown and a disconnected collector.
5. Qualify ordinary SDT/FBT first, then USDT and kinst explicitly. No blanket
   provider compatibility is established. Confirm guest/user memory faults
   remain contained, and exclude early boot and tracing recursion paths.
6. Finish the daemon protocol/concurrency audit: handshake framing/timeouts,
   message ordering, active-job re-enqueue after disconnect, kqueue event-batch
   pointer lifetime, allocation failure, file I/O completeness and socket-info
   snapshot races remain review areas. The existing synchronous control I/O
   can block; retrying EINTR does not solve deadlines or nonblocking framing.
7. Run matched host/guest builds in a disposable qualification environment.
   Measure VM exits, throughput, p99 application latency and trace loss under
   increasing event rates, comparing hypercalls with batched guest collection.

Nothing is wired into the default world/kernel build, installed, loaded or
started by this milestone. Neither reconnect replay nor VM save/restore is
claimed fixed. dev and the original dirty /usr/src checkout remain separate.

Probe inventories must exclude this unbuilt vendor comparison overlay; it
is not additional installed 5BSD probe coverage.

Continuation: bounded transfers and concurrent framing
0003 serializes each full outgoing job frame and ACK/NAK on the connection,
and builds client-info payload/count from the same locked snapshot. It uses
nonblocking per-call socket operations plus poll against a steady-clock
absolute deadline: 10 seconds per data transfer, 2 seconds per handshake
transfer. Slow progress or EINTR does not reset that deadline. Data-transfer
failure shuts down the stream so a partial frame cannot be reused. These
are explicit experimental limits, not an asynchronous event-loop redesign.
Accept uses close-on-exec descriptors; handshake fields are accepted only
after an exact read and role/subscription validation. The kqueue setup error
path no longer closes the descriptor twice.

The round2-final qualification adds sanitized tests with real jobs: 60
concurrent multipart cleanup frames, 300 info responses during client-list
churn, silent-peer deadlines, slow-progress deadlines and handshake field
validation. Earlier component and full-daemon build/link checks are retained.
Artifacts: /usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/round2-final/

Compiler integration probe (not a successful compiler build)
The pinned fork's cddl/lib/libdtrace was tried in isolated /usr/obj storage
with current 5BSD make rules and the existing sysroot. It needs explicit
MK_DTRACE_BENCHMARKS/OPTIMIZED/ASAN/TSAN settings and crypto in _DP_dtrace.
After supplying those, compilation fails on missing machine/runq.h and the
old sys/cdefs.h versus current dirent.h __deprecated1 declaration. Merely
setting SYSROOT for this standalone invocation did not prevent host-header
inclusion. A coherent staged header/compiler environment is required before
this can establish compatibility. No ABI declarations were stubbed out and
no warnings were suppressed to force a misleading successful build.
Log: /usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/compiler/build.log

Still outstanding: event-loop connection retirement and event-pointer lifetime,
worker cleanup after failed transfers, asynchronous handshake admission,
guest-session replay/revocation, VM restore epochs and the compiler/VMM port.
The synchronous accept loop may still wait up to the handshake deadlines for
one peer; this patch bounds that wait but does not eliminate head-of-line blocking.

2026-10-13: Native integration in progress (uncommitted)
The full upstream delta has been applied to experimental/hypertrace, with
conflicts ported to current 5BSD interfaces. This is NOT runtime-qualified.
The original /usr/src checkout and dev branch remain untouched.

Built separately under /usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation:
- compiler-native: integrated libdtrace C/C++ library, static and shared.
- ctf-native: integrated CTF library. Copy provenance now lives in dynamic
  type records, preserving serialized CTF layouts; stock libctf readback passed.
- dtrace-native: CLI built; three valid and two invalid compile-only D programs
  behaved as expected. Libraries were selected through LD_LIBRARY_PATH.
- vmm-native: VMM module with current startup, nested VMX, dirty-log and
  snapshot objects retained; built using GENERIC-NODEBUG option headers.
- dtrace-kmod-native and hypertrace-kmod-native: provider modules built.
- vmmapi-native: VM control library built; ordinary opens no longer require
  HyperTrace support. Only tracing users request the new identity ioctl.
- bhyve-native: bhyve linked with snapshots enabled, current VM generation
  counter retained, and network/vsock USDT definitions in one provider object.
  This standalone link used installed shared nv/uvmem/9p libraries and the
  staged libvmmapi. It is not a complete matched world build.
- cpuid-discovery: explicit bhyve signature/version, plain and KVM-clock modes,
  disabled and unsupported variants tested. Existing feature bits preserved.
- pagewalk: ASan/UBSan tests compile actual kernel walker/scalar-load functions
  against synthetic EPT/NPT and 4/5-level guest page tables. Missing mappings,
  bounds, unchanged page tables, discontiguous cross-page scalar reads and
  overflow rejection pass. No claim about live VM execution follows.

Active port differences from the vendor snapshot:
- VM identity lives in current architecture fields; allocation failure is
  checked, reset preserves identity, destruction releases it.
- Guest hypercall copy faults return failure; probe callbacks use stack-local
  handles, and guest-controlled copy failures no longer assert the host down.
- DTrace load compiler barriers and KMSAN checks are retained.
- Legacy VirtIO network tagging is NOT negotiated: its bit 57 now belongs to
  HASH_REPORT. Current DMA/RSS paths and local provenance probes are retained.
  A compatible cross-guest packet provenance transport remains outstanding.
- VirtIO-DTrace device type moved from WLAN's assigned 10 to experimental
  65535, following VirtIO 1.2 Appendix B.3. Legacy PCI transport is still used.
- CTF copy metadata is not written into standard CTF type records.

Major gaps still open: full world/kernel build, other architectures, bhyve
transport admission/lifetime and bounded ELF assembly, transport reset and
snapshot codecs, reconnect/replay generations, VM-ID reuse retirement, daemon
retirement, remaining guest-memory builtins that translate a base address
once, provider-specific execution, and disposable matched host/guest tests.
The fact that modules link does not establish symbol closure against a newly
built kernel or correct runtime ordering. Nothing has been installed/loaded.

VirtIO references used during this port:
https://docs.oasis-open.org/virtio/virtio/v1.2/virtio-v1.2.html
Sections 5.1.3 (HASH_REPORT bit 57), 5 (device ID 10), B.3 (experimental IDs).

2026-10-13: Further native integration and regression loops

Current native fixes (beyond the first three vendor patches):
- bhyve connections serialize complete messages and descriptor replacement,
  use absolute I/O deadlines, handle short transfers/EINTR/EOF, suppress
  SIGPIPE, preserve a healthy peer direction, and join the connector on
  explicit destruction. Cleanup payloads are consumed before the next header.
- Guest ELF assembly is per device and bounded, validates fragment identity,
  totals and completion, and supplies the correct daemon frame length.
- VirtIO-DTrace now uses the current vi_req completion API, initializes its
  device mutex before common interrupt setup, uses one lock for queue state,
  waits for guest descriptors, and wakes on TX notifications. Reset frees
  queued payloads and discards messages captured across a reset generation.
  Pending whole frames are bounded. This is NOT a complete reset/replay or
  save/restore protocol; external sessions and VM identity still need epochs.
- Kernel probe maps reject invalid VM/probe IDs, accept the last valid slot,
  reject duplicate insertions, count populated entries, and free tables using
  the correct allocation size. Provider hash collisions advance and a full
  table returns an error instead of looping forever.
- Probe-map readers cover lookup and execution with interrupts disabled;
  removal synchronizes before freeing. Nested thread-local handles restore
  the previous value. Hook registration/unload serialize with DTrace control
  paths; active probes or VMM hooks prevent provider unload.
- Virtual-probe creation bounds copied input to 64 MiB, frees it on copy and
  callback failure/success, checks absent hooks, and propagates creation
  errors. The lock order is cpu_lock, provider lock, then DTrace lock.
- Guest strcmp/bcmp/bcopy/strcpy and strjoin preserve the guest address space
  while reading. Cross-page comparison/copy tests pass on noncontiguous
  backing pages. Other DIF builtins still require the same audit.
- Native dtraced uses non-reused event tokens with reference acquisition,
  deletes retired kqueue filters, prevents re-enabling retired clients, and
  removes queued jobs targeting dead clients. Failed worker jobs retire their
  connections; final shutdown frees remaining clients after workers join.
- dtracedctl named cleanup includes all payload bytes in its frame size, uses
  shared bounded exact I/O, handles NAK/EOF, and no longer closes stdin on
  early exit. Its upstream unimplemented statistics option now fails clearly.

Validation completed:
- Existing rootless nested-VMX gate: PASS, 373 cases. Architectural, dirty-log,
  startup, snapshot, public-header and validator checks passed. Live VM cases
  were explicitly skipped because no disposable /dev/vmm test setup is ready.
  Artifacts: nested-model3.log and nested-model-result under the validation root.
