2026-10-13: Guest execution and current bhyve lifecycle integration

- Guest DIF string, path, JSON, substring, tokenizer, aggregation-key and
  by-reference record reads preserve guest address-space handles and translate
  each page instead of treating guest memory as a contiguous host allocation.
  Tokenizer continuations retain their original address-space handle. Nested
  no-fault state is preserved; scratch substring output is bounded.
- Probe firing disables interrupts before fetching a probe-table pointer,
  validates the namespace/ID bounds, and preserves nested ERROR-probe state.
  The firing helper is separate from the provider lookup API.
- The first full world build exposed missing prebuild dependencies for the
  filesystem capability libraries. The next run exposed libdtrace's new
  crypto/C++ prerequisites. Makefile.inc1 now orders these dependencies.
- Guest dttransport and virtio_dtrace no longer form a circular module
  dependency. dttransport owns a registered send callback protected by an sx
  lock; retirement waits for active sends. Writes without a transport return
  ENXIO, and a second transport is rejected.
- Guest TX uses separately allocated descriptors that remain owned by the
  ring until completion or reset drain. Removed unbounded completion polling,
  reuse of a shared TX allocation, invalid frees of static descriptors, the
  leaked thread allocation, and missing communicator-thread shutdown waiting.
  Attach errors use initialized teardown primitives. Receive sizes and ELF
  fragment bounds are checked before copies. Pending queues are bounded.
- dttransport publishes its cdev with initialized driver data, frees entries
  with their matching allocator, rejects unload while open/in use, destroys
  the cdev before its backing state, and drains queued entries on unload.
- bhyve initialization now reports explicitly enabled HyperTrace setup errors
  as startup failures. A virtio-dtrace device requires initialized forwarding;
  duplicate devices are rejected because the protocol has one channel pair.
  Completed ELF forwarding failures mark the VirtIO device NEEDS_RESET.
- HyperTrace implements the current bhyve common VirtIO pause/resume ownership
  contract. This fences device workers; it is NOT a portable checkpoint codec
  and does not assert that external dtraced state can be restored.

