2026-10-13: VM lifetime isolation and guest-daemon pipeline hardening

- Full development world passed: buildworld-resume4.log (16:25 UTC), followed
  by buildworld-epoch.log (16:32 UTC) and buildworld-protocol2.log (16:37 UTC).
- Matched kernel/modules passed: buildkernel-epoch.log (16:31 UTC) and
  buildkernel-augment.log (16:39 UTC).
- Every VM receives a nonzero random 64-bit lifetime token, rotated on VM
  reset and invalidated on destruction. VM_GET_IDENTITY and libvmmapi expose
  it alongside the namespace ID; the bhyve Capsicum ioctl list includes it.
  VMM hook publication/retirement now shares DTrace configuration locking.
- Probe registration, enable and augment validate the current lifetime. Probe
  firing compares the registered token with the running VM. Reused namespaces
  containing old probes return EBUSY until old consumers release them; they
  cannot mix probes from different VM lifetimes. Tokens are probabilistic
  64-bit identifiers, not serialized checkpoint identities.
- Guest-to-host ELF envelopes are explicitly versioned and contain the host
  VM lifetime. Legacy VM-ID-only envelopes are rejected. Program clones and
  CLI program-list lookup preserve/use the lifetime token.
- Compiled host-to-guest programs carry their target lifetime inside the ELF
  transport payload so directory persistence cannot discard it. Async jobs
  copy both identity fields. bhyve ignores programs for another lifetime and
  strips the target wrapper before passing the ELF to the guest. Untargeted
  initial compilation templates remain supported.
- Forwarded KILL messages include the target lifetime; bhyve rejects stale
  targets and process-group/init PIDs. Local daemon protocol version 2 is
  negotiated by all native clients. Daemon greetings are fully initialized.
- Guest receive allocation/queue failures stop the stream instead of dropping
  ELF fragments and acknowledging readiness. Node IDs and kill PIDs are checked.
- Guest daemon socket handshakes and ELF frame reads use complete bounded I/O.
  ELF assembly is shared with bhyve, bounded and owned by the reader thread.
  Files are created only after complete assembly, fully written and synced,
  then atomically renamed into visibility. Device writes are complete records;
  backpressure retries have a deadline. EOF, malformed frames and failures
  release owned buffers and stop the pipeline instead of hanging or aborting.
- Augment now restores the host namespace on every exit and releases locks in
  reverse acquisition order. Failed retain operations destroy their enabling.
  Removed an unused DOF copy that leaked on every enable; probe-list failures
  now reach common DOF/list cleanup.

