2026-10-13: Namespace quarantine and abandoned-registration ownership

- Full world passed with persisted target envelopes: buildworld-target-envelope.log
  (16:45 UTC), then buildworld-owner.log (16:50 UTC). Kernel/modules passed with
  registration ownership: buildkernel-owner.log (16:49 UTC).
- Successful probe-registration batches are now owned by their DTrace consumer.
  Closing it releases probes that were registered but never enabled. Cleanup
  compares the original probe generation and preserves other consumers' active
  ECBs, so a reused numeric probe ID cannot be disabled by an old owner record.
  Owner records have a per-consumer bound; failed batches add no owner record.
- Namespace allocation now belongs to HyperTrace, not a VMM-local ID arena.
  An inactive namespace with retained probes remains unavailable to new VMs,
  including after VMM module reload. A later allocation automatically sees it
  as available after its probes are released. VM reset reserves a fresh identity
  before existing event/coordinator cleanup and returns it on failure. This
  prevents namespace exhaustion from partially resetting a VM. At maximum
  namespace occupancy a reset can return ENOSPC before changing VM state.
- libvmmapi queries the current numeric ID instead of caching one across reset.
- Daemon spool forwarding now requires a nonempty bounded regular file, rejects
  symlinks/FIFOs, handles complete reads, and catches buffer-allocation failure.
  Directory stream teardown no longer closes its owned descriptor twice.

Validation:
- native-port-round19.log: all twenty native suites PASS with ASan/UBSan.
  Includes actual reset transaction rollback, namespace quarantine/exhaustion,
  registration-owner cleanup and quota-boundary checks.
- native-daemon-round9.log: sanitized daemon build/link and four component
  suites PASS, including actual file-to-socket forwarding and invalid spool types.
- buildkernel-namespace.log and buildworld-namespace.log: running for the newest
  namespace allocator and spool-forwarding changes.
- nested-model4.log / nested-model-result4: existing rootless nested-VMX gate
  running again to check integration with the current bhyve coordinator changes.

The same snapshot/replay, per-program cleanup, network provenance, architecture
and live-validation requirements remain. Namespace quarantine provides safe
resource retirement; it is not an external tracing-session checkpoint codec.

2026-10-13: Named cleanup, child ownership and TAP provenance

- Named cleanup now selects exact forwarder VM names. An empty selection still
  explicitly means all VMs. Duplicate names do not duplicate cleanup jobs;
  unsubscribed connections and consumers are excluded.
- Guest termination only signals registered child PIDs. Signaling and reaping
  share the ownership lock, and a PID remains owned until reaped. The background
  reaper no longer steals foreground launchers' exit statuses or leaves stale
  registered PIDs available to kill unrelated processes after numeric PID reuse.
- Foreground child waits use bounded monotonic polling and actually reap exited
  children, without leaking kqueue descriptors. Timed-out live children transfer
  to the managed set for eventual termination/reaping.
- Reset rollback retains explicit error returns after each coordinator step,
  satisfying the existing transactional coordinator source gate as well as the
  executable reset rollback test.
- TAP tagged write framing now matches read framing: optional VirtIO header,
  provenance identifier, packet bytes. MRU accounting includes the metadata.
  bhyve publishes tagging-enabled state only after successful ioctl negotiation.
  Generated packet identifiers initialize their exported padding.

