2026-10-13: DIF ownership, ELF fault injection, daemon startup and service audit

- DIF duplication now owns a separate symbol table and cleans partially copied
  objects even when its first allocation fails. DIF destruction releases that
  table. Parsed DIF, statement and program objects use scoped cleanup; cached
  ECBs retain one explicit reference until parsing ends. The shared-ECB action
  unlink condition was corrected after a real multi-statement OOM test exposed
  an assertion during program destruction. Parsed aggregation identifiers are
  owned by their program and released with it.
- ELF DIF instructions are checked for valid opcodes, immediate table indexes,
  terminated string references and forward in-range branch targets before
  userspace CFG/type analysis. Variable string references and object names are
  checked. Serialized variable records have process-local pointer fields
  cleared by the writer and by the reader before use; type IDs remain available
  because the linker uses them for D module type reconstruction.
- Spawned tracing children receive an unblocked signal mask and default signal
  dispositions, independent of the daemon's blocked INT/TERM mask. The launch
  test verifies SIGTERM reaches a child and leaves the daemon's mask unchanged.
- Daemon -D paths are bounded, absolute and normalized; failed validation
  leaves old paths intact. -t accepts only 1..1024 workers. The rc script
  validates dtraced_type, documents auto/overlord/minion defaults, and provides
  shutdown ordering. The dtraced manual documents these options and the fixed
  control-socket path. Socket acceptance and initial info-job creation now
  unwind safely on allocation failure.

