rebuilt bhyve and dtraced transport objects postdate their source changes.
The earlier buildkernel-final.log remains current; no kernel source changed
after it. This is component/build qualification, not live VM qualification.

Reconnect review: the receive worker reads and publishes one frame before
requesting another, and only that worker retires a broken receive stream;
the connector cannot attach a replacement until the retirement. The reset
path already fences in-flight reads with a generation. Advancing only the
receive generation on ordinary reconnect would leave the VirtIO device's
expected generation unchanged and discard all future frames. Correct daemon
restart handling needs acknowledged program ownership, revocation/replay
rules and a coordinated guest reset/session transition. This remains open;
the connector fix above does not claim replay safety or restored state.

2026-10-13: transport failure fencing and kill-event ownership

The daemon's dttransport listener now handles allocation failure while
queuing a kill request. It requests orderly shutdown instead of allowing an
exception to terminate the listener thread. A sanitized pipe-backed listener
test covers both the failure and a successful later invocation.

The bhyve VirtIO-DTrace device now rejects guest program submissions and
discards host control frames while NEEDS_RESET is set. This closes the
window in which a reconnected daemon could deliver controls after a failed
transport and before a guest reset. The actual device test covers both
directions. This is a failure fence, not acknowledged replay: a lost program
can still be absent after reset, so automatic reconnect and restore remain
unqualified until program ownership, revocation and replay are implemented.

