2026-10-13: acknowledged forwarding boundary, protocol version 3

Forwarding an ELF to bhyve or the guest dttransport writer now requires an
ACK that echoes a nonzero per-frame delivery ID. The sender serializes the
whole frame and ACK under its connection output lock and rejects a missing,
wrong or timed-out token. The two forwarders ACK only after bhyve accepts a
whole frame into its current device queue, or the guest transport accepts all
ELF records. Bhyve refuses an ACK for a stale receive generation. A
forwarder cannot subscribe to both ELFWRITE and READDATA, preventing the
request reader from consuming forwarding ACKs. The local protocol version is
3 so older peers cannot silently use the new framing. These ACKs prove queue
acceptance, not DTrace program installation, and are not yet durable program
ownership or idempotent replay. The outbound spool still marks a file seen
before per-forwarder delivery, so daemon restart/reconnect can still lose it.

native-daemon-round43.log passes sixteen ASan/UBSan suites, including a real
forwarder ELF transfer with matching and mismatched ACKs.
native-port-round32.log passes twenty-five sanitized port suites, including
bhyve and guest-daemon ACK paths. The additional device acceptance assertions
pass in /tmp/bhyve-device-ack. buildworld-forward-ack.log completed at
20:09:35 UTC with -j16; bhyve and dtraced objects postdate the protocol
edits. dtraced.8 mandoc lint and git diff --check pass. No installation,
module load, daemon start or VM launch occurred.

2026-10-13: crash-durable spool publication

Completed spool files now require a directory fsync after the atomic rename,
in addition to the existing file fsync before rename. A failed directory
sync reports failure and attempts to remove the just-published name. On
startup, each daemon directory removes only hidden `.elf.` names followed by
exactly 32 lowercase hex digits, the format of an interrupted spool writer;
completed files remain untouched. Removals are synced before startup
continues. This closes the known rename durability gap when the filesystem
honors directory fsync. A failing fsync can still leave an ambiguous durable
state after a host crash; full replay must tolerate that with stable IDs and
idempotent installation.

native-daemon-round45.log passes all sixteen sanitized suites. Its actual
ZFS directory test covers complete-file preservation, stale-temporary
cleanup, directory-sync failure rollback, short/EINTR I/O and successful
post-rename directory sync. buildworld-spool-durability.log is the matching
full world build. No daemon or guest was started.

The stale-file scan is protected by the pre-existing pidfile_open lock in
dtraced.cc, acquired before state.initialize. A disposable forked test using
the actual FreeBSD pidfile API confirmed that a second process receives
EEXIST until the first releases the lock. An additional directory lock was
prototyped and removed as redundant; it is not part of the final code.
buildworld-spool-durability.log passed at 20:14:20 UTC. The final -j16 world
buildworld-daemon-lock.log passed at 20:18:23 UTC after removal of the
redundant lock, with its dtraced_state object newer than the final source
and headers. mandoc lint and git diff --check pass. No runtime components
were installed, loaded or started.

2026-10-13: stable program identity through the ELF transport

Local protocol version 4 now distinguishes a 128-bit program ID from the
per-delivery ACK token. The daemon derives the former from its durable
`elf.<32 lowercase hex>` outbound spool name and refuses to forward a file
without that canonical name. Bhyve, both VirtIO controls, the dttransport
record and the receiving daemons carry this ID across every ELF fragment.
The receivers reject zero IDs and inconsistent fragment IDs. Host and guest
inbound daemons publish to the same `elf.<ID>` name on retry, compare an
existing file's full contents before accepting a duplicate, and never
overwrite a different program under the same ID. Newly created names require
a directory fsync before publication succeeds. This establishes durable file
identity, but ACK still proves transport queue acceptance only.

native-port-round46 passed all twenty-five sanitized port suites, including
the host and guest transport paths. native-daemon-round50 passed all sixteen
sanitized daemon suites, including same-ID deduplication, conflicting payload
rejection, directory-sync rollback and the real inbound request route. The
focused guest driver and guest daemon ID/fragment tests passed.
buildkernel-program-id.log built
GENERIC-NODEBUG with -j16. buildworld-stable-id-final.log completed at
20:28:43 UTC with -j16; its dtraced directory and transport objects are
newer than the final sources. No runtime component was started.

Remaining replay work: outbound delivery state is still volatile, startup
marks existing inbound files as seen without recovering their launch state,
and revocation has no durable program-ID tombstone. Installation ACKs and
save/restore recovery remain unimplemented; no live VM qualification has
been performed.

2026-10-13: identifier publication ordering

The guest daemon now allocates a pending identifier-list node before writing
the associated ELF, holds the identifier lock while the file becomes visible,
and splices the prepared node into the live list only after publication is
durable. A failed publication leaves the list unchanged. An identical retry
of a stable-ID file does not add a duplicate identifier. The lock prevents
the inbound directory watcher from launching the file between visibility
and identifier commit. The actual inbound request route and an observer
thread at directory fsync are covered by sanitized spool-publish tests.
native-daemon-round52 passed all sixteen suites; buildworld-ident-commit.log
completed at 20:35:33 UTC with -j16 and rebuilt the final request object.

Review of the remaining replay gap: outbound files are marked seen when a
notification job is queued, before delivery or installation. A daemon restart
marks all existing files seen again. Blindly resending on a forwarder
reconnect would replay revoked programs because kill messages carry only
guest PID, VM ID and epoch, and no durable program-ID tombstone exists. Safe
requeue requires an end-to-end program ID in installation and revocation
records, plus persistent per-VM state and installation acknowledgment.
The CLI already carries a 128-byte random `dp_ident` in serialized programs
and copies it into `dp_srcident` for transformed programs; its pgplist scopes
that source identifier by VM ID and epoch. This semantic lineage is distinct
from the 128-bit spool artifact ID added above. The guest's async transformed
ELF send currently requests `send_ident=0`, and the host's kill message
contains only PID, VM ID and epoch. A durable replay protocol must preserve
both artifact and source lineage rather than infer revocation from a spool
filename or a reusable PID.

2026-10-13: source lineage and source-bound guest termination

Protocol version 5 now carries a 128-bit source-lineage ID as well as the
artifact program ID in ELF and kill messages. The library derives lineage
from the original program identifier, preserving `dp_srcident` across
transformation, and the daemons and both VirtIO paths propagate it without
using the transient delivery token as identity. Durable spool names retain
the lineage suffix (`elf.<artifact>.<source>`); retries compare existing
contents and reject one artifact ID under a different lineage. The guest
daemon records each sourced child PID with its source ID. After the durable
revocation is registered, the child manager checks that source under the child
lock immediately before SIGTERM and terminates every owned child from it,
even if the PID named by the sender has already exited. Reaping removes the
PID bindings.
The guest publishes a source tombstone to the inbound directory and syncs it
before accepting a kill event. Incoming ELF publication checks the tombstone
under the same stable-name lock and discards a revoked program. Launch checks
the durable tombstone before spawn and again at child registration; an
in-memory revoked-source set closes the gap between a kill and PID ownership.
The control daemon persists a per-VM/epoch/PID/source kill record before
forwarding a kill. That one record suppresses later ELF sends for its source;
on forwarder reconnect, the daemon queues the recorded kill again. Bhyve's
VM-ID and epoch gate discards records for other VM lifetimes.
Guest trace children now launch through `pdfork` and retain a process
descriptor owned by the daemon. Closing that descriptor on daemon death,
shutdown or re-exec terminates the child. Reaping and SIGTERM use the
descriptor, so neither operation depends on a reusable PID; a post-fork
exec-status pipe reports launch failures without running allocator or C++
locks in the child. The full source tree uses `PD_NOWAITPID` for automatic
reaping, while the native qualification build supports the older installed
headers and explicitly reaps after `pdwait`.
On guest daemon startup, canonical sourced inbound ELF files are left for the
watcher to relaunch instead of being marked permanently seen. The prior
daemon's process descriptors have closed, so its children are terminated;
revoked sources remain blocked by the synced guest tombstone. Revoked files
are then marked seen without repeated launch attempts.
Malformed or legacy `elf.*` guest spool names fail before launching an
unrevocable child. Stable-name publication uses a separate mutex from the
directory watcher, avoiding its inverse lock order with the identifier list.

The native port suite passed all 25 cases (round52), the daemon suite passed
all 16 cases (round74), including the host pending-kill replay and child
ownership cases. The complete world built for the initial lineage wire
change (buildworld-lineage-final). The focused process-launch-reap run verified
`pdwait`-based background reaping against the installed kernel. GENERIC-NODEBUG
with modules built for
the latest kernel source (buildkernel-tombstone). A subsequent world build
entered a full bootstrap compiler rebuild after a transient production-malloc
flag change and was stopped while code integration continued; it found no
compiler error. A final world build remains required. These are native
qualifications only; no live VM was started.

The guest tombstones and host pending-kill records are durable, but there is
still no installation ACK or per-forwarder delivery ledger. Outbound
program delivery cannot yet be safely replayed across control-daemon restart
and forwarder reconnect. Automatic replay was not enabled because the
destination and each persisted program payload require explicit authorization.
The qualification host currently has no `/var/ddtrace/outbound` directory or
forwarder socket, so there is no concrete payload/destination pair to approve.
Forwarder identity is currently supplied by the local hello message; the
daemon does not bind it to peer credentials or an authenticated VM identity.
That must be resolved before unattended replay can establish where a stored
program is going.
The daemon deliberately does not treat a transport ACK as installation.
The guest `DEL ident` response is emitted by the ELF parser before the CLI
calls `dtrace_program_exec`; it also cannot serve as an installation ACK.
An earlier restart review found that startup left canonical sourced ELF files
for the watcher while the old daemon's in-memory identifier list was gone.
The stored-file launch path has since been removed; see the restart guard
entry below. Durable authorization and explicit installation evidence remain
requirements for any future replay design.

The identifier-controlled child launch now has a distinct post-exec result.
After `dtrace_program_exec`, tracing-worker startup, and successful local ELF
publication to dtraced, the CLI sends a
versioned, source-bound installed status to its parent; compile-only programs
report not installed. The guest daemon validates magic, reserved bytes and
the complete source identifier before registering the child, and exposes the
status to its caller. This result is local to the guest daemon: it has not yet
been carried through VirtIO to bhyve and the host, so it is not an end-to-end
installation ACK and does not authorize outbound ELF replay. The sanitized
daemon suite passed all 17 cases (round84), including malformed and truncated
status frames. The focused CLI sender test `cli-identifiers-launch` passed;
the native port suite passed 25 cases (round54). A complete 16-job world build
passed on 2026-10-13. After the post-publication ordering change, the
sanitized daemon suite passed all 17 cases (round85) and the native port suite
passed all 25 cases (round55). The incremental 16-job world rebuild passed on
2026-10-13. After extending the bounded parent installation-result wait to
cover compilation and the daemon's ELF send, a targeted native dtraced rebuild
and all 17 sanitized daemon cases passed (round86).
VM save/restore coordination, packet-provenance overhead qualification,
provider coverage, and live host/guest validation remain open.

Automatic restart replay remains disabled. A proposed implementation would
persist the source identifier before publishing the original outbound ELF,
bind it to the stable 128-bit source ID, and require that exact binding when
relaunching a stored inbound ELF. It would also need a concrete target VM ID,
epoch, and a payload-specific destination decision before any stored ELF is
sent onward. These are design requirements, not implemented behavior; a
local post-exec result is not a host receipt. Automatic approval review
rejected activating stored-identifier launches because doing so would turn
previously disabled replay into automatic ELF execution. No source-identifier
persistence or restart-launch activation from that attempt remains in this
tree.

The guest now emits a distinct `DTT_INSTALLED` observation only after an
identifier-controlled child reports installation and the daemon has registered
it. The event carries the canonical spool program and source IDs through the
guest VirtIO transport to bhyve. Bhyve attaches the current VM ID, epoch and
name and sends `DTRACED_MSG_INSTALLED` to the host daemon. The host validates
the forwarder role, VM name, epoch and IDs, deduplicates observations, and
retains them only for that forwarder connection. Local daemon protocol version
6 rejects older peers. This does not yet correlate
an observation with a specific outbound delivery transaction, durably record
installation or prove the child remains alive. It therefore does not unlock
automatic replay. A complete 16-job world build and GENERIC-NODEBUG kernel
build passed on 2026-10-13; the latter compiled both `dttransport` and
`virtio_dtrace`. The 18-case sanitized native daemon suite and 25-case native
port suite passed, including guest control-event mapping, bhyve VM identity,
and host receipt validation. No live host/guest receipt was exercised.
The next receipt step must carry the host's delivery token to the guest,
persist it with the inbound artifact, and return it with the post-exec result.
The host can then match a result to an acknowledged delivery on the same
forwarder and VM epoch; it must discard in-flight claims when either channel
disconnects. A result sent after a reset must carry the original epoch rather
than borrowing bhyve's current epoch at receive time. Child exit and source
revocation also need to retire the installed state. Until those checks exist,
the connection-scoped collection remains diagnostic only.
The inherited 32-byte `mbufid_t` raises a 256-byte mbuf allocation to the
512-byte size class. At one million resident mbufs, that is roughly 244 MiB
of extra allocation before cache effects; the direct `m_pkthdr.mbufid` users
and TAP framing need a separate representation and throughput review before
claiming the network path is production-ready.
Automatic approval review rejected changing global `MSIZE` and removing
`m_pkthdr.mbufid` during this round because that would alter mbuf layout and
networking behavior across the kernel. The preparatory sidecar API was
reverted. Any later layout change needs a separately reviewable ABI plan,
coverage of the direct readers and writers in BPF, sockets, TAP, sglist and
drivers, and network throughput and memory measurements before deployment.

Until the external session codec and daemon transaction exist, VirtIO DTrace
returns `EOPNOTSUPP` from its snapshot callback. A checkpoint with the device
therefore fails instead of writing a queue-only image that cannot restore the
trace session. Pause/resume still gate guest-memory access, but do not imply
save/restore support. The native port suite passed all 25 cases (round53),
and the focused `bhyve-device-checkpoint` case checked the explicit snapshot
rejection.

The kill ledger now checks a targeted ELF against the matching VM ID and
epoch. A kill for one guest no longer suppresses a program with the same
source lineage in another guest or VM lifetime. Untargeted templates still
reach forwarders, where guest tombstones prevent a revoked source from being
launched. Malformed target envelopes fail before forwarding, and durable kill
records reject out-of-range VM IDs. The native daemon suite passed all 16
ASan/UBSan cases (round80), including a kill naming an already-reaped PID.
Its harness uses installed process-descriptor
headers for the running kernel and the source HyperTrace header for the new
envelope; using source process-descriptor headers exposed a `PD_NOWAITPID`
flag unsupported by this qualification host. The focused `spool-file-scoped`
run also passed, including forwarding the same source to a second VM after
the first VM's kill. The full world build is still running.

Host kill fanout now stages every subscribed forwarder's job before writing
the durable kill record. A failed allocation leaves neither a half-enqueued
fanout nor a ledger entry that an already connected forwarder might never
observe. The daemon releases the subscriber lock while syncing the record;
new subscribers replay it on connection. Once durable, the staged list is
published without allocation. A forwarder whose write event cannot be
rearmed is retired so it can reconnect and replay the record. The two-
forwarder OOM test checks every allocation boundary and confirms failed
requests leave no ledger entry. A separate injected kqueue failure confirms
the affected forwarder is retired after ledger commit. All 17 sanitized
daemon cases passed in native-daemon-round83. The full world build is still
in its bootstrap compiler stage with no reported error.

2026-10-13 delivery context continuation:
The host delivery token and the VM ID and epoch now accompany each host ELF
fragment through bhyve and VirtIO into the guest daemon. The guest checks
fragment consistency, keeps the completed artifact's delivery context only
in volatile daemon memory, and snapshots it before launching a tracing child.
After the child reports installation, the guest returns that original token
and epoch in DTT_INSTALLED. Bhyve rejects an observation whose VM identity
no longer matches the running VM, and the host records the token with the
connection-scoped observation. The local daemon protocol is now version 7.
Bhyve also rechecks a targeted ELF's envelope after reading VM identity so
a reset between target selection and fragment construction cannot assign a
new epoch to an old target. This is an observation path, not restart replay.

The 18 sanitized native daemon suites passed in native-daemon-round91 and
the 25 native port suites passed in native-port-round59c. The focused guest
framing tests, including zero and mismatched token/epoch rejection, a 16-job
buildworld and GENERIC-NODEBUG buildkernel passed;
the latter build compiled dttransport and virtio_dtrace. Build logs are
under /usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation. No live VM was available, and
no module was loaded, installation performed, or reboot requested.

The host does not yet match an installation observation against a pending
and acknowledged outbound delivery on the same forwarder and VM lifetime.
The current host receipt therefore remains diagnostic and cannot authorize
automatic restart or replay. The queue-acceptance ACK also lacks VM identity
for untargeted template deliveries. A durable installation ledger, child
lifetime and revocation retirement, reset reconciliation, an external
snapshot/session codec, packet-provenance memory/throughput work, remaining
provider coverage, and live guest qualification are still open.

2026-10-13 forwarder session and delivery ledger continuation:
The two bhyve forwarder channels now share a fresh random 128-bit process
session ID in their version-8 handshakes. The host stages each outbound
delivery token with its program and source IDs and, for targeted ELFs, VM ID
and epoch before sending. Queue ACK acceptance marks that record accepted;
send or ACK failure removes it. Installation observations may arrive before
the queue ACK and are held against the pending record, but cannot become an
accepted installation until the ACK succeeds. An observation must match the
session ID and VM name of the still-live outbound channel, token, program and
source IDs, and any target VM identity. Retiring the observation channel
clears its claims; retiring the outbound channel destroys its entire ledger.
The host no longer accepts an unpaired observation as an installation
receipt. This supersedes the prior paragraph's missing host correlation.

The 18 sanitized native daemon suites passed in native-daemon-round94,
including early-observation matching, wrong-session and wrong-epoch rejection,
disconnect retirement, ACK acceptance, and failed-delivery cleanup. The 25
native port suites passed in native-port-round60, including the new bhyve
session handshake. A 16-job buildworld and GENERIC-NODEBUG buildkernel passed
with logs under /usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation. No live VM was available.

The ledger is still volatile, bounded to 16384 deliveries per outbound
connection, and does not retire records on child exit. Source revocation
retirement is described below.
Untargeted templates have no host-known VM epoch before delivery. There is
still no durable installation transaction, replay authorization or external
save/restore session codec. Runtime qualification in a live VM remains
required before this can be considered mature.

2026-10-13 revocation and installation-order continuation:
The host serializes durable kill publication and installation receipt
matching with a revocation mutex. Every receipt checks the durable source
and VM-lifetime tombstone before it can mark a delivery observed. After a
kill record is synced, matching accepted delivery records are removed;
matching in-flight deliveries are marked revoked and removed when their send
completes. This covers an observation racing with durable revocation and
prevents revoked accepted records from exhausting the connection ledger.
The installed-receipt suite verifies receipt rejection after a durable kill,
claim retirement, and separate observation-channel disconnect retirement.
All 18 sanitized native daemon suites passed in native-daemon-round96 and a
16-job buildworld passed in buildworld-revocation-ledger.log. Kernel and
port source were unchanged from the previous successful builds.

2026-10-13 child-lifetime retirement continuation:
The guest now emits DTT_UNINSTALLED with the original delivery token, VM
identity, program and source IDs when an identified tracing child is reaped.
Installation and child-exit events share a guest mutex, so the exit cannot
overtake a reported installation. A child already reaped before context
registration produces no installation claim. VirtIO and bhyve carry the exit
event to the host as protocol-version-9 DTRACED_MSG_UNINSTALLED. The host
validates the same session and delivery identity, removes accepted deliveries,
and marks pending deliveries retired until the send finishes. A repeated exit
for an already removed token is harmless. The process-launch test exercises
the real reaper and checks the emitted exit event; guest-driver, bhyve-device
and host-receipt tests cover the remaining hops. All 18 sanitized native
daemon suites passed in native-daemon-round98 and 25 native port suites passed
in native-port-round61. The 16-job buildworld and GENERIC-NODEBUG buildkernel
passed in buildworld-child-retirement.log and buildkernel-child-retirement.log.
No live VM was available for end-to-end qualification.

The host ledger remains volatile and bounded. Deliveries that are accepted
but never installed can still accumulate, and transport failure or guest
restart does not recover a lost lifecycle event. Durable installation
transactions, replay authorization, session save/restore, packet-provenance
performance work, architecture/provider coverage and live VM qualification
remain open.

2026-10-13 stored-ELF restart guard:
A review found that guest startup still omitted canonical sourced ELF files
from its memorized set, allowing the watcher to attempt a stored-file launch
after daemon restart without current delivery context. Startup now memorizes
those files, and process_inbound refuses to launch a canonical ELF unless the
running daemon has a matching volatile delivery token. This removes the
previously retained automatic restart-execution path; it does not implement
authorized replay. If an identified child reports installation but its
delivery cannot be tracked or reported, directory processing now fails and
the daemon shuts down instead of leaving an unreported tracing child running.
The 18 sanitized native daemon suites passed in native-daemon-round99,
including startup memorization, and a 16-job buildworld passed in
buildworld-restart-guard.log. Kernel and port source were unchanged.

2026-10-13 provider-table isolation continuation:
The inherited provider table allowed one guest to reserve all 4096 global
provider slots with distinct names. HyperTrace now limits each VM to 256
distinct provider names while allowing multiple probes to share a provider.
Per-provider VM reference counts release a VM's quota slot on the final probe
removal, including rollback after a failed batch. Existing-provider lookup
no longer allocates a new candidate on every probe. The provider-table test
passed with quota, shared-reference, hole/collision and full-table cases;
all 25 native port suites passed in native-port-round62. The 16-job
GENERIC-NODEBUG buildkernel passed in buildkernel-provider-quota.log, which
compiled and linked hypertrace.ko. This bounds one guest's provider-name
impact, but it is not live guest qualification or a complete resource budget
for every HyperTrace object.

2026-10-13 virtual-probe table budget continuation:
Both DTrace and HyperTrace used dense per-VM arrays indexed by guest probe ID.
A single valid but sparse ID near the one-million limit allocated about 8 MiB
in each layer. Each layer now limits aggregate virtual-probe table capacity to
64 MiB while retaining the existing ID range. DTrace also limits live virtual
probes to 65,536 per VM and 262,144 overall, independently of ID density.
HyperTrace frees a VM's map when its last probe is removed. DTrace tracks live
virtual probes and likewise reclaims an empty table after a failed batch
discards its final probe. Ordinary VM teardown also returns the table's budget.
Quota failures now reach the caller as ENOSPC rather than being reported as
duplicate IDs. The sanitized
map test covers budget exhaustion and reclamation, and the batch test covers
ENOSPC propagation and rollback. All 25 native port suites passed in
native-port-table-budget-reclaim.log, a 16-job development buildworld passed in
buildworld-table-budget.log, and the 16-job GENERIC-NODEBUG buildkernel passed
in buildkernel-probe-count-budget.log. These are steady-state table budgets; live
VM behavior and transient resize memory still need qualification.

2026-10-13 forwarder peer-binding continuation:
Host dtraced now captures LOCAL_PEERCRED for every forwarder socket and rejects
a forwarder whose kernel credential record lacks a valid PID. Installation and
child-exit observations only match an outbound channel when the two sockets
have the same kernel-reported effective UID and PID, as well as the existing
random session ID and claimed VM name. Disconnect retirement uses that same
pairing rule. Forwarder hello messages now require a nonempty terminated name;
previously an unterminated name could be silently truncated by client_fd.
The sanitized installed-receipt test exercises wrong-PID and wrong-UID
rejection, and the transport test covers empty and unterminated names. All 18
sanitized native daemon suites passed in native-daemon-peer-cred-name.log, and
a 16-job development buildworld passed in buildworld-peer-cred-name.log. These
checks bind the two channels to one local process; they do not authenticate a
claimed VM name against VMM or provide durable save/restore authorization.

2026-10-13 VMM-attested forwarder continuation:
The host handshake now uses protocol version 10. A new read-only VMM
attestation ioctl returns the kernel VM name, ID and epoch. Libvmmapi exports
an atomic close-on-exec duplicate of the VM descriptor restricted to that
ioctl, and bhyve passes it over the Unix socket after its hello message.
Overlord dtraced rejects missing, malformed or counterfeit descriptors and
requires the kernel name to equal the claimed forwarder name. It retains the
restricted descriptor to reject stale VM epochs when pairing observer and
outbound channels or transferring a frame. Guest minion dtraced does not
require a VMM descriptor. The descriptor framing test covers valid transfer,
close-on-exec, missing and multiple descriptors, and deadline failure; a
daemon integration test passes /dev/null as a counterfeit descriptor and
checks rejection. All 19 sanitized native daemon suites passed in
native-daemon-attest4.log; all 26 native port suites passed in
native-port-attest-final.log. The 16-job development buildworld and
GENERIC-NODEBUG buildkernel passed in buildworld-attestation-final.log and
buildkernel-attestation.log. No live VMM device exists on this host, so a
successful real descriptor handshake, VM reset, migration and save/restore
still require live qualification. Automatic ELF replay remains disabled.

2026-10-14 attested receipt and routing continuation:
Host installation and child-exit observations now require the reported VM ID
and epoch to match the observer channel's current VMM attestation. This closes
a gap for untargeted programs, whose VM identity was previously asserted only
by the observer. Targeted outbound ELFs are skipped for unrelated attested VM
lifetimes, and live or replayed durable kill records are routed only to their
target VM lifetime. The installed-receipt suite mocks the VMM ioctl to test
valid observations, changed kernel identity, spoofed IDs and epochs, and
untargeted delivery; the spool test checks unrelated VM and epoch routing.
All 19 sanitized native daemon suites passed in native-daemon-routing-final;
the receipt suite passed again after a test-only wrapper cleanup. A 16-job
development buildworld passed in buildworld-receipt-routing.log, and
dtraced.8 passed mandoc lint. These checks do not create durable installation
state or authorize automatic replay.
The kill replay queue now reserves space only for records matching the
forwarder's VM lifetime and leaves an unrelated forwarder unarmed. The
19-suite sanitized daemon rerun passed in native-daemon-route-quota, and the
16-job incremental world rebuild passed in buildworld-route-quota.log.
The thread-startup fault injection now covers every host thread creation
position, plus both guest transport thread creation positions before the
socket thread. It uses real temporary directories, a bound local socket and
a nonblocking mock transport pipe; each partial startup shuts down and joins
its launched threads. The focused sanitized thread-startup case passed.

2026-10-14 terminal delivery continuation:
A guest delivery that completes without installation now emits the existing
terminal lifecycle event, so the host can retire an ACKed but never-installed
record. Re-delivery of an identical stored ELF with a fresh token emits the
same terminal event without replacing the original delivery context; a repeat
of the exact token is idempotent. Guest delivery context is reclaimed after
child exit and after source revocation, with full-token matching so a late
exit cannot erase a newer delivery. A cancelled launch also releases its
context. The host receipt test covers terminal reports without any preceding
installation, and the guest tests cover duplicate artifacts, source
revocation, child-exit reclamation and old-token retirement. The 19 sanitized
native daemon suites passed in native-daemon-terminal-final, the focused
guest-daemon test passed in guest-daemon-terminal, and a 16-job development
buildworld passed in buildworld-terminal-delivery.log. These changes retire
known outcomes within one daemon lifetime; daemon restart still lacks a
durable authorized replay transaction and a live VM qualification.

2026-10-14 concurrent handshake admission continuation:
The control-socket event loop no longer performs potentially multi-second
handshakes. Four bounded admission workers poll a nonblocking listener and
perform the existing deadline-limited handshake. Registration is serialized
with event dispatch while the new client is added to the event registry,
kqueue filters and live socket set; the admission worker retains its own
reference until post-registration jobs are staged. A slow or counterfeit peer
therefore cannot hold the event loop or block a second peer while a worker is
available. The pool joins before its kqueue closes on shutdown. The new
sanitized admission test admits eight concurrent clients beside a stalled
handshake and injects failure partway through pool creation. All 20 sanitized
native daemon suites passed in native-daemon-admissions-final; the expanded
admission case passed again after adding concurrent clients and pool failure.
A 16-job development buildworld passed in buildworld-admissions.log. Four
simultaneously stalled handshakes can still delay new admissions until a
deadline, and live VM behavior remains unqualified.
The socket thread now transfers its kqueue to daemon state after successful
setup. Finalization closes it only after the job workers and client cleanup
have finished, so in-flight client retirement does not use a closed kqueue.
The 20-suite sanitized daemon run passed in native-daemon-kqueue-lifetime;
the focused admission test also rearmed clients after the socket thread
exited. A 16-job world build passed in buildworld-kqueue-lifetime.log.
The final header initialization was also covered by the 16-job incremental
world rebuild in buildworld-kqueue-final.log.

2026-10-14 startup readiness continuation:
Daemon initialization now waits for the control socket listener, its four
admission workers, and both directory watches to become ready. Each directory
watch is armed before a second scan, so files published between the original
populate_existing scan and watch registration are processed. A failed watch
or startup timeout shuts down the partially started daemon and returns the
specific startup error. The startup test covers successful readiness, an
invalid directory descriptor, and a file arriving in that scan-to-watch gap.
All 20 sanitized native daemon suites passed in native-daemon-startup-final,
and the 16-job development buildworld passed in buildworld-startup-final2.log.
This closes the daemon readiness race; durable authorized restart replay and
live VMM/guest qualification remain open.

2026-10-14 bhyve receive-stream ACK continuation:
Bhyve now records the delivery ID of a fully read ELF and accepts exactly one
matching queue-acceptance ACK before reading another frame. Retiring the
socket clears that pending token and advances a separate connection
generation. The VirtIO reader carries both the VM reset generation and the
connection generation to its ACK call; a replacement socket cannot accept an
ACK from its predecessor even if the host reuses the same delivery ID. The
native bhyve-sockets case covers wrong and repeated tokens, pending-read
rejection, and same-ID replacement-stream rejection. All 26 native port
suites passed in native-port-ack-stream, and a 16-job development buildworld
with BHYVE_SNAPSHOT enabled passed in buildworld-ack-stream.log. This binds
queue ACKs to one live stream; it does not make installation state durable or
authorize automatic replay after daemon restart or VM restore.

2026-10-14 atomic VMM attestation continuation:
VM_REINIT changes the HyperTrace VM ID and epoch while holding the VMM
memory-segment write lock. VM_GET_IDENTIFIER, VM_GET_IDENTITY and
VM_GET_ATTESTATION now take the matching read lock, so bhyve and the host
forwarder cannot observe a mixed identity during reset. The 16-job
GENERIC-NODEBUG buildkernel passed in buildkernel-attestation-lock.log and
recompiled vmm_dev_machdep.c. A live VMM reset/attestation race test remains
unavailable on this host because /dev/vmm is absent; this change does not
establish save/restore session replay.

2026-10-14 portable program-transform reliability continuation:
The HyperTrace libdtrace hash table now bounds full-table probes, leaves an
insert slot untouched when managed-key allocation fails, and closes a deleted
collision-chain hole. The virtual-program load table checks the actual
positive errno returned by insert and releases its owned arrays. Program
reorganization unwinds partial statements and ECB references on allocation
failure rather than aborting or double-freeing; source IMMSTACK actions are
changed only after the transformed program succeeds. Probe names are checked
for termination before composing hash keys, and each key buffer is cleared
so a shorter name does not inherit trailing bytes from an earlier one. The
squash loop now visits the statement immediately after a rearranged duplicate.
All 27 native port suites passed in native-port-hashmap-final, including
managed-key OOM, full-table lookup and 5,000 mixed-home collision operations.
A 16-job
development buildworld passed in buildworld-vprog-final.log. The standalone
ASan/UBSan vprog-transform test linked the resulting libdtrace, transformed duplicate
probes, checked ERROR target/source ownership, and rejected an unterminated
name without mutating the source program. This improves portable compiler
reliability; arm64 and RISC-V still lack the HyperTrace VMM namespace and
attestation port, and live guest execution remains unqualified.

2026-10-14 verifier bounds and relocation continuation:
The host verifier now rejects DIFOs with mismatched instruction lengths,
missing instruction buffers or missing variable tables before reading them.
It checks the destination register on USETX relocation, source and destination
registers on ULOAD/UULOAD relocation, and the full encoding of NOP replacements;
a final ULOAD/UULOAD no longer reads an uninitialized next instruction. The
verification context returns allocation failures instead of dereferencing a
null vector or terminating the process. Whole-program verification rejects
an extra returned clause and composes bounded, terminated probe names before
comparing them. The ASan/UBSan vprog-transform test uses the built libdtrace
to cover malformed relocations, short and null guest buffers, missing variable
tables, and an extra guest clause. The 16-job development buildworld passed in
buildworld-verifier-final.log, and the transformed-program test passed in
vprog-transform-verifier-final. The verifier still has broader policy gaps:
it permits some unmatched source clauses and does not comprehensively compare
action-list structure and metadata. Live guest qualification also remains open.
The verifier now also requires matched action lists to end together and rejects
a DIFO attached to only one side. It no longer verifies single-action DIFOs
twice. The ASan/UBSan test covers both mismatches against the actual built
libdtrace, and the 16-job development buildworld passed in
buildworld-verifier-actions.log. Action metadata policy and permitted probe
target changes still require a full linker-to-verifier audit.
The linker/ELF audit found action kind, tuple count, argument, return flag and
ECB user argument are preserved. The verifier now compares them and also
requires matching predicate-DIFO presence and verifies relocated predicates.
The ASan/UBSan test rejects changed action arguments and an injected predicate;
the 16-job development buildworld passed in buildworld-verifier-metadata.log.
All 27 native port suites passed in native-port-verifier.
Source-clause omission policy, DIFO metadata, and live VM behavior remain to
be qualified.

2026-10-14 DIFO metadata verification continuation:
The linker preserves the original integer-table prefix while appending
relocation constants, and does not rewrite string/symbol tables, variable
identities or the destructive flag. Host verification now checks those exact
invariants before applying its permitted type widening. The actual libdtrace
ASan/UBSan test accepts an appended integer and rejects altered original
constants, string/symbol bytes, variable identity and destructive metadata.
The 16-job development buildworld passed in buildworld-verifier-difo-metadata.log,
the focused test passed in vprog-transform-difo-metadata, and all 27 native
port suites passed in native-port-difo-metadata. Return-type inference,
relocation context and live host/guest behavior still need stronger end-to-end
qualification; this check alone does not establish full linker equivalence.

2026-10-14 returned-clause verification continuation:
The base ELF is passed through the daemon and guest linker without clause
filtering or probe-name rewriting. Host verification now requires every
returned clause to appear in the source at the same position with the same
fully qualified target; it rejects missing clauses as well as additions.
The actual libdtrace ASan/UBSan test covers missing and added clauses and a
changed target, and passed in vprog-transform-clauses. The 16-job development
buildworld passed in buildworld-verifier-clauses.log. Six NODEV/NOSYS
compile/write/read ELF round trips and 33 malformed-ELF rejections passed in
elf-roundtrip-clauses. A real VM guest-linker-to-host-verifier exchange is
still needed to qualify this end-to-end.
All 27 native port suites passed in native-port-clauses.
The verifier also requires a relocated USETX/SETX index to exist in the
returned integer table, including when the base DIFO had no integer table.
The actual libdtrace ASan/UBSan test accepts a newly created table and rejects
an out-of-range index in vprog-transform-setx; the 16-job development
buildworld passed in buildworld-verifier-setx.log.

2026-10-14 untargeted receipt identity continuation:
When an untargeted template's installation receipt passes the forwarder
session and VMM attestation checks, the host now records that VM ID and epoch
on the pending delivery. A later observation-channel disconnect clears the
observation, but a durable kill for that attested lifetime can still mark the
in-flight delivery revoked. The receipt test covers that ordering; it passed
with ASan/UBSan after updating its expectation for pre-ACK retirement. All 20
sanitized native daemon suites passed in native-daemon-bind-template before
the final test assertion was added, and the focused updated receipt test
passed in installed-receipt-updated. A 16-job development buildworld passed in
buildworld-bind-template.log. The ledger is still volatile and this binding
does not authorize replay after restart or VM restore.

2026-10-14 forwarder send-time identity and revocation continuation:
On the control host, an untargeted template is now bound to the forwarder's
VMM-attested VM ID and epoch before queueing. A VM-lifetime tombstone is
checked for templates as well as targeted ELFs, so a revoked template is not
forwarded again merely because it had no target envelope. The final tombstone
check and pending-ledger insertion share the revocation mutex with durable
kill publication. A kill committed before insertion is therefore seen by the
send path; a later kill sees the staged delivery and marks it revoked. The
spool test exercises the attested template, ACK, bound ledger and tombstone
suppression. All 20 sanitized native daemon cases passed in
native-daemon-bind-on-send and native-daemon-revocation-atomic. The 16-job
development buildworld passed in buildworld-bind-on-send.log and
buildworld-revocation-atomic.log. The ledger remains volatile, and a kill
committed after insertion can still race the actual ELF wire send; the guest
tombstone and eventual kill fanout remain necessary. This is not durable
restart replay or VM save/restore qualification.

2026-10-14 arm64/RISC-V VMM identity continuation:
The arm64 and RISC-V VMMs now allocate a HyperTrace VM namespace ID and
lifetime epoch at creation, rotate both on successful VM reset, and release
them on teardown. They expose the same read-locked identifier, identity and
name attestation ioctls as amd64. The shared libvmmapi identity and
attestation functions and host dtraced forwarder checks now use these ioctls
on all three VMM architectures. The arm64 GENERIC build with VMM and DTrace
modules passed in buildkernel-arm64-identity-5.log; the matching RISC-V build
passed in buildkernel-riscv-identity-3.log. These builds exposed and fixed
non-amd64 DTrace boot/compiler guards, an RISC-V crypto probe declaration
guard, and a RISC-V cache_ops name collision. Guest hypercalls, guest memory
translation, live VM attestation races and cross-architecture userland
execution are not yet qualified. Guest-address translation on non-amd64
currently returns EOPNOTSUPP; no non-amd64 guest-probe execution is claimed.
The 16-job native development buildworld passed in
buildworld-crossidentity.log, including libvmmapi, bhyve and dtraced. No
packages were published and no upgrade was installed.
The full 16-job arm64 development buildworld passed in
buildworld-arm64-identity-9.log. It includes the target-aware host DTrace
linker, source-tree CTF archive, arm64 libvmmapi, dtraced and bhyve. The
cross build found and fixed shared VM-name header use in libdtrace and the
DTrace CLI, a host/source stdint WCHAR_WIDTH collision, static host-tool
CTF/C++/math link dependencies, and two ELF test fixtures that assumed byte
arrays were suitably aligned for Elf64 headers. This is compile/link
qualification only; no arm64 VM was run.

2026-10-14 guest PTINFO fault handling continuation:
The DTrace guest paging helper now accepts an absent VM handle, allowing
PTINFO in a host context to fail through the translation check instead of
dereferencing a null handle. PTINFO initializes its result, restores the
prior no-fault state, and reports an invalid guest address when translation
fails; it no longer leaves a previous destination-register value visible.
The focused amd64 DTrace module rebuilt and linked in
dtrace-kmod-native/ptinfo-build2.log using the existing GENERIC-NODEBUG
generated headers. This is a build and source-path check, not live guest
fault-containment qualification.
The DIF HyperTrace hypercall opcode now sets CPU_DTRACE_ILLOP on non-amd64
instead of silently completing without an event. The amd64 DTrace module
rebuilt in dtrace-kmod-native/nonamd64-opcode-build.log.
The full 16-job arm64 GENERIC kernel build then passed in
buildkernel-arm64-opcode.log, including its DTrace module and kernel link.
The full 16-job RISC-V GENERIC kernel build also passed in
buildkernel-riscv-opcode.log, including its DTrace module and kernel link.
These establish compile/link support, not live guest execution.
The 16-job native development buildworld then passed in
buildworld-post-arm64.log, rechecking the shared-header and cross-build
changes on amd64. It did not use publication malloc settings, publish
packages, install an upgrade, or boot a HyperTrace VM.

2026-10-14 RISC-V provider ELF ABI continuation:
The first full RISC-V development buildworld reached libc but failed when
the cross-host dtrace -G output had a soft-float ELF header and could not
link with target drti.o's RVC/double-float ABI (buildworld-riscv-identity.log).
The linker now derives RISC-V ELF flags from its input objects, checks that
their float ABI and RVE mode agree, and combines compatible feature flags.
Its native-ABI fallback is used only when there is no input object. A focused
cross-host provider link produced a 0x5 RVC/double-float DOF object matching
both its RISC-V input and drti.o, and rejected deliberately mixed soft/double
float input objects. Fixtures and outputs are under
/usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/riscv-abi-fixture/. The full RISC-V
development buildworld rerun is in progress in buildworld-riscv-abi2.log;
the focused result does not yet establish a passing world or live execution.
The repeatable riscv-provider-abi.py test passed with the rebuilt host DTrace
tool in riscv-abi-regression: its generated provider object, input object and
drti.o all have 0x5 flags, compatible RVC flags from multiple objects are
combined, and mixed soft/double-float inputs are rejected.
The resumed RISC-V build also passed the prior libc nameres_provider.o and
.pico link failure; readelf reports 0x5 for the generated libc object. Later
world stages are still running.

2026-10-14 bounded TAP provenance probe continuation:
The bhyve TAP send and receive probes now copy an identifier only when the
actual record contains the complete 32-byte tag after any VirtIO header.
The copy works across iovec boundaries, including the one-iovec receive and
discard paths; zero-length and short records no longer expose an invalid or
out-of-bounds probe pointer. The bounce-buffer receive probe only reports
bytes copied to its caller. The focused packet-provenance sanitizer test passed
in packet-provenance-probes, exercising the actual TAP send and one-iovec
receive functions, split tags, zero-length sends and short bounce records.
The native bhyve net_backends.pieo rebuilt in tap-probe-native-build4.log.
Virtio-net still has no negotiated end-to-end provenance transport, so this
does not establish cross-guest packet identity or resolve the mbuf allocation
cost. No live network throughput measurement has been made.

2026-10-14 RISC-V full-world qualification result:
The 16-job RISC-V development buildworld completed successfully in
buildworld-riscv-abi2.log (2038 seconds). It compiled and linked the
target-aware DTrace provider linker, libc's provider object, libvmmapi,
dtraced and bhyve. The libc nameres_provider.o ELF flags are 0x5
(RVC/double-float ABI), matching its target input and drti.o. The final
RISC-V bhyve net_backends.pieo was built after the bounded TAP probe source
change. This is compile/link qualification only; no RISC-V guest was run.

2026-10-14 guest page-walk direct-map guard:
The amd64 DTrace guest page walker now rejects physical-to-direct-map
translation when dmaplimit is zero. That value is tolerated by an early-boot
pmap macro, but HyperTrace probe-context translation must not treat it as an
unbounded map. The actual extracted walker passed its ASan/UBSan pagewalk
suite in pagewalk-zero-dmap, including the new zero-limit case. The native
DTrace module rebuilt and linked in dtrace-kmod-native/zero-dmap-build.log.
This does not substitute for live guest fault-containment testing.

2026-10-14 replay acknowledgment path audit:
The host control daemon already sends a one-byte response after handling an
installation observation, and bhyve waits for it in hypertrace_write(). That
response currently ends at bhyve. The guest's dttransport write and VirtIO
enqueue report local queue acceptance, not a durable host receipt; the guest
then retires a completed delivery after queueing its terminal observation.
Restart-safe replay therefore needs an identified host receipt carried back
through VirtIO/dttransport to the guest and a bounded durable ledger whose
installed and terminal states can be reconciled after either daemon restarts.
No replay or stored-ELF launch was enabled by this audit.

2026-10-14 host-observation receipt transport:
Bhyve now queues an identity-bound host-acceptance event only after the host
control daemon acknowledges an INSTALLED or UNINSTALLED report. The VirtIO
guest driver forwards that event through dttransport. The guest daemon checks
the delivery token, VMID, epoch, program ID and source ID; it retains terminal
delivery context until the matching host UNINSTALLED response arrives. Guest
and host queues remain bounded and fail closed on transport errors. The 27
native port suites passed, including valid, malformed and stale receipt cases;
an amd64 16-job development buildworld compiled dtraced and bhyve successfully
in buildworld-host-observation2.log. The first buildworld caught a missing
header; this was fixed before the successful second run. The matching 16-job
GENERIC buildkernel passed in buildkernel-host-observation.log, including the
dttransport and virtio_dtrace sources.
The response reports host handling within the current connection only. Host
installation state is still volatile, so this is neither a durable receipt
nor authorization for automatic replay. A bounded fsynced transaction ledger,
restart reconciliation, VM save/restore session codec and live host/guest
qualification remain open. This machine has no /dev/vmm or /dev/dttransport;
no installed live VM test was performed.

2026-10-14 bounded host delivery journal:
The host now durably records a versioned, SHA-256-checked delivery transaction
in the outbound spool before forwarding any ELF bytes. A record is keyed by
the bhyve session ID and delivery token and binds VMID, epoch, program and
source IDs, and the socket peer UID/PID. Directory-synced atomic renames move
it through pending, queue-accepted, installed and terminal states. The host
does not acknowledge installation or exit until the corresponding state is
durable. It can match an idempotent observation to that record after losing
its in-memory outbound connection. Startup scans and validates all records,
rejecting malformed, duplicate, or excess entries. The journal is capped at
16,384 records and fails closed at capacity; safe epoch/session garbage
collection has not yet been implemented.
Sanitized native-daemon qualification passed all 20 component suites in
native-daemon-ledger-qualified.log. The installed-receipt suite exercises
reopen/recovery, identity mismatch, terminal idempotence and corrupted-record
rejection. The spool-publish suite injects fsync and rename failures into the
actual journal transitions. A 16-job amd64 development buildworld passed in
buildworld-ledger-qualified.log. Cross-target rebuild results follow separately.
This makes the host acknowledgment durable within a verified session, but
neither the guest daemon's delivery context nor the program execution state is
durable yet. Reconnect negotiation, guest retransmission, safe replay,
revocation reconciliation, terminal-record garbage collection, VM snapshot
session codec and live VM validation remain open. Automatic restart execution
remains disabled.

2026-10-14 delivery-journal cross-build qualification:
The final 16-job amd64 development buildworld passed in
buildworld-ledger-qualified.log, arm64/aarch64 buildworld passed in
buildworld-arm64-ledger.log, and riscv/riscv64 buildworld passed in
buildworld-riscv-ledger.log. All three compiled the changed daemon. This
establishes compile/link coverage, not live execution on those machines.

2026-10-14 guest delivery journal in progress:
The guest daemon now records an identity-bound delivery before accepting a
newly published ELF, advances the durable phase before reporting installation
or exit, and retains delivery context until a matching host terminal receipt.
Startup validates guest journal entries and recovers nonterminal identity as
unauthorized for execution; a stored ELF alone cannot launch a tracing child.
The disk format is HTD3 with an explicit host/guest scope and SHA-256 check.
The focused guest protocol test, all 20 sanitized native daemon component
suites, and all 27 native port suites pass; process-launch exercises the real
guest journal through pending, exit, and terminal receipt. A 16-job amd64
development buildworld passed in buildworld-guest-ledger.log.
This does not yet provide reconnect retransmission or authorization to replay
an ELF after restart. Safe session reconciliation, terminal journal garbage
collection, VM save/restore session encoding, and live VM qualification remain
open. Existing HTD2 experimental host journal files are now read with their
original SHA-256 checksum and normalized in memory to host-scoped HTD3
records. They can continue through normal phase transitions without a startup
rewrite; guest records still require HTD3. The compatibility test covers
reopening an HTD2 record, normal phase advancement, and checksum rejection.
The 16-job amd64 development buildworld passed in buildworld-htd2-compat.log.

2026-10-14 recovered guest delivery termination:
A full retransmission with the same VMID, epoch, token, program ID and source
ID as a recovered guest transaction now durably advances that transaction to
exited and reports UNINSTALLED to the host. The guest retains the recovered
identity until the matching host terminal receipt. If the journal transition
fails, it sends no terminal report and a retransmission can retry. This closes
the silent-duplicate path where the host otherwise waited indefinitely after
a guest daemon restart. The received bytes are not executed merely because an
old ELF exists. The focused guest protocol suite covers successful retry,
journal-transition failure and host receipt. All 20 sanitized native daemon
suites and all 27 native port suites passed in the recovered-terminal
validation directories. A 16-job amd64 development buildworld passed in
buildworld-recovered-terminal.log.
This is failure reconciliation, not authorized program replay; reconnect
negotiation, snapshot session state and live VM qualification remain open.

2026-10-14 guest source revocation reconciliation:
A DTT_KILL source tombstone now also removes current launch authority without
discarding its delivery identity. For an unowned delivery, the daemon fsyncs
the exited phase before sending UNINSTALLED and keeps the identity until the
host acknowledges it. For a delivery owned by a tracing child, the reaper
remains responsible for that report after it observes the child exit. The
revocation/OOM component test exercises both paths, the matching host receipt,
and an allocation failure after the tombstone; the tombstone still prevents
launch in the failure case. A 16-job amd64 development buildworld passed in
buildworld-revocation-ledger.log. All 20 sanitized native daemon suites passed
in native-daemon-revocation-ledger-final.
This improves revocation recovery but does not yet implement a complete
reconnect replay protocol or safe terminal-record garbage collection.

2026-10-14 guest terminal journal reclamation:
The guest now deletes a delivery marker only after the matching host terminal
receipt has been durably reflected in the guest journal. The host has already
fsynced its terminal record before sending that receipt. The guest fsyncs the
directory after unlink; a failed unlink is harmless and a startup scan retries
cleanup of validated terminal records. Nonterminal guest records and all host
records remain protected from this reclamation. The real process-launch test
covers pending-record refusal, host receipt deletion, and reopening a terminal
record left by an interrupted cleanup. All 20 sanitized native daemon suites
passed in native-daemon-terminal-gc, all 27 native port suites passed in
native-port-terminal-gc, and a 16-job amd64 development buildworld passed in
buildworld-terminal-gc.log.
This bounds completed guest history but does not solve host journal GC or
session replay. The VirtIO trace snapshot callback still rejects save/restore
because a queue image cannot restore its external daemon session; a complete
session codec remains necessary before enabling snapshots with this device.

2026-10-14 host delivery phase query foundation:
The local dtraced protocol is now version 11 and accepts a read-only STATUS
request from a forwarder with READDATA subscription. The control daemon
checks the current VMM attestation, forwarder session and peer credentials,
VMID/epoch, delivery token, program and source IDs before returning the
durable host phase and per-VM source-revocation bit. An absent matching record
is reported explicitly; a record with mismatched identity is rejected. The
response byte and fixed-size status are written under one socket output lock.
Bhyve has a matching bounded query function that verifies the returned token,
phase, revocation bit and reserved bytes and drops the connection on an
invalid or truncated reply. Host receipt tests cover pending, installed,
absent, revoked and rejected identity cases. The bhyve socket suite covers a
valid status frame and an invalid token. All 20 sanitized native daemon suites
passed in native-daemon-status-query-v2, all 27 native port suites passed in
native-port-status-query-v2, and a 16-job amd64 development buildworld passed
in buildworld-status-query-v2.log. The first buildworld caught a const-qualifier
compile error in the new bhyve sender; it was fixed before the successful run.
The status query currently stops at bhyve. Guest VirtIO/dttransport carriage,
startup reconciliation and explicit replay authorization are still open; no
stored ELF is launched by this query.

2026-10-14 guest recovery status carriage:
The STATUS request now crosses guest dttransport, VirtIO and bhyve to the
host's durable delivery ledger. Bhyve returns the host phase and source
revocation bit through a bounded control-queue receipt. On guest-daemon
startup, recovered identities are queried one at a time with a deadline. A
terminal or revoked-and-absent answer retires the guest marker; pending,
accepted or installed answers durably advance the guest to exited and send
UNINSTALLED. A newly arriving token for the same program is retired while an
older recovered transaction is unresolved. No recovered ELF is executed from
disk on the strength of a status answer. The actual socket recovery component
test covers installed and terminal answers; the focused guest test covers the
overlap and exact retry, and the native port suites cover the VirtIO and bhyve
wire carriage. All 21 sanitized native daemon suites and all 27 native port
suites passed in the status-overlap-final validation directories. A 16-job
amd64 development buildworld and GENERIC buildkernel passed in the
buildworld-status-overlap-final.log and buildkernel-status-carriage.log records.
This path still needs complete reconnect handling, an explicit authorized replay
protocol, a snapshot session codec and live host/guest qualification.

2026-10-14 transient host-query recovery:
If bhyve cannot reach the host control daemon for a delivery STATUS query,
it now sends a distinct unavailable result through VirtIO and dttransport.
The guest keeps its recovered journal record and retries after one second;
the unavailable result never authorizes or launches an ELF. Host protocol
rejection still resets the device. The recovery-query component test uses the
real guest socket reader to check two unavailable replies, repeated queries, and final
installed-phase reconciliation. The bhyve and guest-driver tests check the
new control value and its rejection of a nonzero revocation bit. Silent reply
loss still times out fail-closed; a complete reconnect/session replay protocol
and VM snapshot state remain open. All 21 sanitized daemon suites and 27 native
port suites passed in native-daemon-status-unavailable-repeat and
native-port-status-unavailable. The 16-job amd64, arm64/aarch64 and
riscv/riscv64 development buildworld runs passed in their status-unavailable
logs; GENERIC buildkernel passed for all three targets in the corresponding
status-unavailable logs. These are compile and component-test results, not live
VM or kernel-module execution.

2026-10-14 lost status-reply recovery:
Recovered guest STATUS requests now carry a nonzero query ID across
dttransport, VirtIO and bhyve, which echoes it in the host-status reply.
The guest daemon seeds a per-run counter and advances it on each request.
After ten seconds without a response it repeats the query with a fresh ID;
a delayed reply for the prior ID is ignored before any journal or launch
decision. Lifecycle receipts require a zero query ID. The real socket
component test drops a reply, observes the repeat, injects a stale terminal
answer, and then reconciles the current installed answer without executing
the recovered ELF. This improves liveness under lost replies but does not
provide authorized replay, VM snapshot restoration, or live validation. All
21 sanitized native daemon suites and 27 native port suites passed in the
status-nonce-final2 and status-nonce-final validation directories. The 16-job
amd64, arm64/aarch64 and riscv/riscv64 development buildworld runs passed in
their status-nonce logs; GENERIC buildkernel passed on all three targets.
No live VM or kernel-module run was possible on this host because /dev/vmm
and /dev/dttransport are absent.

2026-10-14 digest-bound replay grant for recovered guest deliveries:
The local protocol is now version 13. A STATUS response can grant replay only
for an accepted or installed, unrevoked delivery still owned by a live matching
forwarder and VM epoch. The host hashes the identified stored ELF and carries
the SHA-256 digest and grant through bhyve, VirtIO and dttransport. The guest
checks the recovered journal record and its local revocation state, compares
the stored ELF with the host digest, schedules that one file for reprocessing,
and checks the digest again before launch. A replayed child must report a
positive installation before the guest treats it as installed. With no current
tracing identifiers, replay waits instead of launching an unconfirmed child.
The directory watcher now observes its periodic wakeup so an authorized
reprocess request can actually run. A failed open is the only condition treated
as a missing artifact; malformed or unreadable opened files fail closed.

This authorizes recovery while the original host forwarder is still live. It
does not persist that forwarder ownership across host-daemon restart, encode an
external session in a VM snapshot, or provide end-to-end VirtIO-net packet
provenance. The VirtIO trace device still rejects save/restore rather than
restoring an incomplete queue image. Live host/guest qualification remains
unavailable on this host without /dev/vmm and /dev/dttransport.
The replay-digest native daemon run passed all 21 sanitized component suites
after correcting the process-launch fixture. The native port run passed all 27
component suites, including bhyve socket and VirtIO grant carriage. The
16-job amd64, arm64/aarch64 and riscv/riscv64 world and GENERIC kernel builds
passed. The amd64 and arm64 world builds were repeated after the final
post-open error classification fix.

2026-10-14 host-daemon restart ownership reconstruction:
The local protocol is now version 14. On admission of a forwarder's ELF
receive socket, the control daemon scans the durable delivery journal before
publishing the socket. It restores only accepted or installed, unrevoked
deliveries whose session ID, peer PID and UID, attested VMID and VM epoch all
match that live socket. Pending, terminal, revoked and foreign records grant
no ownership. A STATUS query for an accepted or installed delivery whose
receive socket has not yet rejoined returns the existing unavailable phase,
so the guest keeps its marker and retries rather than terminating it. Bhyve
accepts that authenticated unavailable answer and forwards it through the
existing VirtIO/dttransport retry path. The native daemon suite passed all 21
sanitized cases, including the host receipt reconstruction checks, and the
native port suite passed all 27 cases, including the bhyve status socket.

This recovery applies while the same bhyve process and VM epoch survive a
control-daemon restart. It does not authorize a new bhyve process, restore a
VM snapshot session, or settle host terminal-ledger garbage collection. A
missing or invalid stored ELF still receives no replay grant. Live VM testing
remains unavailable without /dev/vmm and /dev/dttransport.
The final protocol-14 world build passed on amd64, arm64/aarch64 and
riscv/riscv64 with 16 jobs per build. No kernel source changed in this
continuation; the GENERIC builds from the preceding replay-digest pass remain
the latest kernel compile checks.

2026-10-14 packet-ID access preparation and snapshot dependency check:
The active kernel sources now access the packet provenance ID through
`m_mbufid()` in BPF, socket send/receive, UDP probes, sglist, TAP and VirtIO-net.
The accessor still returns the existing packet-header field, so packet layout,
wire format and allocation size have not changed. This isolates the direct
call sites before an optional sidecar representation and avoids mixing the
future layout change with protocol work. The focused TAP provenance test passed.
GENERIC kernel builds passed for amd64, arm64/aarch64 and riscv/riscv64 after
the source was held stable. An earlier amd64 build hit clang lexer assertions
in unrelated driver files while `mbuf.h` was being edited; the stable-source
rebuild passed. All 27 native port suites passed after the access refactor.
The 512-byte mbuf cost and its throughput qualification remain open.

Snapshot inspection confirms that a restored VM has a newly allocated
HyperTrace epoch while the saved guest journal contains the previous epoch.
The current VirtIO trace snapshot callback continues to reject save/restore.
Enabling it requires an explicit external-session handoff, guest journal
reconciliation, stale-callback retirement and reinstallation under the new
epoch; copying its queue alone would not satisfy those invariants.

2026-10-14 BPF feedback packet lineage:
The BPF write path now assigns and audits the packet ID before creating its
optional feedback copy. `m_dup()` copies the packet header and tags, so the
transmitted packet and feedback packet now retain the same provenance ID.
Previously the feedback copy was made first and carried a zero ID. GENERIC
buildkernel passed with 16 jobs on amd64, arm64/aarch64 and riscv/riscv64.
This does not change the mbuf layout or resolve the 512-byte allocation cost;
the proposed sidecar and VirtIO-net wire propagation still need review and
performance qualification.

2026-10-14 packet-ID writer and allocation-failure preparation:
`m_mbufid_ensure(m, how)` now marks every active packet-ID writer; it still
returns the embedded header field and cannot fail in the current layout.
BPF and datagram sockets request `M_WAITOK`. TAP requests `M_NOWAIT` only for
a valid incoming ID and drops the packet with `ENOBUFS` if a future sidecar
allocation fails; an all-zero prefix leaves the packet untagged. The focused
TAP test injects that failure and checks that the packet is freed rather
than delivered. The full 27-case native port suite passed. Sixteen-job
GENERIC kernel builds, including modules affected by mbuf.h, passed on
amd64, arm64/aarch64 and riscv/riscv64. No world, live kernel or VM traffic
measurement was run in this round.

The proposed layout change is still separate because the earlier automatic
review rejected the kernel-wide ABI change. Active `sys/` users of the field
are now isolated behind accessors; direct references remain only in the
vendored upstream copy and native test stub. A tag-backed implementation
must use a persistent packet tag so `m_dup_pkthdr()` deep-copies it,
`m_move_pkthdr()` transfers it and interface cleanup retains it. Its missing
ID read must use stable zero storage, including the scatter/gather path that
retains a DMA address. The 24-byte LP64 tag header plus 32-byte ID requests
56 bytes per tagged packet before allocator rounding, while an untagged
packet needs no tag. Returning to `MSIZE=256` would change the mbuf ABI for
all kernel modules, so a full rebuild, live packet-copy/forwarding tests,
resident-memory measurements and throughput/latency comparison are required
before deployment. These measurements and the VirtIO-net end-to-end format
are still open.

VirtIO-net wire audit in this round: the bhyve TAP backend implements
`tap_tagging_enable()` and can inspect a provenance prefix, but production
`pci_virtio_net.c` never calls `netbe_tagging_enable()`. Its transmit path
strips the guest VirtIO header and writes a backend header plus Ethernet
frame, without an ID; its receive path interprets backend bytes as a VirtIO
header plus Ethernet frame. Enabling TAP tagging alone would therefore
misframe packets. Guest `if_vtnet` currently observes local IDs in probes
but has no negotiated ID-bearing wire header. A complete change must
negotiate a 5BSD-specific format on both ends, preserve the ordinary VirtIO
format for other guests and backends, adjust MTU/length and snapshot checks,
and test both directions and queue modes. The OASIS VirtIO 1.4 feature table
does not provide a vendor-reserved net feature bit; choosing an apparently
unused standard bit without an extension identity risks a future collision
(https://docs.oasis-open.org/virtio/virtio/v1.4/cs01/virtio-v1.4-cs01.pdf).

2026-10-14 VirtIO boundary and lifecycle qualification:
The TAP backend can now disable provenance tagging on device reset, and the
bhyve VirtIO-net device refuses to activate ordinary guest framing while a
tagged backend remains enabled. This fails closed rather than silently
interpreting the 32-byte prefix as Ethernet. Host and backend harness cases
cover reset, disable failure, and refusal of unnegotiated framing. The guest
VirtIO DTrace driver now tears down its interrupt before draining queues.
The bhyve VirtIO DTrace worker honors suspend/resume, replaces its fixed
reconnect sleep with an interruptible condition wait, and explicitly rejects
checkpoint preflight because its external session cannot be restored. The
WASPNest private-interface ledger records this checkpoint boundary.

The VirtIO requirements gate passed, all 27 native HyperTrace suites passed,
the bhyve device built, and GENERIC kernel builds passed with 16 jobs on
amd64, arm64/aarch64 and riscv/riscv64. The aggregate WASPNest device harness
also passed. These checks do not establish
live guest transport or checkpoint support; both remain open.

2026-10-14 monitor fork and host worker shutdown:
The HyperTrace connector now starts after the bhyve monitor forks, in the VM
child and before PCI device initialization. Previously the child inherited a
`started` flag and mutex state but not the connector thread. Successful host
transport initialization registers process-exit cleanup; the later VirtIO
DTrace registration stops and joins its reader and communicator before the
transport closes its sockets. The device leaves PCI state allocated until
process termination because other vCPU threads may still refer to it during
exit. A native worker test exercises reader and communicator shutdown.
The 27-suite native port check, VirtIO requirements gate, and incremental
bhyve build passed. A live
monitor restart and guest reconnect remain unverified without /dev/vmm.

2026-10-14 durable source-revocation capacity:
The host revocation journal now limits `.kill` records to 16,384, matching
the delivery-record bound. New kills fail closed at capacity, while an exact
retry of an already durable revocation still succeeds. Replay scans and
revocation queries reject an over-cap directory, and a failed replay scan
does not publish a partial result. A sanitized boundary test fills the
journal, checks the duplicate and overflow paths, and confirms recovery
rejects an extra record. All 22 sanitized native daemon component suites and
the incremental dtraced build passed. This bounds resource use but does not
implement safe, session-aware revocation or terminal-record reclamation.

2026-10-14 restore ingress fence:
HyperTrace host initialization now prepares its directories and session ID
without starting the connector. On an ordinary boot, bhyve starts the
connector after PCI and TPM device initialization. On checkpoint restore or
inbound migration, it starts only after the restore transaction succeeds.
The VirtIO DTrace reader may exist during restore preflight, but it sees no
configured transport, so no external ELF or KILL frame can mutate guest
queues or memory before validation. DTrace checkpoints still fail closed
until external-session handoff, guest journal remapping, and probe
reinstallation exist. The native connect test exercises prepared, started,
and destroyed states, plus an injected connector-thread creation failure
followed by a successful retry. All 27 native port suites and the incremental
bhyve build passed. Live checkpoint/migration execution remains unverified because
/dev/vmm and /dev/dttransport are absent on this host.

2026-10-14 guest VirtIO-net control-command result:
The guest control-queue helper now reports a busy queue, enqueue failure,
or broken queue as an error instead of returning success after an
unsubmitted or incomplete command. This is necessary before a private
packet-provenance control exchange can safely change framing. An incremental
amd64 GENERIC buildkernel passed with 16 jobs. Packet-ID wire negotiation
and live host/guest qualification remain open.

2026-10-14 negotiated VirtIO-net packet provenance (experimental):
Modern 5BSD vtnet guests now request the private 5BSDID01 control-queue
format before posting receive buffers. bhyve enables TAP tagging only for a
quiescent TAP backend with no backend VirtIO header, and carries the 32-byte
packet ID after the standard guest VirtIO header in both directions. A
rejected command leaves ordinary guests and backends on standard framing;
failure to publish the ACK rolls back tagging while the receive lock is held.
An exact command retry is idempotent. The guest creates an ID for otherwise
unidentified transmit packets, imports valid receive IDs before stripping
the header, and bypasses software LRO for identified packets so two packet
lineages cannot be collapsed into one. IDs with a zero node origin are
invalid: before VirtIO DTrace assigns a node ID, the guest transmits a zero
ID in the negotiated prefix rather than exporting a colliding identity.
TAP's peek buffer now fits a full GSO record plus the ID prefix.

The private format is recorded in virtio-nonstandard-interfaces.tsv as
VIRTIO-PRIVATE-116. Active tagged network mode rejects VM checkpoint
preflight and snapshot callbacks until a versioned state codec exists.
The full WASPNest device harness passed (63 VirtIO-net checks, zero failures),
as did the focused TAP provenance sanitizer test and 27 native HyperTrace
port suites. The bhyve build and final 16-job GENERIC kernel builds for
amd64, arm64/aarch64 and riscv/riscv64 passed; logs are in
/usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation. These tests
do not prove a live cross-guest packet path: /dev/vmm and /dev/dttransport
are absent on this host. The 512-byte mbuf allocation cost, full
checkpoint/session replay and live qualification remain open.

2026-10-14 revocation/admission handoff:
The daemon now holds its revocation lock from live KILL subscriber selection
through durable kill publication and job queueing. A newly admitted KILL
subscriber takes the same lock while scanning durable kills. This closes the
selection-to-commit gap in which a connecting subscriber could miss both the
live notification and replay. A regression case checks that the replay scan
waits for an in-progress publication. The sanitized daemon build and all 22
component suites passed, as did all 27 native port suites and the 16-job
native dtraced build. This does not yet
make revocation session-aware, reclaim terminal records, or enable VM restore.

2026-10-14 kill-request receiver boundary:
The daemon now accepts a KILL request only from a live local consumer
subscribed to READDATA, with a header-only frame and valid PID, VM ID,
epoch, and source ID. A forwarder can no longer submit an arbitrary
revocation through the local control socket. The receipt test rejects
forwarder-origin and extra-payload kills before the journal changes; the
OOM and failed-rearm tests now use a consumer origin. The native dtraced
build and all 22 sanitized daemon component suites passed. This verifies
the receiver role and framing, not ownership of a source by a particular
consumer or session.

2026-10-14 daemon descriptor-reaper lifecycle:
The retired-client reaper now waits on a condition variable instead of an
uninterruptible five-second sleep. Socket retirement and completed job
cleanup wake it promptly; a timed sweep still handles references released
later. Shutdown wakes the reaper immediately, so finalization need not wait
for the old polling interval. A lifecycle test retires a clean client,
checks prompt reclamation, then checks that the idle reaper exits promptly
on shutdown. The native dtraced build and all 22 sanitized daemon suites
passed. This is daemon shutdown qualification, not live VM teardown.

2026-10-14 recovered guest ELF retry:
A retransmission of the same delivery token while the guest daemon awaits
host status no longer turns that token into a terminal uninstall. The guest
verifies the received bytes against its identified spool and keeps the
recovered journal entry unauthorized until the acknowledged status query
settles it. A different token is still refused while the predecessor is
unresolved; a token already marked terminal repeats its uninstall. The
focused sanitized guest daemon test covers matching and changed bytes and
the terminal retry. The native dtraced build, all 22 sanitized daemon
component suites, and all 27 native port suites passed. This fixes one
idempotence path, not restore-epoch remapping or complete session replay.

2026-10-14 guest-local forwarder admission:
The guest daemon's dttransport writer previously sent a FORWARDER hello
with an all-zero session ID. The listener rejected it, and even a valid
session would have been passed to host-only outbound-delivery recovery,
which requires a VMM identity absent in a guest. The writer now creates a
nonzero random session and validates its own hello before transmission;
the listener performs outbound ownership recovery only in control-machine
mode. The handshake constructor is shared with a validation test, and the
sender checks its own frame before transmission. A socket-level native test
verifies guest-local ELFWRITE forwarder admission without a host ledger
while retaining rejection of an unattested host forwarder. The native
dtraced build and final 22-suite sanitized daemon run passed with the
shared constructor. Live guest transport
still needs validation with /dev/dttransport and a VM.

2026-10-14 guest ELF forwarding after admission:
The newly admitted guest-local forwarder still could not send its outbound
ELF: job::send_elf required a host-attested VM target and a durable host
delivery record, while the guest device writer accepts only a bare ELF.
The sender now keeps host target, revocation, and delivery-ledger handling
on control machines; guest forwarders send a bare ELF without the default
SHA prefix and await the device writer's matching delivery acknowledgment.
The host stamps the actual guest VM identity when bhyve receives the ELF.
A sanitized native spool test covers guest framing, acknowledgment, and
rejection of a host target envelope on that path while retaining the host
delivery tests. The native dtraced build, all 22 sanitized daemon suites,
and diff whitespace check passed. This does not prove reliable end-to-end
delivery across a guest or host crash, nor live VM operation; those remain
open integration work.

2026-10-14 guest-to-host ELF delivery identity:
The guest device writer now copies its nonzero local delivery token into
every dttransport ELF fragment. The guest VirtIO driver rejects a zero
token, and bhyve rejects zero or changed tokens across fragments before
submitting the assembled ELF to the host daemon. Bhyve includes that token
in the host ELF header; VM identity still comes from vm_get_identity, never
from a guest-supplied VM field. Guest writer, guest driver and bhyve device
regressions cover the token and malformed fragments. All 27 native port
suites, the focused guest daemon and driver tests, the bhyve native build,
the 16-job GENERIC buildkernel (guest-delivery-kernel.log), and diff
whitespace check passed. This preserves the identity needed for
an end-to-end host receipt; the guest still receives only local queue
acceptance today, so crash-safe guest-to-host delivery remains open.

2026-10-14 guest ELF host-acceptance receipt:
Bhyve now queues a HOST_ELF event only after the host control daemon
acknowledges the complete guest ELF. The event carries the preserved
delivery token, VMM-derived VMID and epoch, and program/source IDs through
VirtIO and dttransport. The guest daemon checks those fields and withholds
its local forwarder acknowledgment until the matching host event arrives;
late or mismatched tokens cannot release a different sender. Timeout and
shutdown fail closed. The host daemon also rejects revoked inbound sources
under its spool publication lock, avoiding a success receipt for an ELF it
discarded. The guest tests cover receipt gating, wrong identities, stale
tokens and shutdown without a host receipt; the device tests cover the new
event. All 22 sanitized native daemon suites, 27 native port suites, the
native dtraced and bhyve builds, the 16-job GENERIC buildkernel, and diff
whitespace check passed. This acknowledges current host-daemon handling;
it does not yet provide a persistent guest outbound transaction or
automatic replay after a guest or bhyve crash. No live VM test ran because
/dev/vmm and /dev/dttransport are absent here.

2026-10-14 live outbound forwarder-admission gap:
The outbound watcher previously marked a newly published ELF as handled
even if no forwarder was connected, leaving no job to send when the local
guest writer or a host VM forwarder later connected. It now leaves such a
file pending and requests a directory scan after ELFWRITE forwarder
admission. The subscriber selection and file memorization occur under the
same socket-list lock, closing the handoff race. Startup files remain
memorized by populate_existing(), so this does not enable automatic replay
of stale ELF across daemon restart. A native regression publishes without
a forwarder, then verifies that the same file queues after one appears.
The native dtraced build and all 23 sanitized daemon suites passed, as did
diff whitespace validation. Persistent guest outbound transaction replay,
session save/restore and live VM qualification remain open.

2026-10-14 guest outbound transaction journal:
The guest daemon now writes a bounded, checksummed scope-2 transaction
record before sending any outbound ELF bytes to its local device writer.
The record binds the forwarder session and peer credentials, delivery token,
program ID and source ID. A matching host-acceptance receipt advances it
to accepted; normal completion durably retires the accepted record so steady
traffic does not exhaust the 16,384-record journal limit. A missing or wrong
acknowledgment leaves a pending record. Startup validates scope and phase,
retires any accepted record left by a crash, and keeps pending artifacts
fenced from automatic replay. The spool and journal regressions cover
accepted and pending outcomes, reopened scope-2 records, and retirement.
The native dtraced build, all 23 sanitized daemon suites, and diff
whitespace check passed. This records uncertainty after a crash but does
not yet reconcile a pending token with the host or authorize retransmission;
the current record also lacks a full payload digest for such a grant.

2026-10-14 guest outbound payload digest:
New guest outbound transactions use an HTD4 journal record carrying the
full SHA-256 digest of the outbound ELF, alongside the session, peer,
delivery token, program and source identities. HTD3 host and guest-inbound
records retain their existing format. Startup can read and retire old HTD3
scope-2 records, but cannot create new undigested scope-2 records. Journal
readback rejects a corrupted payload digest or record checksum. The native
dtraced build and all 23 sanitized daemon suites passed; a focused updated
spool-publish regression additionally passed corrupted-digest rejection and
legacy HTD3 readback. The digest is evidence for a future reconciliation
protocol, not authorization to retransmit: pending scope-2 transactions stay
fenced on restart. Host reconciliation, VM snapshot session restoration and
live host/guest validation remain open.

2026-10-14 pending guest outbound restart check:
During guest daemon recovery, each pending HTD4 outbound transaction is
checked against the identified spool file if it still exists. A changed or
invalid ELF now fails startup with a protocol error; a missing file remains
an unresolved pending transaction and is never replayed from the journal.
Legacy HTD3 transactions remain fenced. This validates the stored digest
before any future host reconciliation can use it; it does not establish
host acceptance or authorize a retry. The focused regression covers absent,
matching and modified spool files. The native dtraced build, all 23 sanitized
daemon suites and diff whitespace validation passed.

2026-10-14 host guest-ELF envelope validation:
Before the host control daemon publishes an ELF from a VMM forwarder, it now
requires the inbound location and subscription, a live VM attestation, a
nonzero delivery token and program/source IDs, and a complete versioned
envelope whose VMID, epoch and name match that attestation. The bytes after
the envelope must begin with an ELF header. Direct local host submissions
retain their existing path. Malformed-envelope regression cases, the native
dtraced build, all 23 sanitized daemon suites and whitespace validation
passed. This closes a forged or stale forwarded-payload acceptance path and
provides authenticated context for the future host ingest ledger. The host
still needs that durable token ledger and a query/reconciliation protocol;
this check alone does not authorize guest retry.

2026-10-14 host guest-ingest transaction journal:
An attested guest ELF now gets a bounded, digest-bound HTD4 scope-3 record
in the host inbound directory. The host fsyncs its pending token before
publishing the spool file, then fsyncs the accepted transition before
acknowledging bhyve. The record binds the VMM forwarder session and peer,
VMID and epoch, delivery token, program and source IDs, and the SHA-256 of
the bare guest ELF. The inbound watcher leaves a pending file unhandled;
after acceptance it can queue the file once. An exact accepted retry checks
the stored payload when present and returns acceptance without requeuing it.
An unresolved pending retry can finish publication and clear a startup
watcher marker while the pending fence is still active. Host startup now
accepts only pending or accepted scope-3 records in that directory.

The native dtraced build and all 24 sanitized daemon suites passed,
including an attested host-ingest regression for malformed envelope,
accepted retry, changed payload, recovered pending file and watcher fence;
diff whitespace validation passed. This ledger does not yet expose an
authenticated query to the guest, retire completed host records, or resolve
accepted files that were memorized during host restart before execution.
Automatic guest retry remains disabled. Live host/guest validation remains
unavailable without /dev/vmm and /dev/dttransport.

2026-10-14 host ingest status-query foundation:
The local protocol is now version 15. A bhyve READDATA forwarder with a
current VMM attestation can query its host inbound transaction by session,
peer credentials, VMID/epoch, delivery token, program and source IDs. The
host returns absent, pending or accepted, the source revocation bit, and the
stored bare-ELF digest for a known transaction. A conflicting identity under
the same session/token fails the query. Bhyve validates the reply shape and
disconnects on malformed phases, missing digests, nonzero replay flags or
wrong tokens. The attested daemon regression and bhyve socket regression
cover matching and conflicting queries. Native dtraced and bhyve builds,
all 24 sanitized daemon suites and all 27 native port suites passed.
The guest VirtIO/dttransport query path and safe retry/retirement policy are
still open; an absent answer does not authorize retransmission.

2026-10-14 host ingest query transport:
Distinct ingress-status request/reply events now cross dttransport, guest
VirtIO, bhyve and the attested host-daemon socket. Bhyve stamps the VM's
current VMID, epoch and name, validates the host reply, and returns an
explicit unavailable phase when only the host control socket is lost.
The guest VirtIO driver validates pending/accepted digests, absent and
unavailable zero-digest replies, query IDs and the no-replay flag before
queuing a response to dttransport. Malformed identity or protocol replies
fail closed. The native bhyve build, virtio_dtrace kernel-module build and
all 27 native port suites passed, including VMM and guest-driver cases.
The guest daemon does not yet issue this query or reconcile pending scope-2
records at this point in the log, so this was transport qualification rather
than crash-safe replay.

2026-10-14 recovered guest outbound ingest reconciliation:
At startup, the guest daemon queries each recovered HTD4 scope-2 pending
token through dttransport. Guest queries carry zero VMID and epoch because
only bhyve knows the current host-assigned VM identity; the guest VirtIO
driver admits that shape only for ingress queries, and bhyve stamps the
attested VMID and epoch before asking the host. The response must match the
query ID, token, program, source and the journal's SHA-256 digest. A host
accepted response durably advances and retires the local pending marker.
Absent and host-pending responses remain fenced, and a temporarily
unavailable host control socket is retried with a fresh query ID. The
sanitized native daemon build and all 24 component suites pass, as do all
27 native port suites and the scoped virtio_dtrace.ko module build. These
tests do not run a live VM. Replaying an absent or host-pending token, polling
newly unresolved runtime tokens, and live guest/host crash testing remain
open; the branch is still experimental.

2026-10-14 attested cross-session host ingest and pending-token replay:
An attested VM may reconnect to the host daemon under a new forwarder
session without changing its VMID and epoch. Host ingest status and exact
ELF retry now find the original scope-3 token across that session change;
the host still rejects a changed program, source or payload digest. A
recovered guest scope-2 token with a matching host-pending status can queue
the original bare ELF using the same durable token. The replay worker
rechecks the local spool digest and pending journal before socket I/O, and
only a host acceptance ACK advances and retires the guest marker. Revoked or
missing spools remain fenced. A new send cannot mint a different token for
the same program/source while a scope-2 journal record remains unresolved.
Host-absent remains fenced: current scope-2 HTD4 records do not bind the
original VM epoch, so an absent response after restore cannot prove that
replaying is safe. The host-ingress, recovery-query and outbound-replay
sanitized regressions cover cross-session acceptance, pending query-driven
queueing, exact-token ACK retirement, changed spool rejection and duplicate
token admission. Live VM validation, epoch-bound absent replay and runtime
reconciliation remain open.
The complete sanitized native daemon build and 25 component suites passed
after the journal lock fix; the subsequent listener allocation guard was
compiled and the recovery-query suite passed separately. No live VM ran.

2026-10-14 VM-incarnation binding for guest outbound recovery:
The guest daemon can now request the current VMID and epoch through
dttransport, guest VirtIO and bhyve's node-ID exchange. A nonzero query ID
binds the reply to its requester, and repeated identity queries do not
reset an already-ready bhyve device. Fresh scope-2 HTD4 pending records
persist that identity before sending an ELF. Replayed records query again
and refuse to send if the VM incarnation changed. Older scope-2 records
with zero VMID/epoch remain readable: host-pending can still prove a safe
exact retry, while host-absent stays fenced. For a new bound record, a
host-absent answer from the same VM incarnation can queue an exact retry;
an epoch mismatch leaves the record pending. Host ingest also refuses an
orphan spool artifact with no matching transaction instead of treating it
as a new publish. The native bhyve build, scoped virtio_dtrace.ko build,
27 native port suites and 25 sanitized daemon suites pass. The bhyve build
used worktree libvmmapi, lib9p and libucl artifacts under /usr/obj.
No VM was run on this host. VM snapshot restore remains unsupported by the
VirtIO DTrace device, and an epoch change between the identity query and
ELF forwarding can still leave a pending record fenced rather than
automatically complete it. A wire-level epoch check and live crash/restore
qualification remain open.

2026-10-14 guest ELF wire-level VM-incarnation check:
Guest dtraced now stamps the queried VMID and epoch in the ELF request,
and dttransport carries both on every VirtIO ELF fragment. The guest driver
rejects missing identity, while bhyve compares every fragment with the
current VMM identity and rechecks the completed ELF immediately before
host-daemon delivery. A changed identity resets the device and discards any
partial ELF; the durable guest pending token remains available for status
reconciliation. The daemon socket protocol is version 16, so mixed-version
daemon processes must be restarted together. The full bhyve build, scoped
virtio_dtrace.ko build, 27 native port suites, and 25 sanitized daemon
suites passed. The regression suite covers a stale first fragment and an
epoch change during assembly. Live VM crash/restore qualification and
runtime reconciliation remain open.

2026-10-14 runtime guest-outbound reconciliation:
The guest dttransport listener now rescans the durable scope-2 journal while
running, so a token left pending after a failed in-process send is eventually
queried without restarting dtraced. It waits beyond the 45-second guest
send/ACK deadline before querying a newly observed token and limits repeat
queries to one per token per 50 seconds. A host-accepted answer retires the
journal; a host-pending or same-incarnation host-absent answer still uses the
exact-token, digest-checked replay path. A reply for a token already retired
by the normal send is validated and ignored without stopping the listener.
The sanitized native daemon build and 26 component suites passed, including
runtime discovery. The updated recovery-query suite separately passed the
settled-before-reply race. No live VM ran here.

2026-10-14 packet-footprint and snapshot audit (no kernel layout change):
The worktree sets MSIZE to 512, while its packet header carries a 32-byte
mbufid_t. On LP64, the existing assertions give a 96-byte packet header and
160-byte external-storage union; with the 32-byte mbuf prefix this exceeds
256 bytes. The host currently reports 256-byte mbuf zones, but that running
kernel is not evidence that this worktree's 512-byte layout is safe or fast.
There are 23 packet-ID accessor call sites across nine kernel files. A
persistent m_tag sidecar could restore the 256-byte mbuf shape while storing
identities only on identified packets, but it changes global allocation and
copy behavior. Required review gates include null-ID readers, allocation
failure paths, copy/move/loopback/epair tag persistence, scatter-gather DMA
pointer lifetime, cross-guest VirtIO/TAP provenance, all supported kernel
architectures, and packet throughput/memory measurements. The automatic
approval reviewer rejected the proposed global layout edit as too broad for
the current authorization, so no sidecar or MSIZE edit was applied.

The snapshot callback still rejects VirtIO DTrace restore. Bhyve defers
hypertrace_start until after the restore transaction, and a newly created VM
gets a new VMM HyperTrace epoch. The saved guest journal remains bound to its
old epoch. Queue-state serialization alone cannot grant safe token replay;
an external-session handoff and explicit old-to-new incarnation reconciliation
must precede enabling device restore.

2026-10-14 arm64 provider-link qualification:
A repeatable arm64-provider-abi.py fixture now compiles a target provider
caller, generates its DOF object with the cross-host DTrace linker, and links
both with the arm64 drti.o using ld.lld. It checks ELF64 little-endian arm64
machine identity, the DOF section, and rejection of mixed amd64/arm64 inputs.
The fixture passed against the arm64 host-tool and target drti.o artifacts in
/usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/arm64. The existing RISC-V ABI fixture
also passed again against its target artifacts. These are object/link checks;
they do not prove provider execution in a live arm64 or RISC-V guest.

2026-10-14 host-to-guest queued-event epoch fence:
The bhyve VirtIO DTrace communicator now checks the VMM's current VMID and
epoch against every identity-bound event in a batch before copying any of the
batch into guest descriptors. An ELF, node-ID reply, or host lifecycle/status
receipt queued under an older incarnation causes NEEDS_RESET and discards the
queue. This closes the window between the host reader's identity check and a
later guest descriptor becoming available. The native bhyve build and all 27
native port suites passed; the device regression changes the VM epoch while an
ELF is queued and verifies that no descriptor is completed. There is still a
small interval between a batch check and guest DMA; live VM restore tests are
needed before claiming a complete incarnation fence.

2026-10-14 guest receive-side VM-incarnation binding:
Bhyve now queues an initial node-ID/epoch event before starting its host
reader, so the guest sees identity before any host ELF or lifecycle receipt.
The guest VirtIO DTrace driver retains that identity and rejects later ELF or
status events stamped for another incarnation. A changed node-ID/epoch in an
already bound driver returns ESTALE and stops the transport, requiring a
fresh device session instead of silently switching active tracing context.
Initial node-ID allocation failures set NEEDS_RESET, and failed device init
releases any queued identity event. The native bhyve build, scoped
virtio_dtrace.ko build, and all 27 native port suites passed; guest-driver
regressions cover stale ELF, status and node-ID epochs. A live VM reset and
restore test is still needed to verify host and guest behavior together.

2026-10-14 KILL control VM-incarnation binding:
Host KILL requests already carried an attested VMID and epoch when bhyve read
them, but the VirtIO KILL control discarded that identity. The control now
carries both values. Bhyve's queued-batch check rejects a KILL assembled for
an older VM incarnation, and the guest driver rejects a KILL whose identity
does not match its current node-ID binding. A guest-to-host KILL control is
still rejected by bhyve. The bhyve build, scoped virtio_dtrace.ko build and
all 27 native port suites passed, including stale queued and received KILL
regressions. This private VirtIO wire change requires matched host and guest
components; live delivery and restore remain unverified.

2026-10-14 CLEANUP control VM-incarnation binding:
Dtraced's cleanup frame remains global, but bhyve now stamps the VirtIO
cleanup control with the receiving VM's current VMID and epoch. Its queued
batch check discards a cleanup assembled before a VM-incarnation change, and
the guest driver rejects a cleanup that does not match its bound identity.
All 27 native port suites passed, including stale queued and received cleanup
regressions. The scoped virtio_dtrace.ko and bhyve builds also passed. The
private VirtIO wire change requires matched host and guest components; live
delivery and restore remain unverified.

2026-10-14 queued-control identity rule:
Bhyve's pre-delivery epoch check now explicitly allows only READY and EOF as
identity-free controls. Unknown or newly added queued controls fail closed
until they have an identity rule, avoiding another silent incarnation bypass.
The bhyve build and all 27 native port suites passed.

2026-10-14 VirtIO-net packet-tag reset failure:
If TAP refuses to disable packet-ID tagging during a device reset, bhyve now
records a VirtIO reset failure instead of silently clearing only its local
packet-ID mode. The common reset path keeps status zero during the reset and
exposes NEEDS_RESET when the guest begins reinitialization. Backend receive
remains disabled until feature negotiation; that path also rejects a still
tagged backend. The bhyve build and packet-provenance regression passed.

2026-10-14 TAP tagged-format transition:
After successfully disabling TAP packet-ID tagging, the backend now discards
any packet already peeked into its bounce buffer under the old wire format.
If the disable ioctl fails, it retains both the tagged state and buffered
packet. The packet-provenance regression exercises both outcomes, and the
bhyve build passed.

2026-10-14 arm64 and RISC-V VirtIO DTrace module coverage:
The VirtIO module list previously gated dtrace together with the amd64-only
PMEM frontend. It now builds virtio_dtrace for amd64, aarch64 and RISC-V,
while PMEM stays amd64-only. Fresh 16-job GENERIC buildkernel runs for arm64
and RISC-V produced architecture-correct virtio_dtrace.ko artifacts alongside
dttransport.ko and dtrace.ko. This qualifies compilation and linking, not
runtime attachment or delivery in a guest.
The module list now follows MK_DTRACE; a WITHOUT_DTRACE build omits the
frontend rather than packaging a module whose DTrace dependency is absent.

2026-10-14 shared VirtIO DTrace control layout:
Bhyve and the guest driver now use one private wire descriptor definition in
sys/dev/virtio/dtrace/virtio_dtrace_wire.h. Local host and guest field aliases
wrap that descriptor without changing its size; compile-time assertions guard
the wrappers. This removes independent payload layouts that could diverge as
the ELF, KILL, cleanup and lifecycle receipts evolve. The amd64 bhyve and
guest-module builds, all 27 native port suites, and 16-job arm64 and RISC-V
GENERIC builds (including virtio_dtrace.ko) passed. Runtime host/guest
compatibility still needs a matched live VM test.

2026-10-14 required VirtIO DTrace control-wire feature:
The host now offers VTDTR_WIRE_F_CONTROL_V1 and refuses to make the device
ready when a guest did not negotiate it. The guest requests that bit, fails
attach when an older host lacks it, and finalizes modern VirtIO features
before allocating queues. This fences mixed binaries that would otherwise
ignore newly added VMID/epoch fields while retaining the old descriptor size.
Native tests cover both missing-bit paths, finalization failure and success;
all 27 native port suites pass. The amd64 bhyve and guest-module builds,
16-job arm64 and RISC-V world builds, and 16-job arm64 and RISC-V GENERIC
builds passed. No live host/guest negotiation was exercised.

2026-10-14 daemon-to-bhyve cleanup lifetime binding:
The daemon now checks the forwarder's live VM attestation before sending a
queued CLEANUP request and places the attested VMID and epoch in its header.
Bhyve accepts the request only if that identity still matches its VMM. This
closes the gap where a request queued for an old incarnation could be stamped
with a newer incarnation's identity on receipt. The daemon protocol version
is 17, so mixed daemon/bhyve binaries fail their hello exchange. The private
VirtIO control descriptor is unchanged. The stale-attestation and stale-epoch
native regressions pass; all 27 native port and 26 native daemon suites pass,
and bhyve and dtraced build with the new header. Live VM save/restore remains
unverified.

2026-10-14 outbound attestation and administrative cleanup:
The daemon now rechecks a host forwarder's VM attestation before sending a
queued ELF and checks both attestation and target VMID/epoch before sending a
queued KILL. This retires stale jobs at the daemon boundary while bhyve still
independently checks the received target. A stale-forwarder regression covers
both send paths.
`dtracedctl` now uses the consumer role accepted by the daemon handshake;
its former forwarder role lacked a session ID and could not connect. The
daemon obtains local peer credentials for consumers and requires a live root
consumer to request VM cleanup. The client handles a nonblocking connect in
progress and its manual documents the credential requirement. Native tests
cover the real client hello and denial of nonroot and wrong-role cleanup.
All 27 native port and 26 sanitized native daemon suites pass after these
changes; incremental dtraced and dtracedctl builds pass. The source VM
snapshot remains unsupported until an authenticated session handoff and guest
journal reconciliation are implemented.

2026-10-14 control-client read dispatch correction:
The consumer-role dtracedctl handshake must retain DTD_SUB_READDATA: the
daemon's read dispatcher ignores frames from clients without that
subscription. The client now advertises it, the hello test verifies the bit,
and a daemon component test invokes the real read dispatcher and confirms it
queues a READ_DATA job for an administrative consumer. The focused dispatcher
test, all 27 native port suites, and the incremental dtracedctl build pass.

2026-10-14 stale KILL frame failure signal:
Bhyve previously discarded an epoch-mismatched daemon KILL frame without
marking the control batch failed. The host daemon had already sent its durable
revocation, but no guest control or device reset followed, so replay could
wait indefinitely for an unrelated reconnect. The mismatch now marks the
batch failed and sets VirtIO NEEDS_RESET through the existing publication
path. The queued-control test already covers stale KILL delivery suppression;
all 27 native port suites and the incremental bhyve build pass. Durable
guest-applied revocation acknowledgment remains open.
The bhyve device regression now injects a stale daemon KILL frame into the
reader and waits for NEEDS_RESET, so the test exercises the assembled-frame
failure path rather than only the queued-control epoch check. All 27 native
port suites pass with that regression.

2026-10-14 guest revocation recovery journal:
The guest VirtIO driver now carries KILL VMID and epoch into dttransport. The
guest daemon validates the lifetime and fsyncs an exact durable kill record
before its source tombstone and in-memory termination queue. A recovered kill
record itself fences source relaunch; daemon startup recreates a missing
tombstone and in-memory revocation after a crash between those syncs. The
fault test exercises that window and all 26 sanitized daemon suites pass.
All 27 native port suites pass, and amd64, arm64, and RISC-V VirtIO DTrace
modules compile. This journal is the prerequisite for a durable guest-applied
ACK; no ACK or guest journal retirement is implemented yet.

2026-10-14 durable guest KILL observation:
Protocol 18 and a required VirtIO feature add an exact KILL_APPLIED control
round trip. The guest daemon reports a KILL only after the kill journal and
source tombstone have been synchronized and the in-memory termination request
has been queued. Bhyve binds the report to its current VMID and epoch, and
the host control daemon accepts it only from the attested forwarder after
checking the exact durable host KILL record. It fsyncs a `.killack` marker
before bhyve returns HOST_KILL_APPLIED to the guest. The guest retries pending
observations on restart and while running; a receipt only clears its volatile
retry list. Its durable `.kill` record remains as a recovery fence. The
16,384-record guest journal cap therefore remains a lifetime capacity limit.
Live VM execution, snapshot session handoff and journal reconciliation remain
unverified or unsupported; this control round trip does not enable snapshot
callbacks.
The incremental dtraced and bhyve builds, amd64 VirtIO DTrace module build,
27 native port suites and 26 sanitized native daemon suites pass. The native
guest driver test exercises ACK descriptors and stale-epoch rejection.

2026-10-14 guest ACK restart reconciliation:
After HOST_KILL_APPLIED, guest dtraced now fsyncs its own `.killack` marker
before removing the KILL from its volatile retry list. On restart it first
restores every source tombstone and in-memory revocation, then retries only
records without a durable local ACK marker. The original `.kill` recovery
record remains intact, including for confirmed kills. A new socket-backed
restart test checks the observation, host receipt, guest marker, retained
revocation, suppressed confirmed resend and replay of an unconfirmed KILL. The
incremental dtraced build and all 27
sanitized native daemon component suites pass. This improves restart behavior
but does not resolve the 16,384-record guest journal capacity or VM restore.
An exact host replay of a confirmed KILL still runs the guest revocation and
child-source check, but it no longer reopens a guest ACK retry. The same
socket-backed test covers that duplicate and all 27 sanitized daemon suites
pass after this change.

2026-10-14 guest VirtIO DTrace attach lock correction:
The attach path called vtdtr_enable_interrupts() with vtdtr_mtx held, while
the helper took that same nonrecursive mutex again. The helper now requires
the caller's lock and schedules the RX task when enabling interrupts reports
an already pending control. The native driver fixture calls it under a real
nonrecursive mutex with a pending RX completion. The amd64 VirtIO DTrace
module build and all 27 native port suites pass. Live attach remains
unverified because this host has no /dev/vmm or /dev/dttransport.

2026-10-14 guest ACK identity publication fence:
The RX task publishes NODE_ID and epoch under the same control mutex used by
dttransport KILL_APPLIED admission and queue insertion. This removes a race
between publishing the identity pair and checking an ACK written by guest
dtraced. A valid ACK before NODE_ID is rejected and freed; the same ACK after
NODE_ID is admitted. The native driver fixture exercises both cases, the
amd64 VirtIO DTrace module builds, and all 27 native port suites pass. Live
host/guest execution is still unavailable here.

2026-10-14 retryable KILL receipt transport loss:
Bhyve no longer puts VirtIO DTrace into NEEDS_RESET when a guest
KILL_APPLIED observation loses the host daemon socket. It withholds the
HOST_KILL_APPLIED receipt so the guest's durable pending record retries after
reconnect. A host protocol rejection still resets the device; queue pressure
or receipt-allocation failure likewise withholds the receipt without claiming
that it reached the guest. The bhyve device regression injects EPIPE, then a
successful retry, then EPROTO and checks the exact host message and returned
receipt. It also checks that a full receipt queue withholds the response
without resetting the device. The incremental bhyve build and all 27 native
port suites pass.
Live daemon disconnect/reconnect behavior remains unverified on this host.

2026-10-14 packet provenance wire validation:
The private TAP and VirtIO-net packet-ID format now accepts only the all-zero
early-boot sentinel or a canonical 32-byte ID with nonzero origin and message,
the expected magic, and zero padding. TAP ingress, guest RX, and both bhyve
network directions reject malformed nonzero IDs instead of forwarding the
packet without reliable lineage. Guest TX regenerates a noncanonical local ID
before writing the private header. The focused sanitized packet-provenance
test covers zero, missing origin, bad magic and bad padding; all 27 native
port suites, incremental bhyve build, and 16-job amd64 GENERIC buildkernel
pass. This validates the wire contract without claiming authentication of a
peer's asserted ID. Packet-provenance mbuf overhead, snapshot session
handoff, and live host/guest execution remain open.

2026-10-14 packet-ID device-harness coverage and error probes:
The bhyve VirtIO-net harness previously filled the private ID with arbitrary
bytes, so the canonical wire checks correctly rejected its positive case.
It now uses a canonical ID and checks that malformed guest and backend IDs
are dropped without sending to TAP or completing a receive chain. All 63
VirtIO-net device cases pass. Bhyve emits VirtIO error probes for both malformed
directions, and the guest vtnet driver emits an SDT invalid-packet-ID probe
before dropping a malformed receive. The incremental bhyve build and 16-job
amd64 GENERIC buildkernel pass. The VirtIO DTrace snapshot gate is unchanged:
the restored VMM epoch differs from the saved guest journal epoch, and the
guest driver currently rejects a second NODE_ID with a different epoch.
An explicit daemon/device handoff remains necessary before enabling restore.

2026-10-14 tagged VirtIO-net checkpoint codec:
VirtIO-net private packet-ID mode now has a version-3 checkpoint record with
an explicit mode byte; version-2 plain-network images remain readable. Restore
validates modern transport, negotiated control and version features, a
tag-capable backend with no conflicting offloads, the derived header lengths,
and any staged packet ID before accepting the image. Restore changes TAP
tagging only when the saved mode differs from the destination mode; an
already-tagged destination keeps its peeked backend record. Failure
compensates the backend and restores the old in-memory
state; a failed compensation marks the device restore-incomplete. Saving a
malformed staged ID is rejected. The device harness covers tagged save,
side-effect-free validation, fresh and repeated tagged restore without a
backend mode toggle, tagged-to-plain
restore, version-2 compatibility, malformed state rejection, and failed tag
reactivation. All 64 VirtIO-net device cases and the incremental bhyve build
pass. This removes the network-device snapshot gate, but the VirtIO DTrace
device still rejects VM restore until its external session and guest journal
can be reconciled under the destination epoch. No live VM snapshot was run.

2026-10-14 VM identity query deadline and restore boundary:
The guest daemon's VM identity query now spends one fixed 10-second budget
across device-write retries and the reply wait. A sanitized runtime regression
fills its nonblocking device socket for five seconds, then verifies that the
query was written and the unanswered reply still times out within the original
budget before the existing durable outbound-reconciliation case runs. The
focused regression and the preceding full 27-suite sanitized daemon run pass.
The current snapshot gate is still required:
the saved guest driver and durable journal carry the source VMID/epoch, while
the destination VMM allocates a new identity. The bhyve queue image alone
does not transfer authority to the destination daemon, retire the source
session, or reconcile outstanding install, ELF, and revocation receipts.
Enabling restore requires a one-use authenticated session handoff bound to
the checkpoint and both VM identities, a guest identity transition explicitly
authorized by that handoff, and durable journal reconciliation before guest
tracing resumes. The gate must remain until those steps and live crash/restore
qualification are implemented.

2026-10-14 packet-ID scatter/gather correctness:
The packet-ID scatter/gather helper now reads the packet header from the
original head mbuf even when its payload is empty and later mbufs contain
data. It also rolls the entire list back if payload mapping fails after the
ID segment was added, so callers never receive a partial ID-only mapping.
A source-extracted ASan/UBSan regression covers both cases and ID append
failure; the 16-job amd64 GENERIC build passes. This preserves provenance
through an empty-head chain but does not resolve the 512-byte mbuf footprint
or prove cross-guest throughput.

2026-10-14 bhyve VirtIO DTrace shutdown admission:
Device shutdown now closes guest-to-host control admission before retiring
the host session. A late READY, ELF or receipt can no longer change device
state or call dtraced after `vsd_stopping` is set; queue notifications also
leave descriptors untouched. The native device regression exercises a READY
control and RX kick after worker shutdown and checks that no control was
accepted or forwarded. The incremental bhyve build and all 27 sanitized
native port suites pass. Live host/guest shutdown remains unverified.
The device reset callback also refuses a late guest status-zero reset after
shutdown, retaining reset failure rather than touching the retired transport.
The native fixture checks that the host reset generation does not advance;
the bhyve build and all 27 sanitized native port suites pass again.

2026-10-14 restore transaction dependency audit:
`RESTORE_PROTOCOL.5BSD.md` records the source daemon fence, durable one-use
ticket, destination attestation and claim, restricted guest rebind, journal
reconciliation, and failure ordering required before the VirtIO DTrace
snapshot gate can be removed. The current checkpoint transaction releases
its guest CPU startup fence immediately after device resume; that point must
move behind an authorized host handoff. The guest journal reconciliation
itself runs only after vCPUs are released, while the tracing device remains
fenced. Existing version-4 unbound pending outbound records require an exact
host-pending digest response; host absence is not replay authority. The
snapshot portability structural gate passes. A new sanitized recovery-query
case proves host absence leaves an unbound record pending without queueing
replay, while an exact host-pending response still queues it. No restore
protocol code or live VM execution was qualified by this audit.

2026-10-14 durable restore handoff ledger primitive:
The host daemon directory now persists fixed-width, SHA-256-checked handoff
records in prepared, committed, reserved, mutating and claimed phases. It
binds a random ticket to the source session/VM identity, checkpoint digest
and one destination VM identity. It fsyncs each phase, recovers predecessor
records left by a crash, rejects conflicting claims and malformed records, and
supports an abort while still prepared. Reservation may be cancelled before
the durable mutating phase; after that phase a restart cannot silently
release its destination lease. The sanitized handoff-ledger suite checks
phase transitions, idempotence, restart recovery, conflicting source and
destination identities, and corruption refusal. The incremental dtraced
build, all 28 sanitized native daemon suites, and the focused final test
with conflicting reservation digests and mutation destinations pass. This
is an internal primitive only: checkpoint publication, source ingress/egress
fencing, live
VMM destination attestation, destination reservation/claim orchestration,
guest rebind and journal reconciliation are not wired to it. VirtIO DTrace
VM restore remains gated by EOPNOTSUPP.

2026-10-14 guest string address arithmetic:
The DIF `strstr`, `index` and `rindex` arms now iterate over integer guest
virtual addresses instead of doing host C pointer arithmetic, including the
one-before-start reverse-search sentinel. The shared string length and
comparison helpers also handle guest address zero as an address rather than
a null host string and reject address wrap before another load. A source-
extracted guest builtin regression covers a zero-address string and needle.
The runner now makes ASan/UBSan findings fatal, correcting its prior false
PASS on a UBSan diagnostic. The final sanitized regression and 16-job GENERIC
kernel/module builds pass on amd64, arm64/aarch64 and riscv/riscv64; each
build produced its architecture's dtrace.ko. This does not establish live
guest provider execution or change the restore gate.

2026-10-14 cross-process handoff ledger serialization:
Each durable handoff operation now holds an advisory lock on the daemon's
private `.handoff.lock` file as well as its in-process mutex. This prevents
overlapping old and new daemon processes from independently advancing or
cancelling the same ticket during restart. The lock file is opened without
following symlinks and must be regular. A sanitized forked regression holds
the lock in one process, confirms the other cannot query the ledger until
release, and checks symlink refusal. The daemon build and focused regression
pass. The corrected final run also passes all 28 sanitized native daemon
suites. Checkpoint source fencing and protocol integration remain open.

2026-10-14 destination handoff lease exclusion:
Reservation now scans the durable handoff ledger under its cross-process lock
and refuses a second ticket for the same destination VM ID and epoch while
the first is reserved, mutating, or claimed. Malformed handoff filenames or
records fail closed. The regression covers a conflicting ticket before and
after claim, and permits that ticket to reserve a different destination.
The native dtraced build and all 28 sanitized native daemon suites pass.
The handoff remains an internal primitive; source fencing, checkpoint
publication, guest journal remapping, and live VM validation remain open.

2026-10-14 handoff crash-state validation:
The ledger now accepts at most two adjacent phase records for one ticket.
Each transition fsyncs its new phase before removing the predecessor, so a
non-adjacent pair or three records cannot be a legitimate crash remainder.
The sanitized regression inserts a valid reserved record beside a claimed
record and verifies that recovery fails closed with EPROTO. The full 28-case
sanitized daemon suite passes. This strengthens the dormant handoff ledger;
runtime checkpoint and restore orchestration remain unwired.

2026-10-14 attested source handoff control:
Daemon protocol 19 adds a fixed 49-byte HANDOFF request for source prepare,
commit and abort. The host daemon derives the source session, VM ID and epoch
from the live VMM-attested forwarder; the request carries only an operation,
random ticket and checkpoint digest. A different VM/session cannot advance
the ticket, zero digests cannot commit, and a committed handoff cannot be
aborted. The new sanitized control regression covers those decisions and
durable phase recovery. All 29 sanitized daemon suites and the native
dtraced and bhyve builds pass; the bhyve build used worktree lib9p,
libvmmapi and libucl artifacts under /usr/obj. Bhyve does not yet send the
new request at checkpoint publication, and destination reservation, mutation,
claim, source fencing, guest rebind and journal reconciliation are not wired.
VirtIO DTrace restore remains gated by EOPNOTSUPP.

2026-10-14 bhyve source handoff sender:
Bhyve now has a source prepare/commit/abort helper that sends the fixed
protocol-19 request through its existing acknowledged, mutex-owned forwarder
channel. It rejects a zero ticket, a missing commit digest, and a digest on
prepare or abort before sending. The socket fixture checks exact framing and
the daemon ACK for prepare and commit; all 27 native port suites and the
incremental bhyve build pass. The helper is not yet called from the
checkpoint transaction: a durable checkpoint ticket/digest publication
contract and source event fence are required before that call is safe.

2026-10-14 checkpoint handoff identity contract:
The existing version-3 checkpoint manifest already carries a random 128-bit
generation suffix in all three member names. One helper decodes that
generation as the handoff ticket before capture; another hashes the canonical
published manifest bytes as the checkpoint digest, without adding an on-disk
field. Publication
and handoff share one serializer so the computed digest matches the actual
manifest file. The manifest harness checks the pre-publication ticket, disk
hash, a parsed reload,
and digest sensitivity to a member hash; all 37 rootless snapshot-model cases
and the incremental bhyve build pass. The checkpoint transaction does not
yet call the source handoff sender or fence daemon traffic, and restore
remains gated.

2026-10-14 attested destination handoff reservation:
Protocol-19 HANDOFF now accepts destination reserve and cancel requests with
the ticket and exact manifest digest. The host daemon reads the committed
source record, derives the destination VM ID and epoch from the requester's
live VMM attestation, and persists the lease under the cross-process ledger
lock. A rival destination cannot take or cancel an active lease; the original
destination can retry either operation, and cancellation returns the ticket
to committed state before mutation. Bhyve has an acknowledged destination
sender for both operations. The sanitized daemon regression covers source,
destination and rival identities; the bhyve socket fixture checks framing.
All 29 sanitized daemon suites, 27 native port suites, and the native
dtraced/bhyve builds pass. Destination mutation/claim, source fencing, checkpoint
publication and guest rebind are still unwired; restore remains gated.

2026-10-14 attested destination mutation fence:
Protocol 19 now accepts a destination MUTATE request after a durable
reservation. The control daemon verifies the live VMM-attested destination
VMID and epoch, checkpoint digest, and ticket ownership before advancing the
ledger to its durable mutating phase. The same destination may retry MUTATE;
cancel and rival reservation fail after mutation begins. Bhyve can send this
operation over either its normal channel or the short-lived prestart control
connection. The native handoff-control and socket fixtures cover these paths;
all 29 sanitized native daemon suites, 27 native port suites, and the
snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. Restore does not yet invoke the handoff
operations, and the VirtIO DTrace restore gate remains in place.

2026-10-14 attested destination claim control:
The destination can now advance a mutating ticket to the durable claimed
phase using the same VMM-attested control channel. The daemon admits CLAIM
only after mutation for the reserved destination VM incarnation and verified
checkpoint digest; a repeated claim is idempotent. Source and rival callers
cannot claim the ticket, and reservation or cancellation cannot follow claim.
The daemon handoff fixture covers phase ordering and ownership; the bhyve
socket fixtures cover normal and prestart framing. All 29 sanitized native
daemon suites, 27 native port suites, and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build
pass. The restore path must claim only after daemon and guest state reconcile;
it does not yet call CLAIM or release the VirtIO DTrace restore gate.

VirtIO DTrace state-size audit: the pending-control limit is 32,770
descriptors, each 2,144 bytes on amd64, before queue metadata and partial
ELF data. The current per-device checkpoint buffer is fixed at 40 MiB, so a
codec that copies every descriptor into that buffer would reject otherwise
valid device state. The eventual codec needs bounded variable-length records
and a larger or streaming per-device snapshot allocation, in addition to
daemon receipt reconciliation. This remains an open restore requirement.

2026-10-14 variable device checkpoint capacity:
The save coordinator now accepts an optional per-PCI-device extra-capacity
callback while devices are paused. VirtIO DTrace computes a bounded allowance
from its validated pending-control count and partial guest ELF bytes; an idle
device retains the standard 40 MiB buffer and other PCI devices are unchanged.
The coordinator rejects overflow and requests above 160 MiB of extra space,
and allocates the larger temporary buffer only for that device record. This
removes the fixed-buffer obstacle for a future private-state codec but does
not serialize the queue or lift EOPNOTSUPP. The actual snapshot-enabled bhyve
build, 27 native port suites, and 37 rootless snapshot-model cases pass.

2026-10-14 queued-control checkpoint shape validation:
VirtIO DTrace capture preflight now checks queued controls beyond VM identity:
ELF fragment lengths, total bounds, continuation flags and delivery IDs;
KILL PID and source identity; receipt IDs and boolean flags; and node IDs.
The device harness exercises malformed KILL and ELF entries followed by a
valid entry. All 27 sanitized native port suites and the snapshot-enabled
bhyve build pass. The queue codec and external receipt reconciliation remain
open, so save/restore still returns EOPNOTSUPP.

2026-10-14 VirtIO DTrace private image codec, gated:
A versioned little-endian private image now describes source VM identity,
device readiness/configuration, bounded queued controls and the received
prefix of a partial guest ELF. Controls encode only their defined fields and
used ELF bytes, not host pointers, size_t values or structure padding. A
side-effect-free parser validates source identity and bounds in bhyve's device
payload preflight. The source callback can serialize the private section, but
still returns EOPNOTSUPP rather than publish an image that cannot be restored;
the destination preflight likewise remains gated after parsing. Native tests
cover KILL and ELF round trips, bad versions, invalid event/PID/VMID/length,
and truncated images. All 27 sanitized native port suites, 37 snapshot-model
cases, and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. Daemon receipt
reconciliation, destination epoch rebind and a safe traffic fence remain
necessary before either callback can permit save/restore.

2026-10-14 bounded destination private-state staging:
The private image parser can now optionally copy a validated control array
and only the received prefix of a partial guest ELF into a separate staging
object. Parsing an invalid or truncated image frees its temporary allocations
and leaves the caller's stage empty; repeated staging into a live object is
rejected. Ordinary private-state validation remains allocation-free and rejects
restore with EOPNOTSUPP. The native device harness checks staged KILL and ELF
controls, source identity, partial bytes, cleanup and failure atomicity. All
27 sanitized native port suites and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass.
The stage is not published into the live VirtIO device; each queued receipt
must first be reconciled with the daemon and rebound to the destination VM.

Source-fence ordering audit: host-to-guest ELF jobs hold a forwarder's output
mutex through guest queue ACK, but hold the revocation mutex only while
publishing their pending token. A prepared-ledger check at token publication
alone would still permit an earlier in-flight send to finish after prepare.
Simply rejecting a later job is also insufficient: the dispatcher then
retires its socket and removes the job, so an aborted checkpoint could lose
an unforwarded spool. The source prepare path must drain paired output
channels, persist its fence under the admission lock, and retain or rescan
deferred work on abort; guest-to-host ingress needs the same cut. No source
fence is claimed by the reservation work above.

2026-10-14 prepared-source uniqueness:
The durable ledger can now find a prepared ticket by attested source VM ID and
epoch, and rejects a second simultaneous prepare for that VM incarnation even
if the request arrives from a different forwarding session.
Committed tickets do not prevent a later checkpoint in the same VM lifetime.
The scan is serialized with ledger writers across daemon processes and checks
each ticket's complete crash-state record before using it. All 29 sanitized
native daemon suites pass. This lookup is a prerequisite for the traffic fence;
it does not itself stop an in-flight delivery or wire bhyve checkpoint calls.

2026-10-14 cross-process prepare and device-state audit:
The handoff-ledger regression now races two independent daemon processes with
different forwarding sessions against one VMM incarnation. Exactly one
prepare may succeed; the loser receives EBUSY, and abort releases the slot.
All 29 sanitized native daemon suites pass. The VirtIO DTrace device audit
found two private state groups that must accompany its restored rings: the
pending host-to-guest control queue, including receipts stamped with a VMID
and epoch, and a partially assembled guest-to-host ELF with its delivery ID,
program/source IDs, VMID and epoch. The host connector generation is local
process state and cannot be copied as an authenticated restore credential.
Queued receipt epochs cannot be changed merely by rewriting fields; their
durable daemon transactions must be reconciled under an authorized handoff.
The existing EOPNOTSUPP restore gate remains in place.

2026-10-14 VirtIO DTrace checkpoint preflight:
The device's checkpoint pause now validates its private state while owning
the device mutex. It checks the live VMM identity, the exact queued-control
count, each queued control's VM incarnation, and whether a partially assembled
guest ELF has bounded progress and matching delivery identity. It releases
the lock on failure so the common VirtIO pause rollback can run. The device
harness covers stale queued controls, count mismatch, orphaned metadata,
valid partial ELF and stale or completed partial ELF; all 27 sanitized native
port suites and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. This is a capture
preflight, not a state codec or external-daemon traffic fence. The snapshot
callback still returns EOPNOTSUPP.

2026-10-14 restore manifest handoff identity retention:
The verified checkpoint loader now carries the manifest's random 128-bit
generation ticket and SHA-256 digest in restore_state, with a validity flag
set only after all three generation members and metadata have loaded. State
destruction clears those bytes. This gives the eventual destination reserve
request the identity of the exact image admitted for restore; it does not yet
send that request or release the VirtIO DTrace restore gate. The 37 rootless
snapshot-model cases and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass.

2026-10-14 pre-restore attested handoff control:
Bhyve can now send destination reserve/cancel over a short-lived, VMM-attested
READDATA socket before starting its normal HyperTrace connector. This matches
restore startup order: the ordinary rx/wx channels remain stopped until
device state is safe, while a destination lease must be acquired before any
memory or device mutation. The one-off socket uses the same random bhyve
session and protocol-19 frame as the ordinary sender and closes after the
daemon's ACK. A native socket fixture verifies the attestation descriptor,
subscription, exact frame, accepted reserve/cancel, NAK handling and absence
of a running connector. All 27 sanitized native port suites and the
snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. The restore transaction does not yet call
this helper; source fencing, destination mutation/claim and guest rebind are
still prerequisites.

2026-10-14 ticket-scoped restore receipt inspection:
An attested destination holding an active reserve, mutation, or claim lease
can query the source's durable outbound delivery phase by checkpoint ticket,
digest, delivery ID, program ID and source ID. The daemon derives the source
VM incarnation and session from its checksummed handoff ledger and derives
the destination from current VMM attestation. Wrong destinations, digests,
scopes and reserved bytes fail closed. An absent source receipt is reported
as absent, never as permission to resend; the reply never grants replay.
The sanitized native daemon build and all 29 component suites pass, including
the new handoff-control coverage. Bhyve does not yet call this query, and
source fencing, destination reissue, queue reconciliation and live restore
qualification remain open. The VirtIO DTrace restore gate stays enabled.

2026-10-14 bhyve pre-restore receipt query transport:
Bhyve can now send the ticket-scoped source receipt lookup on a short-lived,
VMM-attested READDATA connection before starting its normal connector. It
validates the exact delivery ID, phase, reserved fields, and zero replay and
digest, and clears the result on a NAK or malformed response. The socket
fixture covers accepted, NAK and wrong-ID replies. All 27 sanitized native
port suites and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. The restore transaction
does not yet use this helper or reconcile the staged device queue; the
EOPNOTSUPP save/restore gate remains in place.

2026-10-14 handoff NAK channel preservation:
The running bhyve handoff sender now serializes its own complete frame and
ACK under the write-channel lock. A daemon NAK means it consumed the whole
request, so the sender returns EPROTO while keeping that channel and its
queued device work alive; only I/O failure or an invalid ACK retires it.
The socket fixture rejects one COMMIT, verifies the same descriptor remains
configured, then accepts a second COMMIT and subsequent destination requests.
All 27 sanitized native port suites and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build
pass. This removes the channel-retirement hazard identified in the earlier
source-fence review, but it does not itself fence source traffic or enable
save/restore.

2026-10-14 source-incarnation ticket exclusivity:
The durable handoff scan now treats prepared, committed, reserved, mutating,
and claimed records as owning their source VM incarnation. Previously it
ignored every phase after prepared, allowing the same incarnation to prepare
a second ticket once the first checkpoint was committed. The control test now
rejects a second ticket after claim and accepts it only after a new attested
VMM epoch; the ledger test checks committed ownership. The sanitized native
daemon build and all 29 component suites pass. This closes a ticket
duplication path, but source traffic fencing and VM restore remain open.

2026-10-14 module quiesce ownership check:
The HyperTrace kernel module now refuses MOD_QUIESCE while virtual probes or
a VMM hook still own its provider state. FreeBSD treats EOPNOTSUPP from
MOD_QUIESCE as success, so the previous default branch could let the linker
continue an unload walk even though MOD_UNLOAD later rejected it. The
amd64 hypertrace.ko build passes with kernel warnings as errors. Incremental
16-job GENERIC buildkernel runs also pass for arm64/aarch64 and riscv/riscv64;
their refreshed hypertrace.ko files have AArch64 and RISC-V ELF machine IDs.
Live module load/unload and non-amd64 provider execution remain unverified.

2026-10-14 absent packet-ID handling and mbuf layout review:
Packet-ID validity helpers now treat a missing ID as absent. TAP emits the
all-zero wire ID for a packet with no ID, and the scatter/gather helper uses
a stable all-zero ID for that case. The native provenance fixture covers the
absent-ID result. Fresh 16-job GENERIC buildkernel runs pass on amd64,
arm64/aarch64, and riscv/riscv64, and the source-extracted provenance test
passes. The existing inline ID and MSIZE=512 remain unchanged.

Automatic approval review rejected a proposed global 256-byte mbuf layout
edit because it removed the inline ID without a complete persistent-tag
sidecar and all caller updates, risking system-wide network corruption. No
part of that edit landed. The remaining design must cover packet-header
move/copy and tag persistence, optional-ID readers, allocation failures,
VirtIO/TAP wire identity, DMA pointer lifetime, cross-guest traffic, and
throughput/memory measurements before a layout change can be accepted.

2026-10-14 packet-ID writer allocation guard and measured layout:
The BPF feedback path and both socket datagram send paths now reject a
missing `m_mbufid_ensure()` result with `ENOBUFS` before generating an ID.
The existing inline accessor always succeeds; this closes the remaining
writer failure paths needed by a future lazy sidecar. An isolated LP64 header
compile measured a 288-byte `struct mbuf` with `MSIZE=512` today and a
256-byte structure with `MSIZE=256` after removing the inline 32-byte ID.
The candidate is structurally viable but not integrated or runtime tested.
An i386 FreeBSD 16 cross-target check also passes: the current structure is
284 bytes inside `MSIZE=512`, while the isolated candidate is 252 bytes inside
`MSIZE=256`. This is layout evidence, not a 32-bit network run.
The native packet-provenance fixture and a 16-job amd64 GENERIC buildkernel
pass; the latter compiled the edited BPF and socket objects with `-Werror`.
The rejected global layout change remains unapplied.

2026-10-14 read-only VirtIO DTrace checkpoint receipt query:
The bhyve device now offers a private-image query that parses and stages a
bounded saved queue, then asks the attested host daemon for exact source ELF
delivery, guest-ELF host receipt, and applied KILL records under the reserved
checkpoint ticket. It queries one ELF fragment run once, checks identity,
phase, revocation and payload-digest presence, and rejects unsupported saved
control classes or an unfinished guest-to-host ELF. It does not bind receipts
or publish device state. The native device fixture covers matching, pending,
revoked, wrong-ID, timeout, malformed sequence and trailing-image cases,
including a serialized `HOST_ELF` receipt query. All 29 native port suites
and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. The restore coordinator does not
invoke this helper yet;
the VirtIO DTrace snapshot gate remains EOPNOTSUPP.

2026-10-14 exact saved-delivery bind primitive:
The bhyve device now has a separate private-image bind helper for the
`MUTATING` ticket phase. It stages and queries the whole image before
durably binding each queued ELF delivery and guest-ELF host receipt. A
partial bind is recoverable by an exact idempotent retry; the native fixture
injects a timeout after the first of two binds and checks the retry. All 29
native port suites, all 32 sanitized daemon suites, and the snapshot-enabled
bhyve build pass. The helper is
not wired into restore and does not publish the queue or lift its gate.

2026-10-14 ticket-scoped source KILL lookup:
The daemon protocol now has a read-only KILL query for an attested destination
holding an active checkpoint lease. It binds the PID and source-program ID to
the source VM incarnation in the durable ticket, checks the exact journal
record and applied marker, and reports absent, pending, or applied. The bhyve
prestart helper validates the fixed response and clears it on NAK or malformed
data. The reply has replay=0 in every case; a KILL applied to the source
incarnation cannot by itself decide whether a restored guest must receive it.
The control and socket fixtures cover lease, rival VM, wrong digest, malformed
request, pending and applied cases. Full checkpoint-cut reconciliation,
source traffic fencing, destination reissue, and snapshot codec are still
required before restore can be enabled.

The KILL lookup now treats a missing KILL with an orphan applied marker as
corrupt journal state. Its revoked flag describes the source ID across the
source VM lifetime even when the queried PID is absent; the phase remains an
exact PID lookup. The daemon and bhyve fixtures cover both cases. The next
checkpoint work must serialize source admission and in-flight ELF/KILL sends
with PREPARE before source pause; merely recording a ticket does not fence the
reader or prevent a control queued after the snapshot cut.
The corrected protocol-20 implementation passes all 29 sanitized daemon
component suites, all 27 native port suites, the snapshot-enabled bhyve
build, and git diff --check. These checks do not exercise a live VM.

2026-10-14 source handoff admission and deferred worker fence:
PREPARE now rejects an incarnation with an unresolved host-to-guest ELF
receipt or unapplied KILL. A newly admitted host ELF receipt serializes with
the durable handoff lock and fails while that source incarnation has a ticket.
Host ELF/KILL workers retain jobs deferred by the ticket rather than retiring
the socket; ABORT wakes matching attested source writers, including the case
where ABORT races a worker requeue. A sanitized worker fixture checks that a
KILL remains queued during PREPARE and is emitted after ABORT. A fresh native
daemon build and all 29 component suites pass, as does the updated handoff
worker fixture under ASan/UBSan. The source checkpoint transaction still does
not invoke PREPARE, so this fence is not yet an end-to-end snapshot guarantee.
The receipt admission lock must remain held until the new durable record is
flushed; a follow-up review caught and fixed an early release between the
ticket check and receipt publication. New KILL publication also holds the
cross-process handoff lock through its directory flush, so PREPARE cannot miss
a KILL that precedes it. The sanitized build and 26 unaffected daemon suites
passed after that change; the three fixtures affected by the new persistent
lock file were repaired, rebuilt against the same daemon objects and passed
individually. `git diff --check` passes. There is still no live VM evidence.

2026-10-14 device reader checkpoint drain:
The VirtIO DTrace checkpoint pause now closes reader admission, waits up to
12 seconds for the current daemon frame and its ACK to finish, and leaves the
reader parked until resume. Timeout or invalid staged state rolls the pause
flag back and fails the checkpoint. This closes a device-side race where a
frame could be read after the queue image was captured. The native device
fixture checks reader-drain synchronization and pause ownership; all 27
sanitized native port suites and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass.
The daemon source ticket is not yet invoked from vm_checkpoint(), and the
VirtIO DTrace snapshot callback still returns EOPNOTSUPP: the reader drain
alone does not make daemon state or guest replay portable.
The successful stop-VM path now explicitly marks the VirtIO DTrace device
stopping, releases checkpoint's mutex ownership and joins its workers before
exit invokes the atexit handler. This avoids a same-thread mutex deadlock
once the save gate is lifted. The native fixture checks the drain and stop
handoff; all 27 sanitized port suites and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build
pass after this change. The callback still rejects save/restore until daemon
ticket commit and destination reconciliation are wired into the transaction.

2026-10-14 independent source handoff control channel:
Bhyve can now send source PREPARE, COMMIT and ABORT over a short-lived,
VMM-attested READDATA connection while its normal connector is running. The
sender reuses the fixed handoff frame and forwarder session identity, but a
broken write socket cannot prevent a later ABORT attempt over a fresh
connection. The native socket fixture checks admission while started,
attestation descriptor transfer, exact frame, a reply lost after PREPARE,
and a subsequent ABORT; all 27 sanitized port suites and the snapshot-enabled
bhyve build pass. vm_checkpoint() does not yet call this helper. A failed
fresh connection still leaves an ambiguous remote state and requires a
fail-closed source transaction before snapshots may be enabled.

2026-10-14 attested source ticket status:
Protocol 21 adds a read-only HANDOFF operation for a source to query its exact
durable ticket after a lost PREPARE/ABORT reply. The daemon requires the live
VMM-attested source session, VMID and epoch; an absent ticket returns phase 0,
a prepared ticket returns 'p' with no digest, and committed or destination-
leased phases return their checkpoint digest. Another VM's ticket is denied.
Bhyve uses a fresh control connection, validates the fixed status reply and
zeros it on every failure. The daemon fixture covers absent, prepared,
committed, wrong-owner and post-ABORT states; the socket fixture covers a lost
PREPARE reply, subsequent status/ABORT, malformed status and early failure.
All 29 sanitized daemon suites, all 27 native port suites, the snapshot-enabled
bhyve build and git diff --check pass. The query is recovery evidence, not
permission to replay or a substitute for a fail-closed checkpoint transaction.

2026-10-14 source ticket checkpoint transaction:
Bhyve now prepares the exact generation ticket after pausing vCPUs, aborts it
when no manifest is published, and commits it against the published manifest's
digest before releasing the kernel snapshot session. A lost response is
resolved with the durable ticket-status query. COMMIT and ABORT may retry
once; an uncertain PREPARE is never resent because a late first request
could recreate the ticket after ABORT. An
ambiguous source ticket prevents guest resume and kernel-session release;
an absent status only permits resume after the first request was explicitly
rejected, since a timed-out request could still be processing on another
connection. The socket fixture also covers that delayed-request race.
Even a published manifest followed by a directory fsync error is committed
and leaves the source stopped. Non-stop HyperTrace checkpoints are rejected
until clone semantics have a separate ticket. The socket fixture covers lost
PREPARE, ABORT and COMMIT replies and a confirmed absent ticket; all 27
sanitized native port suites and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass.
The VirtIO DTrace snapshot callback still returns EOPNOTSUPP, so this is a
fail-closed transaction scaffold, not functional HyperTrace save/restore.
Destination queue/journal reconciliation, a portable device-state codec,
and live VM tests remain open.

2026-10-14 attested destination ticket status:
Protocol 22 adds a read-only destination ticket query bound to the manifest
digest. The daemon returns committed state to an attested destination holding
the exact ticket and digest; reserved, mutating and claimed states are visible
only to their VMM-attested destination incarnation. Bhyve validates phase,
digest and reserved fields on a fresh control channel, and has a bounded
transition helper for lost replies. An uncertain RESERVE is not retried,
because a late first request could reserve again after CANCEL. The daemon
fixture covers committed, prepared, reserved, mutating, claimed, wrong digest
and wrong destination; the socket fixture covers valid and malformed replies
and lost RESERVE responses. All 29 sanitized daemon suites, all 27 sanitized
native port suites, the snapshot-enabled bhyve build and git diff --check
pass. Bhyve now records whether an image requires the HyperTrace handoff and
uses the bounded destination transition helper in vm_restore_transaction():
RESERVE follows preflight, MUTATE precedes guest-memory writes, CLAIM follows
kernel-session commit, and a failure before MUTATE attempts CANCEL. Guest
vCPUs remain held through each transition. A failed or late RESERVE may still
strand a reservation; the destination exits stopped rather than trusting an
absent status. The snapshot-enabled bhyve build passes after the wiring.
The VirtIO DTrace EOPNOTSUPP save/restore gate remains in place until the
device queue and daemon journal are reconciled before CLAIM.

2026-10-14 device receipt image validation:
The VirtIO DTrace private-image validator now rejects impossible host receipt
phase, replay, digest, revocation and query-ID combinations. In particular a
queued status cannot claim replay without its durable digest, and a transport
unavailable receipt cannot claim replay or revocation. The staging parser
also scans all variable-length controls before allocating its full queue
array, so a short forged stream cannot force a large staging allocation.
The native device fixture covers malformed receipts and a forged queue count;
all 27 sanitized native port suites, the snapshot-enabled bhyve build and
git diff --check pass. This strengthens the portable image boundary while
the external journal reissue and live restore remain unfinished.

2026-10-14 durable ticket-status recovery:
The daemon now fsyncs the handoff directory under the cross-process ticket
lock before returning a read-only ticket phase. A transition can write and
fsync its record yet report failure when the final directory fsync fails;
without this barrier, a later status query could mistake a merely visible
phase for a durable PREPARE, COMMIT, RESERVE or CANCEL. The serialized status
query now either proves that phase durable or fails closed. The ledger
fixture injects an EIO from the status fsync and verifies that the query
fails, then succeeds after the fault clears. All 29 sanitized native daemon
suites pass. Live disk-error injection and VM save/restore validation remain
open.

2026-10-14 host ELF receipt payload binding:
New host-forwarded ELF receipts use journal version 4 and bind the SHA-256
digest of the bare ELF delivered to the guest. The optional VM-target
envelope is excluded because its destination identity changes at handoff.
The attested destination delivery-status query now returns this digest;
legacy version-3 host receipts remain readable but provide no digest for
future reissue authorization. The daemon fixture checks the journal digest
against the bare payload and checks the destination status reply. All 29
sanitized native daemon suites and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass.
The digest alone does not authorize reissue: the daemon still needs to
verify retained payload bytes, make a durable reissue decision, and
reconcile that decision with the staged VirtIO DTrace queue. The snapshot
callback still returns EOPNOTSUPP and live VM qualification remains open.

2026-10-14 retained host ELF proof for handoff status:
The destination delivery-status query now returns a version-4 payload digest
only after the host daemon reopens the exact named spool file, validates any
source VM envelope, hashes the guest-visible ELF, and matches the durable
receipt. A missing file leaves the digest zero; changed content fails the
query. A revoked source reports revocation and withholds the digest even if
its old payload changed. The file is fsynced and checked against its original
inode and metadata before the reply. The handoff fixture exercises missing,
matching, changed, and revoked payloads; all 29 sanitized native daemon
suites pass. This is
read-only evidence at query time. A future reissue transaction must hold and
recheck the payload while making the durable new-delivery decision.

2026-10-14 handoff receipt evidence sync:
The daemon now fsyncs the shared journal directory under its cross-process
handoff lock before returning present or absent delivery and KILL status to
an attested destination. This prevents a visible phase left by a failed
transition sync from being treated as durable reconciliation evidence. The
ledger fixture injects EIO for both present and absent lookups of each kind
and verifies fail-closed behavior. All 29 sanitized daemon suites pass.
The binding of source receipts to a destination VM incarnation and the
staged VirtIO queue remains open; these status replies grant no replay.

2026-10-14 HTD4 host observation resolution:
Host-to-guest ELF sends now write digest-bound HTD4 receipts. Guest install,
exit, and status messages carry only the receipt owner and delivery key, so
the host daemon now resolves the stored HTD3 or HTD4 record before advancing
its phase. For an HTD4 replay offer, it verifies the retained host ELF and
returns the digest of the bare guest-visible bytes; a missing file grants no
replay. The installed-receipt fixture covers status, install, exit, and a
fresh directory reopen for a targeted HTD4 ELF. All 29 sanitized daemon
suites pass. Destination binding still needs an explicit source-to-destination
receipt link so a source-targeted spool can be authenticated after VMID and
epoch remapping; no cross-VM replay is enabled yet.

2026-10-14 non-reissueable handoff receipt phases:
The daemon now exposes a retained payload digest through the destination
handoff lookup only for accepted or installed, unrevoked HTD4 receipts. A
terminal receipt remains visible but has no replayable digest. Bhyve rejects
a nonzero digest on absent, pending, terminal, or revoked status replies.
The daemon fixture checks a terminal receipt and the port socket fixture
checks a malformed terminal reply. All 29 sanitized daemon suites, all 27
native port suites, and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. These are
preconditions for a destination binding transaction, which is still open.

2026-10-14 destination-bound HTD5 receipt scaffold:
The daemon journal can now encode an HTD5 host receipt containing the
destination owner, handoff ticket, immediate source session, payload digest,
and the VM identity named by the retained ELF envelope. Under the shared
handoff lock, bind_handoff_delivery() accepts only a MUTATING destination
ticket, an unrevoked accepted or installed HTD4/HTD5 source receipt, and
retained payload bytes matching that receipt. It creates or resumes a
destination receipt with the same delivery ID, rejects a competing owner,
and allows an exact retry after CLAIM. The handoff fixture checks binding,
idempotence, owner conflict, payload proof, and reopen from disk; all 29
sanitized native daemon suites pass. This method is not yet exposed through
the attested control protocol or called by bhyve. Recovery must also gate
HTD5 receipts on CLAIM and propagate source revocation before save/restore
can be enabled.

2026-10-14 claimed HTD5 lineage and recovery:
The daemon now withholds an HTD5 destination receipt from recovery and live
status until its handoff ticket is CLAIMED. It validates each earlier claimed
ticket and source receipt in a multi-hop migration, propagates revocation from
any source or destination incarnation, and refuses a new bind if an ancestor
is revoked. The handoff fixture covers pre-CLAIM replay denial, post-CLAIM
install and recovery, two-hop migration, late original-source KILL, and a
rejected third bind. All 29 sanitized native daemon suites pass. The bind is
still an internal journal API: attested daemon protocol support, bhyve
handoff use, durable reissue reconciliation with the staged VirtIO queue,
and live VM save/restore qualification remain open.

2026-10-14 attested HTD5 bind protocol:
Protocol 24 adds HANDOFF_BIND_DELIVERY as a distinct mutating operation.
The daemon takes the destination VM incarnation, session, UID and PID from
the live VMM-attested forwarder and calls the durable HTD5 journal bind only
for an exact MUTATING ticket (or an exact retry after CLAIM). Its reply names
the accepted or installed receipt and payload digest without granting replay
before CLAIM. Bhyve has a prestart client which rejects malformed bind
replies. The handoff fixture exercises a premature bind, a wrong-VM bind,
durable binding, and an exact retry. All 29 sanitized daemon suites, 27
native port suites, and a snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. Bhyve restore
still must invoke the bind for each staged ELF, reconcile staged KILLs and
queue state, and retain the save/restore gate until that work is verified.

2026-10-14 staged ELF queue sequence validation:
VirtIO DTrace checkpoint save and image preflight now reject an impossible
ELF fragment sequence: a truncated queue tail, interleaved controls,
changed delivery identity, or noncanonical fragment lengths. A valid queued
suffix is accepted because earlier fragments may already be in guest memory.
The native device fixture checks an unfinished queued frame and forged image;
all 27 native port suites and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. This
establishes a bounded queue shape for later receipt-to-byte reconciliation;
the device restore gate remains EOPNOTSUPP.

2026-10-14 guest VM identity rekey prerequisite:
The guest VirtIO DTrace driver previously rejected every NODE_ID change once
it had adopted a VMID and epoch, so a restored guest could not accept the
destination identity. A negotiated NODE_REKEY_V1 control now requires the
exact old identity, validates the new identity, updates the guest node and
epoch, and rejects a repeated transition. The host wire, required feature,
private-image codec, and checkpoint validator understand the control. Guest
driver tests, all 27 native port suites, the snapshot-enabled bhyve build,
and the actual virtio_dtrace kernel-module build pass. Restore does not yet
emit the rekey control or reconcile its saved queues; EOPNOTSUPP remains.

2026-10-14 post-CLAIM destination KILL binding:
Protocol 25 adds HANDOFF_BIND_KILL. An attested destination can use it only
after CLAIM to copy an exact source KILL into the destination VM incarnation.
The daemon fsyncs the destination KILL journal entry, accepts an exact retry,
and reports pending or guest-applied state. Creating it after CLAIM avoids
pre-CLAIM replay by a destination forwarder; the restore startup fence must
remain held until all required KILLs are bound. The handoff fixture checks
premature and wrong-VM rejection, durable binding, retry, and the applied
marker required before a second migration. All 29 sanitized daemon suites,
27 native port suites, and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. Restore
still must call the bind for every relevant source KILL; late source KILLs
after CLAIM still need lineage propagation. The device gate remains closed.

2026-10-14 post-CLAIM bulk KILL binding:
Protocol 26 adds HANDOFF_BIND_KILLS_ALL. An attested destination can request
an exact post-CLAIM scan that durably copies every source KILL into its VM
incarnation before guest startup. The operation is idempotent, so a retry
finishes a partial copy after an interrupted journal write. The handoff
fixture checks premature and wrong-destination rejection, two source KILLs,
and an exact retry. All 29 sanitized daemon suites, 27 native port suites,
and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. This is a point-in-time copy:
late source KILL propagation and restore queue reconciliation remain open.
The device restore gate remains EOPNOTSUPP.

2026-10-14 late KILL propagation across claimed handoffs:
The host KILL journal now follows claimed ticket descendants under its
cross-process handoff lock, prepares matching live forwarder jobs before
publication, and fsyncs destination KILLs before the source KILL. A failed
later journal write still dispatches the prepared revocations, while a
reconnecting forwarder replays the durable records. The guest inbound path
retains its allocation-free KILL write under memory pressure. The handoff
ledger checks a two-hop lineage, retry after a directory fsync failure,
and failed job preparation. The control fixture checks that a consumer KILL
queues both incarnations, defers the old one, and sends the current guest's
KILL on the wire. All 29 sanitized daemon suites pass with the live-wire
fixture. Restore queue reconciliation and live VM validation remain open;
the device restore gate remains EOPNOTSUPP.

2026-10-14 destination restore KILL fence:
The bhyve restore transaction now invokes the attested bulk KILL bind after
the durable CLAIM and before device resume or vCPU startup release. An
uncertain ACK gets one exact idempotent retry; either failure keeps the
destination stopped. The snapshot-enabled bhyve build passes. The VirtIO
DTrace device restore gate still rejects images because queued ELF receipt
reconciliation, queue rekey publication, and connector readiness remain
unfinished.

2026-10-14 attested forwarder admission acknowledgment:
The two long-lived bhyve channels now opt into an admission ACK. The host
daemon sends it only after validating the VM attestation, recovering outbound
delivery state, and staging durable KILL replay. The daemon holds the client
output lock from event registration until the ACK is written, so a replayed
KILL or ELF frame cannot overtake it. Bhyve marks a channel configured only
after receiving a positive ACK; a rejection or timeout closes the socket and
leaves it unconfigured. Short-lived checkpoint control sockets retain their
existing request/reply protocol. All 29 sanitized daemon suites, 27 native
port suites, and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. This ACK proves
admission and replay staging, not guest application of KILLs. At this step,
restore still starts the connector after startup-fence release. The VirtIO
DTrace device restore gate remains EOPNOTSUPP pending queue reconciliation.

2026-10-14 file-restore connector startup fence:
The file restore transaction now starts the two attested daemon channels
after any required CLAIM and bulk KILL binding, then waits up to 30 seconds
for both admission ACKs before device resume or vCPU startup-fence release.
A failed admission or deadline leaves the destination stopped. Bhyverun no
longer starts a second connector after successful file restore. The bhyve
socket fixture checks positive ACK readiness, invalid deadline rejection,
and timeout with no daemon; all 27 native port suites and the snapshot-enabled
bhyve build pass. This still does not prove guest application of pending
KILLs. Live migration uses a separate commit/resume bridge and still lacks
HyperTrace handoff binding; the VirtIO DTrace snapshot gate prevents that
device from reaching an unsafe resume while queue/receipt reconciliation is
unfinished.

2026-10-14 admission ACK and raced KILL replay fixture:
An attested host-forwarder test now admits a VM with a durable KILL already
in the host journal while a replay worker races the accept path. The client
must receive the admission ACK before the KILL frame, and the replay job must
carry the exact VMID, epoch, PID, and source ID. This exercises the output
lock ordering through the actual host daemon admission and job code. All 30
sanitized native daemon suites pass. It does not establish guest application
of the KILL or make the device snapshot gate safe to open.

2026-10-14 live-migration HyperTrace gate and restore contract:
The live-migration source now refuses a HyperTrace VM before producing a
device-state blob. The destination refuses a locally enabled HyperTrace VM
before reading the blob, rejects an incoming HyperTrace handoff marker after
metadata parsing but before device mutation, and refuses a direct resume
before consuming the one-shot time-rebase credential. The snapshot-enabled
bhyve build passes. DEVICE-RESTORE-CONTRACT.md records the full queue, daemon
receipt, guest-journal rekey, and startup invariants needed to lift these
gates. In particular, the guest driver handles NODE_REKEY but guest dtraced
does not yet rekey its durable journals; live migration also lacks the file
restore handoff ticket. No live VM test was possible on this host.

2026-10-14 guest rekey recovery fence:
The dttransport device now exposes a synchronous VMID/epoch ioctl backed by
the registered VirtIO DTrace driver, with the same retirement lock as sends.
Guest dtraced checks every recovered inbound and outbound delivery and KILL
journal against that identity before retiring or replaying records. A live
NODE_REKEY must enqueue a priority daemon notice before the driver switches
identity;
failure stops the transport without switching. The daemon stops on the
notice. Identity-bearing guest sends are checked against the current driver
identity; identity-free ingress-status queries are blocked after rekey until
journal migration is implemented. The dttransport module exports its
registration symbols. The scoped dttransport and virtio_dtrace module builds,
30 sanitized daemon suites, 29 native port suites, and focused stale-journal,
priority ordering, and rekey enqueue-failure tests pass. The arm64 and riscv64 GENERIC builds
also pass and emit both guest modules. This guard does not migrate journals
or open the VirtIO DTrace snapshot gate. Live /dev/vmm, /dev/dttransport and
/dev/dtrace are unavailable on this host.

The identity ioctl now also reports the previous VMID/epoch and a persistent
in-driver rekey-pending flag for daemon restarts. Guest startup refuses any
recovered journal while that flag is set; the driver refuses another rekey
until the pending transaction is explicitly committed. The focused driver
and daemon recovery tests and scoped module builds pass. No ACK clears the
flag yet, so this still cannot restore a guest; it makes a missing journal
commit explicit rather than silently accepting a second transition.

2026-10-14 guest journal rekey groundwork:
Guest startup now queries the driver's identity even if all journals are
empty, so pending rekey cannot start unrelated daemon threads. The guest
inbound directory has a durable, idempotent old-to-new KILL rename operation:
it removes source applied markers before a rename, accepts an already-bound
destination KILL, and rejects any third VM incarnation before mutation.
It is not invoked yet because the host handoff and guest delivery receipts
must be reconciled first. The 32 sanitized native daemon suites pass; a
separate injected fsync failure confirms a retry leaves no lost KILL.

Guest delivery journals now have a low-level exact-record rekey operation.
It checks the complete source record, rewrites VMID/epoch and checksum through
an atomic rename, rejects duplicate journal phases, and handles both a leftover
temporary file and an already renamed destination on retry. It accepts only
bound guest inbound and HTD4 outbound records; older digestless and unbound
outbound records still need their original host receipt.
The operation is not wired into restore because host receipt authorization,
the guest journal commit, and the device queue transaction remain open.
The native daemon test exercises both delivery formats and injected fsync
failures before and after the rename, duplicate phase rejection, and refusal
of digestless outbound journals. The 32 sanitized native daemon suites and
29 native port suites pass; neither suite is a live VM restore test.

2026-10-14 guest startup and transport unload hardening:
Guest dtraced now refuses a successful identity ioctl with a zero VMID or
epoch, unknown flags, or stale old-identity fields without a pending rekey.
The dttransport registration path also refuses a provider after module unload
passes its busy check, closing the attach-after-unload window. Focused
identity and registration fixtures and the amd64 dttransport module build
pass; arm64 and riscv64 scoped module builds, all 32 sanitized native daemon
suites, and all 29 native port suites also pass. This does not clear the
pending-rekey or snapshot restore gates. The installed BSDTrace broker cannot
delegate a DTrace consumer on this host until the root-owned `dtraceall`
kernel module is loaded; `/dev/dtrace`, `/dev/dttransport`, and `/dev/vmm`
are currently absent.

2026-10-14 BSDTrace late DTrace module lookup:
The experimental branch's `system.Trace` provider now retries opening
`/dev/dtrace/dtrace` when an authorized session arrives after a late module
load. This closes the startup-order gap in the broker; it does not load
`dtraceall` or create the device itself. The scoped BSDTrace provider and
test binaries build. The native `session_test` consumer case cannot be
qualified on the current running kernel (it terminates with a bad system
call even under Kyua), and no live DTrace device is present.

2026-10-14 queued guest-upload receipt direction:
The private VirtIO DTrace restore query now identifies a queued `HOST_ELF`
as a guest-to-host upload and queries the host inbound scope-3 acceptance
journal. The daemon validates the reserved ticket and source KILL log in host
outbound while it reads the distinct inbound ledger, including records
accepted under a prior forwarding session of the same source VM incarnation.
Binding remains limited to queued host-to-guest scope-0 deliveries; it cannot
create a false outbound receipt for a guest upload. Native bhyve device tests,
all 29 native port suites, all 32 sanitized daemon suites, and the scoped
snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. The restore coordinator still does not
call the image helpers, and device save/restore remains gated by
`EOPNOTSUPP`; this is not live VM qualification.

The helper now hashes complete queued host-to-guest ELF runs and requires
the exact daemon receipt digest before either query or bind succeeds. A
checkpoint that captured only the suffix of an ELF run remains unsupported
because the missing prefix cannot be authenticated from the device image.
The native fixture covers a modified saved byte and a suffix-only queue;
all 29 native port suites and the scoped bhyve build pass after this change.

2026-10-14 host upload source revocation:
The host guest-to-host ELF admission path and its scope-3 receipt query now
consult the durable host outbound KILL journal as well as the inbound upload
journal. They take the same revocation lock as KILL publication, including
through the accepted upload commit, so a revoked source cannot write a new
pending receipt or complete an exact retry. Revocation is matched to the
attested VM ID and epoch; KILLs for a different incarnation do not suppress
this upload. The host-ingress native fixture covers first upload, retry,
receipt status, and different VM/epoch KILLs. This does not lift the device
save/restore gate or qualify a live VM.

2026-10-14 host consumer upload authorization:
The host inbound watcher now requires an accepted scope-3 journal record
before it queues an identified guest ELF. An orphan spool is memorized
without being forwarded, and pending records remain fenced. The watcher
holds the revocation lock through queue publication and checks the source
VM/epoch against the outbound KILL journal. A consumer job queued before a
later KILL repeats the accepted-record and KILL checks before sending bytes;
suppressed jobs rearm the connection for subsequent work. The host-ingress
fixture tests live forwarding, orphan suppression, and post-KILL queued
suppression. The queued send also revalidates the saved VM identity and the
bare ELF SHA-256 against the durable accepted receipt, preventing a replaced
spool from changing the bytes seen by the consumer. Hashing runs before the
final KILL lock so it does not delay revocation publication. An ELF already
sent to a host consumer before KILL still needs a
separate consumer-side cancellation protocol; no live cancellation is claimed.
The final socket write remains inside the revocation lock to order an
unsent job against KILL publication; a backpressured consumer can delay that
publication up to the bounded send deadline.
The sanitized native daemon build and all 32 component suites pass, including
the updated host-ingress fixture; the final send-lock placement also passes
the focused sanitized fixture. No live DTrace device is available here.

2026-10-14 guest upload receipt digest:
The host now hashes the accepted bare ELF before queuing `HOST_ELF` and
places its SHA-256 in the existing receipt field. The guest VirtIO driver
requires a nonzero digest and forwards it to `dtraced`; the waiting guest
daemon compares it with the ELF it actually submitted before releasing its
sender. A new mandatory `VTDTR_WIRE_F_HOST_ELF_DIGEST_V1` feature bit rejects
older peers. Private-image version 2 requires the queued digest, and the
read-only restore query compares it with the exact scope-3 durable host
receipt rather than accepting any nonzero digest. Native fixtures cover
missing and mismatched digests. All 29 native port suites, all 32 sanitized
daemon suites, the snapshot-enabled bhyve build, the actual dtraced build,
and the virtio_dtrace kernel-module build pass. The coordinator still does
not call the private-image helpers, and device save/restore remains gated by
`EOPNOTSUPP`; no live VM behavior is claimed.

2026-10-14 runtime host upload receipt confirmation:
After the daemon's write ACK, bhyve now queries the exact attested scope-3
host ingress receipt and compares its accepted phase, revocation state and
SHA-256 with the bare ELF it submitted. Only then does it queue `HOST_ELF`
for the guest. A lost query, mismatched digest or revoked source resets the
device without acknowledging the guest sender; recovery must reconcile the
already durable host acceptance. The native bhyve fixture exercises all
three failure paths. All 29 native port suites and the snapshot-enabled
bhyve build pass. This strengthens runtime acceptance but does not implement
the restore coordinator, guest journal rekey, or live qualification.

2026-10-14 queued KILL restore phase:
The private-image handoff query now accepts a pending or applied durable
source KILL for a queued `KILL` control. A queued `HOST_KILL_APPLIED` receipt
still requires an applied marker. Both paths match the exact ticket, PID and
source ID and require revocation. This fixes a false rejection of the
unconsumed KILL that a restored guest must replay; it does not publish the
device queue or relax the startup fence. The native bhyve fixture covers
both phases, all 29 native port suites pass, and the snapshot-enabled bhyve
build passes.

2026-10-14 restore coordinator private-image query:
The file-restore preflight now captures the exact VirtIO DTrace private-image
span during validation of the complete PCI/VirtIO record. After memory
preflight and durable handoff RESERVE, the coordinator queries that image
against the reserved ticket. Query failure requests reservation cancellation;
query success deliberately returns EOPNOTSUPP and requests the same before
opening the kernel restore session or changing guest memory. Cancellation
failure is logged and the stopped destination remains fenced. Direct device
preflight retains its original EOPNOTSUPP gate. The native fixture checks the
captured span and refuses an injected generic-validator error; all 29 native
port suites and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass. Queue publication,
guest journal rekey, and live host/guest qualification remain open.

2026-10-14 guest transport rekey commit primitive:
The root-owned dttransport device now accepts `DTT_COMMIT_VM_REKEY` with an
exact old and new VMID/epoch. Its registered VirtIO DTrace callback checks
the pending transition under the driver lock and clears the pending flag
only on an exact match; mismatches, shutdown, and duplicate commits fail.
The transport holds registration through the callback so unload waits for
in-flight commits. The daemon does not invoke this operation: host receipt
authorization, durable guest journal migration, and device queue publication
must precede it. Focused retirement and driver identity fixtures and scoped
amd64 dttransport and virtio_dtrace module builds pass. All 29 native port
suites pass; no live rekey or VM restore was exercised.

2026-10-14 guest rekey ABI and recovery validation:
The changed `dtt_register_transport` callback signature now has dttransport
module version 2, and virtio_dtrace requires exactly that version. This
prevents mixing the new driver with a version-1 transport module. Guest
daemon startup now rejects malformed pending old identities and VMIDs outside
the namespace before treating rekey as pending. The focused recovery test
and rebuilt amd64 modules pass. Incremental 16-job arm64 and RISC-V GENERIC
builds also pass and emit target-architecture dttransport and virtio_dtrace
modules. The actual dtraced build and all 32 sanitized native daemon suites
pass. The daemon still fences pending rekey until the durable journal
transaction and host authorization exist.

2026-10-14 guest KILL ACK rekey retry hardening:
The low-level guest KILL journal rekey now clears durable applied markers for
all old and new identity records before moving any source KILL. An interrupted
retry with destination-only records therefore cannot carry a stale applied
ACK into the restored guest. The focused sanitized fixture covers an existing
destination ACK, an injected fsync failure, and an idempotent retry. The
ACK writer now uses the same cross-process journal lock as rekey, so an
overlapping old daemon cannot recreate a source ACK after the rewrite clears
it. A two-process contention fixture, the actual dtraced build, and all 32
sanitized daemon suites pass on the final source. The production restore gate
remains closed; this primitive is not invoked yet.

2026-10-14 destination VirtIO DTrace queue plan:
The private-image query and bind paths now build a disposable destination
queue before contacting the daemon. The plan starts with an exact
`NODE_REKEY`, moves KILLs before saved ELF controls, rewrites supported
controls to the destination VMID/epoch, and drops source
`HOST_KILL_APPLIED` receipts because the guest KILL journal rekey invalidates
their applied markers. Saved READY and EOF markers are also dropped: they
cannot authorize TX replay after rekey. The guest's current automatic READY
exchange is not yet tied to journal commit, so queue publication remains
gated until an explicit rekey-complete handshake exists. Unsupported
controls, partial guest uploads, and capacity or allocation failures leave
no published queue. The plan is discarded after validation; no production
restore publishes it yet.
All 29 native port suites and the snapshot-enabled bhyve build pass; the
native fixture checks rekey-first order, KILL priority, identity rewriting,
ACK and READY omission, and refusal of a partial image.

2026-10-14 packet-ID layout and live-device qualification gate:
The current packet ID remains inline in every mbuf header and `MSIZE` remains
512. A call-site audit found that the ID writers in BPF, socket send, TAP,
and VirtIO net already check `m_mbufid_ensure()` failure, while read-only
probe paths tolerate a missing ID. Packet-tag move, copy, and persistence
primitives support a lazy sidecar design, but the proposed complete helper
and 256-byte mbuf layout change was rejected by automatic approval review
because it changes a core kernel ABI and allocation behavior across drivers.
No part of that rejected change was applied. The existing isolated LP64 and
ILP32 size checks in `PACKET-PROVENANCE-LAYOUT.md` are insufficient to qualify
the runtime change. Current-tree `native-port.py` passes 29 suites and
`native-daemon.py` passes 32 sanitized suites. This host has no
`/dev/dtrace/dtrace`, `/dev/dttransport`, or `/dev/vmm`; BSDTrace can delegate
an already present DTrace device but cannot create it, so live HyperTrace
host/guest qualification remains open.

2026-10-14 guest delivery rekey writer serialization:
Guest scope-1 and scope-2 delivery transitions, terminal inbound retirement,
and accepted outbound retirement now take the same cross-process journal
lock as the exact-record guest delivery rekey. An older daemon process can no
longer mutate those journal records in the middle of a rekey rename. The
guest-delivery-rekey fixture exercises each operation with a second process
and verifies it waits behind the lock. The actual native daemon builds, all
32 sanitized daemon suites pass, and `git diff --check` passes. This is a
serialization prerequisite only: the restore coordinator still needs host
receipt authorization, a durable journal transaction and rekey-complete
handshake before invoking the rekey or publishing the device queue.

2026-10-14 durable retired-identity journal fence:
The cross-process lock alone did not stop an old daemon from creating a new
source-identity record after a rekey finished. Guest KILL and delivery rekey
now publish a durable marker for the exact old-to-new VMID/epoch before
changing records. Bound guest delivery and KILL writes, ACK writes, and
delivery retirement reject a retired old identity under that lock. An exact
rekey retry validates the marker's destination; a conflicting destination or
malformed marker fails closed. A failed fsync after marker rename can be
retried without losing the source records. Identity-free outbound records
remain representable and retireable; the driver still fences their ingress
query while rekey is pending. The focused fixtures cover stale writers,
conflicting destinations, marker-publication failure and retry, and the
identity-free outbound case. The actual native daemon builds and all 32
sanitized daemon suites pass, as does `git diff --check`. No production path
invokes guest journal rekey yet; host receipt authorization, completion
handshake, queue publication, and live VM tests remain open.

2026-10-14 empty guest journal rekey fence and marker hardening:
An exact `fence_guest_rekey()` operation now checks guest delivery and KILL
records under the cross-process lock, rejects duplicate delivery phases,
unsupported scope/version or foreign identities, and durably retires the old
VMID/epoch even when the inbound or outbound directory is empty. Future
recovery must call it on both guest directories before per-record rewrites;
it does not authorize replay or invoke the driver commit ioctl. The marker
reader opens nonblocking, so a malformed FIFO at the marker pathname fails
closed instead of hanging before its regular-file check. Focused fixtures
cover empty journals, exact retries, a conflicting destination, duplicate
phases, a foreign KILL, and the malformed FIFO. The actual native daemon
builds and all 32 sanitized suites pass; `git diff --check` passes. Host
receipt reconciliation, daemon journal transaction/restart, rekey-complete
handshake, device publication, and live VM qualification remain open.

2026-10-14 BSDTrace capability and rekey batch audit:
The installed `system.Trace` BSDTrace service is running, but this ordinary
shell cannot reach its capability endpoint: direct `tracecmp_open()` and
read-only `tracecmp_stats()` probes both returned `ENOENT`. That result does
not establish whether an authorized service label can connect. The running
kernel has no `/dev/dtrace/dtrace`, `/dev/dttransport`, or `/dev/vmm`, and
`dtraceall` is not loaded. BSDTrace delegates an existing DTrace consumer;
it cannot create the kernel device. The separate lowercase `bsdtrace`
command uses hardware trace. Live validation still requires the root-owned
kernel modules/devices and an authorized test component.

The bhyve communicator can currently deliver `NODE_REKEY` and later saved
controls in one descriptor batch, before the guest daemon commits its
journals. The guest driver's automatic READY is not a commit acknowledgement.
The required exact old/new-identity completion exchange is recorded in
`DEVICE-RESTORE-CONTRACT.md`. A proposed production-only queue barrier was
rejected by automatic approval review because it had no completion path and
could indefinitely stop VirtIO tracing; no part of that barrier was applied.
The snapshot/restore gate remains closed with `EOPNOTSUPP`.

2026-10-14 rekey ACK implementation review:
The guest driver has no commit event today: `DTT_COMMIT_VM_REKEY` only clears
its pending identity flag. It also sends an ordinary READY after receiving
`NODE_REKEY`, and its normal TX worker waits for host READY. A direct TX
commit event must therefore be paired with negotiated feature support,
exact transition validation, guest TX suppression, a bounded host timeout,
and retry handling for a full ring or lost ioctl response. The first proposed
host barrier was rejected by automatic approval review because it lacked a
guest completion path. A second patch that added a guest-side event and host
gate was rejected because a production wire change without live VM
validation and explicit compatibility/timeout behavior could stall or reset
tracing. Neither rejected patch landed. The contract now records these
conditions; rootless component tests alone cannot qualify the wire cutover.

2026-10-14 dttransport enqueue/unload lifetime:
The exported guest enqueue path now holds the transport lifetime lock while
reading and using its global softc. Module unload withdraws that pointer
under the same exclusive lock before destroying the cdev or freeing memory;
an enqueue arriving after the withdrawal returns `ENXIO`. This closes a
possible softc use-after-free when a late producer races unload. The
sanitized queue fixture covers a blocked enqueuer crossing the withdrawal
and a dying softc, while the registration retirement fixture still passes.
The actual amd64 `dttransport.ko` builds with `-Werror` against the matching
kernel headers, and `git diff --check` passes. A live module unload/reload
race remains unverified until the device can be loaded on a test host.

2026-10-14 paired guest journal fence:
The guest journal now has `fence_guest_rekey_pair()` for the future restore
coordinator. It locks both directories in stable physical-directory order,
preflights both sets of records and existing markers, and only then publishes
the inbound and outbound old-identity fences. A corrupt outbound journal or
conflicting marker no longer causes the healthy inbound journal to be fenced.
If the second marker's fsync fails after the first was published, an exact
retry completes the pair; no record rewrite or driver commit is authorized
until both fences are durable. A new sanitized fixture exercises bad outbound
records, a conflicting marker, stale-writer rejection, and partial-fsync
retry. The actual native daemon builds and all 32 sanitized component suites
pass, including the original paired-fence cases; the revised partial-fsync
fixture also passes on its own. This is a journal primitive only: there is no
production restore caller or live VM qualification yet.

2026-10-14 dttransport read delivery and unload wake:
The guest transport no longer removes an event before copying it to the
daemon. One reader reserves the head record while copyout runs; a failed
copy leaves that record queued, and a newly arrived priority VM rekey notice
remains ahead of it. Other readers wait or receive `EWOULDBLOCK` until the
copy finishes. Module unload now wakes an empty-queue reader and the read
loop exits with `ENXIO` when the transport is dying, so `destroy_dev()` does
not wait on an unwoken reader. The sanitized test extracts the production
read function and covers failed copy, priority insertion during copy,
nonblocking concurrent read, and dying transport. The actual amd64 module
builds with `-Werror`; all 30 native port suites pass. Module load also
publishes the global softc only after its locks, queue, condition variable,
and cdev are ready, under the same lock used by enqueue and unload. Live
close/unload and daemon-crash behavior remain unqualified without loaded
devices.

2026-10-14 guest VirtIO-net packet-ID allocation guard:
The guest TX path now preserves an existing canonical packet ID without
calling the allocation helper. If no node identity exists yet, it emits the
reserved zero wire ID without allocating storage for a new ID; a malformed
existing ID is cleared. This prepares the path for an optional lazy sidecar
without changing the current inline mbuf layout or the VirtIO wire format.
The source-extracted sanitized fixture covers no-origin packets, inherited
lineage, allocation failure, and malformed IDs. The amd64 `if_vtnet.ko` build
passes with `-Werror`, all 31 native port suites pass, and `git diff --check`
passes. Bhyve currently validates a guest-supplied packet ID's canonical
shape but cannot authenticate its claimed origin; this remains a trust gap
for provenance claims, alongside the mbuf allocation cost and unavailable
live cross-guest qualification.

2026-10-14 retired guest-rekey destination fence:
The paired and single-directory guest journal preflights now reject a
destination VMID/epoch that already has its own rekey marker. Both low-level
KILL and delivery rewrites repeat the check under the journal lock before
publishing their old-to-new marker or changing records. This prevents an
exact retry of an earlier handoff from writing into an incarnation already
retired by a later handoff. The sanitized guest KILL and delivery fixtures
cover the retired destination and verify the source records remain intact;
the actual native daemon builds and all 32 suites passed after the paired
fence change, and both focused fixtures passed after the low-level guards.
There is still no production restore coordinator or live VM qualification.

2026-10-14 packet-ID generation and validity correction:
The guest TX helper now checks whether a node origin is ready before any
allocation, then generates an ID directly into the mbuf. That retains the
previous `ddtrace::pkttag::gen` probe context and avoids emitting a generated
ID for a packet dropped on allocation failure. If the node identity clears
between the check and generation, the helper clears the ID and sends the
reserved zero wire value. `mbufid_isvalid()` now applies the same canonical
magic and padding check as VirtIO/TAP, so audit and LRO do not treat a malformed
ID as valid. The source-extracted tests cover zero origin, inherited ID,
failed allocation, race, malformed magic and padding. The 16-job amd64
GENERIC build passes. Live packet traffic and mbuf-size qualification remain
open.

2026-10-14 arm64 and RISC-V DTrace provider user-stack coverage:
The arm64 and RISC-V DTrace ISA modules now supply user PC/frame-pointer
stacks instead of printing an unimplemented diagnostic. The walkers follow
each architecture's frame layout, handle entry probes, cap circular walks,
and zero unused PC slots. Arm64 entry stack depth now includes the entry PC;
RISC-V stack depth returns its computed value. RISC-V invop argument fetching
now reads a0-a7 from the saved trapframe and later arguments from a checked
kernel stack address; its ISA module includes the DTrace thread accessor
header used by the existing invop dispatcher. A source-extracted sanitized
fixture exercises both architectures' frames, entry cases, bad stacks,
arguments, and invalid context. All 32 native port suites and 16-job arm64
and RISC-V GENERIC builds pass, producing target-architecture dtrace modules.
No live non-amd64 provider execution has been exercised.

2026-10-14 dttransport reader/unload synchronization:
Unload now sets the transport's dying flag while holding the same queue lock
that protects the blocked reader's wait predicate, then broadcasts before
releasing that lock. The prior write under only the device and lifetime locks
could race a reader inspecting the flag under the queue lock. The sanitized
read and queue fixtures pass, and the 16-job amd64 GENERIC build produces a
new dttransport module. Live close/unload remains unqualified without a
loaded device.

2026-10-14 dttransport opener identity after descriptor transfer:
The root-owned transport now records the opener's PID and immutable process
start time alongside its process pointer. Read, write, and ioctl reject a
descriptor presented by a different incarnation even if the original
process exited and the kernel reused its address. This avoids a long-lived
process hold that could stall the opener's exit. Read and write also return
the ownership error if the opener has disappeared instead of asserting.
The source-extracted read fixture mutates PID and start time while preserving
the same pointer, then checks an absent opener and that queued data cannot
be consumed. All 32 native port suites and 16-job amd64, arm64, and RISC-V
GENERIC builds pass. A live
transferred-descriptor test remains outstanding.

2026-10-14 attestation descriptor clock failure and standalone mbuf ID header:
The shared host/daemon descriptor send and receive helpers now fail with the
clock error if their monotonic deadline source fails, matching the ordinary
frame-transfer helper instead of continuing with an invalid remaining time.
The attestation-fd fixture injects that error into both directions. The full
sanitized native daemon build and all 32 component suites pass, as do all 32
native port suites. An amd64 16-job development buildworld initially exposed
that the recently added packet-ID canonical helpers used `NULL` without
including its definition outside the kernel; `sys/mbufid.h` now includes
`stddef.h`. The repeated buildworld passes its standalone-header check and
completes successfully. This validates compilation, not a live DTrace or VM
session.

2026-10-14 guest rekey recovery deadline failures:
Guest startup identity recovery now checks a failed monotonic-clock read
before its first `DTT_GET_VM_IDENTITY` ioctl and after each retry; it cannot
spin indefinitely when the driver returns `EAGAIN` or `ENXIO`. Runtime
`query_vm_identity()` likewise fails before publishing a query ID or clears
an outstanding ID on a later clock error, so a late reply cannot complete
another request. The socket startup wait returns the clock error, and the
guest transport listener shuts down if its deadline clock disappears rather
than silently skipping replay, ACK and status timeouts. Source-extracted
startup and socket fixtures, the wrapped runtime query/listener fixture,
the sanitized daemon build and all 32 component suites pass. Final 16-job
development buildworld runs pass on amd64, arm64 and RISC-V. The VirtIO
DTrace save/restore gate remains `EOPNOTSUPP`; these checks do not qualify a
live guest rekey or queue publication.

2026-10-14 BSDTrace device check and repeated ELF receipt boundary:
The installed BSDTrace provider delegates an existing DTrace consumer after
authorization; it does not load `dtraceall`. This boot has no loaded
`dtraceall` and no `/dev/dtrace/dtrace`, `/dev/dttransport`, or `/dev/vmm`, so
BSDTrace cannot supply a DTrace descriptor for live HyperTrace validation yet.
The lowercase `bsdtrace` utility uses hardware tracing and is a separate
facility. In the private-image restore preflight and bind helpers, only an
ELF fragment following an open `elfhasmore` run now skips its own receipt
lookup. A second complete ELF with a reused delivery ID must get its payload
digest checked against the source journal. The bhyve device regression case
demonstrates that a mismatched second payload fails with `EPROTO`; all 32
sanitized native port suites and the 16-job amd64 development buildworld pass.
The device restore gate remains closed.

2026-10-14 guest rekey destination collision and BSDTrace host ABI:
Guest KILL and delivery preflight now treats destination-identity journal
records as an interrupted retry only when the exact old-to-new durable rekey
marker already exists. An unfenced destination record fails before the
coordinator can publish either marker, clear a KILL ACK, or rewrite a
delivery. A direct per-record delivery retry likewise refuses destination
bytes without the old identity fence. Focused collision cases cover direct
KILL rekey, per-record delivery rekey, single-directory fence, and paired
fence; the sanitized daemon build, all 32 component suites, and a 16-job
amd64 development buildworld pass. This does not itself perform the host
receipt transaction or open restore.

The rootless BSDTrace consumer test's earlier `SIGSYS` is now identified:
`truss` shows the experimental world calling syscall 1000
(`cap_xfer_limit`), while the running kernel reports it as unknown and sends
`SIGSYS`. The test has no DTrace-device dependency, so this is a kernel/world
ABI mismatch on the live validation host, not evidence of a provider
failure. A matching installed kernel must be booted before that test or a
BSDTrace delegated consumer can be qualified.

2026-10-14 BSDTrace mixed-kernel capability ABI guard:
The kernel now advertises `kern.features.capability_transfer_limits` when
the 5BSD descriptor transfer and fork/exec limit syscalls are compiled in.
BSDTrace checks this CAPRD feature before touching those syscalls in either
its DTrace-directory open path or session startup. A missing feature returns
`ENOSYS`; a late DTrace device no longer turns an older-kernel mismatch into
`SIGSYS`, and the provider stays up to report rejected sessions. The
rootless consumer-open test exercises this failure path on the current
kernel and passes. A capability-mode probe also read an existing CAPRD
feature successfully. This does not qualify the positive delegated-consumer
path, because the running kernel predates syscall 1000 and has no DTrace
device. The packet-ID header was also corrected after GENERIC exposed a
kernel-only `<stddef.h>` include: userland uses that header, while kernel
code uses its normal `sys/param.h` and `sys/systm.h` definitions. The 16-job
amd64 development buildworld, amd64/arm64/RISC-V GENERIC builds, and all
32 sanitized native port suites pass. No kernel or module was installed.

2026-10-14 guest rekey journal completion predicate:
The paired guest journal helper now checks the exact durable fences while
holding both in-process and cross-process locks. It refuses completion if
either directory still contains an old or foreign identity, an unbound
outbound delivery, any KILL applied marker (including an orphan), or a
malformed journal, or if the destination was subsequently retired. The
focused sanitized fixture exercises missing and
partial fences, phased rewrites, stale ACKs, unbound outbound work, and an
orphan ACK. This is only a prerequisite for `DTT_COMMIT_VM_REKEY`: host
receipt authorization, daemon restart recovery, the distinct guest-to-host
commit acknowledgement, queue publication, and live VM validation remain
open. The sanitized daemon build and all 32 component suites passed before
the final retired-destination check; that check and its regression fixture
also pass in a focused sanitized rebuild. The final 16-job amd64 development
buildworld passes. The restore gate remains `EOPNOTSUPP`.

2026-10-14 guest transport monotonic deadline failures:
The guest transport now creates checked absolute deadlines for host socket
startup, recovered-delivery queries, inbound-status queries, ELF receive and
forwarding, host receipt ACKs, and retry scheduling. A failed clock read or
overflow no longer becomes a plausible positive deadline. In particular,
the ELF fragment writer now treats a failed clock read as terminal while a
full `dttransport` queue returns `EAGAIN`, instead of retrying indefinitely.
The source-extracted writer fixture fills the queue, injects clock failure
after one retry, and requires shutdown; the descriptor helper fixture checks
failed and overflowing deadline construction. All 32 sanitized native port
suites, all 32 sanitized daemon component suites, the actual daemon link, and
a 16-job amd64 development buildworld pass. This does not qualify a live
guest/host session or open the VM restore gate.

2026-10-14 packet ID before guest identity and BSDTrace device check:
The shared mbuf ID generator now writes the reserved all-zero ID when the
VirtIO DTrace node identity has not arrived, so BPF and datagram sends do not
create a noncanonical ID that the VirtIO/TAP wire path would reject.  The
source-extracted VirtIO packet fixture exercises the real generator with and
without an identity, including an identity disappearing after the driver's
readiness check.  The focused sanitized fixture and 32 native port suites
pass; 16-job GENERIC buildkernel runs pass on amd64, arm64/aarch64 and
riscv/riscv64.  The current host still has
no /dev/dtrace/dtrace, /dev/dttransport or /dev/vmm; only hwt.ko is loaded.
The installed bsdtrace(8) binary is hardware tracing, while the BSDTrace
service delegates an existing DTrace descriptor and cannot create one.
The 512-byte mbuf footprint and live guest/host provenance qualification
remain open.

2026-10-14 fail-closed NODE_REKEY control barrier:
Bhyve now delivers NODE_REKEY alone even when several guest descriptors are
available, retains later KILL/ELF controls, and records a pending rekey gate.
An ordinary guest READY cannot reopen that gate; an ordinary guest-to-host
control instead marks the device for reset. Checkpoint preflight returns
EBUSY while the barrier is pending, and device reset discards the queued
controls and clears the gate. The guest driver suppresses automatic READY
and queued TX while its old/new identity is pending and rejects an inbound
control before journal commit. Source-extracted host and guest fixtures cover
the multi-descriptor batch, late READY, premature ELF/KILL, retained queue,
and reset. All 32 sanitized native port suites pass; 16-job GENERIC kernel
builds pass on amd64, arm64/aarch64, and riscv/riscv64. The amd64 development
buildworld and a subsequent bhyve compile/link pass. This is a fail-closed
partial barrier: no distinct authenticated commit-ACK wire control, daemon
transaction, host release, timeout/quarantine, or live restored guest run
exists yet. The save/restore callback still returns EOPNOTSUPP.

2026-10-15 host connector and daemon deadline audit:
The host HyperTrace connector now constructs checked absolute deadlines for
session setup, admission ACK, ELF reads/ACKs, writes, handoff queries, and
status requests. Guest recovery identity and the forwarder attestation-fd
receipt also reject a failed or overflowing clock before using a deadline.
Daemon process launch, client send/receive defaults, job sends/reads, VM
identity query, and runtime retry/scan timers use the same checked clock
arithmetic. The C++ socket helper rejects zero and overflowing budgets before
constructing a time point. The shared monotonic conversion rejects an
overflowing seconds value before multiplication. A source-extracted recovery
test covers deadline overflow without issuing the identity ioctl; the
descriptor helper fixture covers both deadline and clock-conversion overflow,
and the transport fixture checks socket-budget rejection. The 32 sanitized
native port suites pass with the shared clock header. All 32 sanitized native
daemon component suites, the actual daemon link, and an amd64 16-job
development buildworld pass after the final socket-header change.
This is defensive timing work, not a guest/host session qualification or a
reason to open the restore gate.

2026-10-15 idempotent local guest rekey commit:
The guest driver no longer drops the old identity or lifts its traffic fence
when DTT_COMMIT_VM_REKEY records a local journal commit. It retains the exact
old/new VMID and epoch, returns success for an exact repeated ioctl after a
lost reply, rejects a different transition, and reports a distinct
DTT_IDENTITY_REKEY_LOCAL_COMMITTED phase through DTT_GET_VM_IDENTITY. Ordinary
READY, queued TX, and premature inbound controls remain blocked. Guest daemon
startup recognizes the new phase but refuses ordinary journal recovery and
replay until a separate host acknowledgement exists. The identity ioctl ABI
size remains 24 bytes. Focused sanitized guest-driver and recovery fixtures
cover exact retry, mismatched retry, and the pending and locally committed
phases. All 32 native port suites, all 32 native daemon component suites
with the actual daemon
link, a 16-job amd64 development buildworld, and 16-job GENERIC kernel builds
on amd64, arm64/aarch64, and riscv/riscv64 pass. No production daemon path
invokes the commit ioctl. The authenticated host commit control, exact host
receipt, release of the pending queue, timeout/quarantine, and live restored
guest qualification remain open; snapshot still returns EOPNOTSUPP.

2026-10-15 VirtIO DTrace transport ownership and node-ID teardown:
Guest attach now claims the singleton dttransport callback slot before
installing its interrupt handler or advertising READY. A second attach that
cannot claim the slot therefore cannot process NODE_ID and overwrite the
active guest's global packet origin. During detach, the driver stops the
device, tears down interrupts, joins the communicator and drains RX tasks
while retaining transport ownership. It then clears dtrace_node_id only if
this registered device still owns the matching node ID, synchronizes CPUs,
and unregisters the callback before freeing queues. The source-extracted
guest driver fixture checks unregistered, foreign-ID and owned-ID clearing;
all 32 native port suites and 16-job GENERIC builds on amd64,
arm64/aarch64 and riscv/riscv64 pass. Live module attach/detach and packet
origin behavior still need a matching kernel and DTrace device.

2026-10-15 host delivery revocation serialization:
Every host delivery phase transition now holds the same durable directory
lock as KILL publication and checks source revocation while holding it.
An INSTALLED receipt, accepted retry, or new pending delivery therefore
cannot advance after another daemon process commits a KILL for that VM
incarnation. Terminal retirement remains possible after revocation.
Global source tombstones also use this directory lock. The installed-receipt
fixture checks reopened-ledger retries and new admission after KILL; all 32
sanitized native daemon component suites, the actual daemon link, and a
16-job amd64 development buildworld pass.
The host still has only hwt.ko loaded and lacks /dev/dtrace/dtrace,
/dev/dttransport, and /dev/vmm. BSDTrace cannot create those devices here;
live VM qualification remains open.

2026-10-15 host guest-ELF ingress versus cross-process KILL:
An attested guest ELF upload now holds the outbound journal's source-live
transaction across its inbound pending record, spool publication, and
accepted-record fsync. A second daemon process cannot commit a source KILL
between the initial check and the durable acceptance. The guard uses the
same process lock as KILL publication and preserves the outbound-to-inbound
lock order used by handoff queries. A focused host-ingress fixture forks a
second process, verifies its KILL waits while the source transaction is held,
and checks that subsequent acceptance is refused. The actual daemon link and
all 32 sanitized native daemon component suites pass. Live host/guest
qualification and the restore commit exchange remain open. A 16-job amd64
development buildworld also passes.

2026-10-15 cross-process host ingress status snapshot:
Ingress-status queries now hold the outbound journal's process lock while
reading the exact inbound acceptance receipt and its local tombstone. A KILL
from another daemon process cannot commit between the host revocation check
and the receipt lookup. The source transaction reports whether the source was
already revoked; acceptance still requires a live source, while status can
report the revoked receipt. The sanitized daemon link and all 32 component
suites, including before/after-KILL ingress status cases, pass. A 16-job amd64
development buildworld also passes. This does not complete the restore commit
acknowledgement or qualify live host/guest replay.

2026-10-15 rootless DTrace guest boot and early-loader fix:
The ELF kernel linker called dtrace_fault_func before DTrace registered that
callback. A QEMU TCG boot of the experimental amd64 GENERIC kernel panicked
at link_elf_search_symbol before init, with or without DTrace autoload.
Both ELF linker implementations now check the callback for NULL at all four
call sites. After the fix, a repeatable rootless QEMU smoke boot reached
userland, loaded dtraceall and dttransport, exposed /dev/dtrace/dtrace and
/dev/dttransport, completed a DTrace BEGIN program, and fired a
syscall::openat:entry probe. The guest reported an mbuf zone size of 512
bytes. The smoke harness and result are in
contrib/hypertrace/tests/live-qemu-boot.py and
/usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/live-qemu-harness-nodes-20261015/result.json.
GENERIC kernel builds pass on amd64, arm64/aarch64, and riscv/riscv64.
This proves DTrace works in a booted experimental kernel; QEMU does not
provide the HyperTrace VirtIO DTrace device, so live bhyve transport,
host/guest replay, and restored-VM qualification remain open. Restore still
returns EOPNOTSUPP.

2026-10-15 arm64 immediate-stack module and live provider qualification:
A rootless arm64 QEMU boot first reached userland but could not load
dtrace.ko: it referenced the missing dtrace_getpcimmstack symbol. Arm64 and
RISC-V now implement that ISA entry point using a shared immediate-stack
symbol formatter, with the source included in both kernel and module builds.
The formatter preserves each aligned symbol-and-offset slot and uses the
existing DTrace symbol cache. A separate defect made immstack() empty on
ordinary QEMU guests, including amd64: the core treated every VM guest as a
HyperTrace hypercall guest. It now selects the remote stack buffer only when
the HyperTrace hypercall capability is present; PRINTIMMSTACK requires an
actual remote probe argument. After these changes, amd64 QEMU prints named
frames including amd64_syscall and fast_syscall_common. Arm64 QEMU loads
dtraceall and dttransport, creates both device nodes, runs BEGIN and a
syscall::openat:entry probe, and prints named immstack() frames including
do_el0_sync and handle_el0_sync. The repeatable rootless harness is
contrib/hypertrace/tests/live-qemu-boot.py; the arm64 passing result is
/usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/live-qemu-arm64-guest-detect-20261015/result.json.
After the final core change, 16-job GENERIC builds pass for amd64,
arm64/aarch64 and riscv/riscv64. Both non-amd64 dtrace.ko builds export
dtrace_getpcimmstack and dtrace_immstack_format.

The first RISC-V QEMU boot timed out before the kernel banner. A bounded
exception trace located the first fault in uart_getenv: the FDT UART path
used an uninitialized bus-space tag from the loader's console override.
uart_getenv now returns ENXIO when that tag is unavailable, allowing the
platform FDT probe to establish it. The next boot exposed the QEMU UEFI
ACPI configuration's missing CPU ISA extensions; the smoke harness selects
FDT with acpi=off. RISC-V then booted to userland and ran BEGIN, a
syscall::openat:entry probe, and immstack(). Its first stack lookup chose
the ELF $x code-mapping marker. RISC-V kernel/module symbol searches now
skip $x and $d mapping markers, so the final live run resolves the named
do_trap_user frame. The passing result is
/usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/live-qemu-riscv-native-symbol-20261015/result.json.
The initial failed result and bounded trace remain under
/usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/live-qemu-riscv-pflash-20261015/.
Final 16-job GENERIC builds pass on amd64, arm64/aarch64, and
riscv/riscv64 after the shared UART guard. These QEMU tests establish
native DTrace device and provider execution on all three architectures;
they do not supply the HyperTrace VirtIO DTrace device or qualify live
bhyve transport and VM restore. The EOPNOTSUPP restore gate remains.

2026-10-15 guest transport startup failure handling:
The VirtIO DTrace guest driver now checks whether its initial READY and
node-identity query controls were actually queued. A failed allocation or
full control queue aborts attach, rather than leaving a live-looking device
whose host waits indefinitely. The RX path likewise stops the transport if
it cannot queue a later READY. The sanitized guest-driver fault test covers
first- and second-allocation failures and cleanup; all 32 native port suites
and 16-job GENERIC builds on amd64, arm64/aarch64, and riscv/riscv64 pass.
Native DTrace QEMU boots use
/dev/dtrace/dtrace directly; BSDTrace is not needed to create that node.
This does not qualify the separate HyperTrace VirtIO host/guest device or
change the EOPNOTSUPP restore gate.

2026-10-15 host control rejection and EOF batching:
The bhyve VirtIO DTrace endpoint now resets its transport on a guest-to-host
KILL or an unknown control ID. The guest driver never sends KILL in that
direction, so silently accepting either control hid a protocol failure.
The host RX walk now treats EOF as a batch boundary instead of discarding
the rest of the ring: a READY-only batch can be followed by another batch
before the host handles the interrupt. A sanitized fixture covers both
invalid controls and a NODE_ID query queued after EOF. All 32 native port
suites and a 16-job development buildworld pass. This strengthens ordinary
session handling but does not implement the restore rekey commit exchange,
queue publication, or live bhyve qualification. Save and restore stay gated.

2026-10-15 checkpoint generation retention during uncertain handoff:
The source checkpoint coordinator now waits for a confirmed HyperTrace
handoff COMMIT before deleting the previous generated checkpoint members.
Publication can make a new manifest visible before the daemon reply, so an
uncertain COMMIT keeps the source VM stopped and retains the older member
files for manual recovery. Non-HyperTrace cleanup keeps its prior timing;
legacy checkpoints have no generated members to remove. A 16-job amd64
development buildworld passes. This does not make the new manifest
restorable if its ticket is unresolved, and it does not open device
save/restore, which still returns EOPNOTSUPP.

2026-10-15 isolated guest receipt-driver candidate:
The source-only packet-sidecar candidate now negotiates the optional
REKEY_RECEIPT_V1 feature and can send one exact old-incarnation delivery,
ingress, or KILL receipt query while NODE_REKEY fences ordinary traffic.
It waits for an empty TX ring, retains the query key, validates the matching
old identity, nonce, record IDs, phase, revocation state and digest in the
host response, and passes the result to dttransport. A mismatched response
fails closed; a temporary unavailable response is distinct from success.
The sanitized guest-driver fixture covers all three query types, ring
contention, reply mismatch, and daemon-queue failure. All 33 native port
suites pass, and the changed guest module source compiles for amd64, arm64
and RISC-V. Qualification: /usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/
rekey-commit-candidate/qualification.json.

The guest daemon still shuts down on DTT_VM_REKEY. It does not yet call the
durable journal-rekey helper, issue these queries, or perform the commit
ioctl. The candidate is not end-to-end restore support, remains separate
from experimental/hypertrace, and the production restore gate remains
EOPNOTSUPP. Live bhyve host/guest validation is still outstanding.

2026-10-15 isolated daemon startup-rekey candidate:
The candidate guest daemon now has a startup-only coordinator for a driver
identity already in REKEY_PENDING or REKEY_LOCAL_COMMITTED. Before starting
normal threads, it queries exact source delivery, ingress and KILL receipts,
uses the spool ELF digest for version-3 inbound records, durably rekeys the
paired journals, invokes DTT_COMMIT_VM_REKEY, waits for the distinct host
acknowledgement, and reloads journals before ordinary replay. A locally
committed restart checks pair completion and waits for that ACK without
repeating the commit. Recovered inbound delivery objects retain their
original durable session key after the VMID/epoch rewrite.

The source-extracted receipt, startup-coordinator and journal-key
sanitizer fixtures pass, including malformed nonce/digest, temporary unavailability, ordered
commit, and interrupted restart. All 36 native port suites pass. Changed
daemon sources compile for amd64, arm64 and RISC-V. The already-running
daemon still shuts down when it receives DTT_VM_REKEY; it needs a safe
quiescence/restart handoff to reach this startup path. Version-3 inbound
records without readable ELF bytes, and lifecycle states without a verifiable
host receipt, fail closed. No live host/guest restore test has passed. The
production restore gate remains EOPNOTSUPP and these candidate changes have
not been promoted to experimental/hypertrace. Qualification: /usr/obj/
5BSD-hypertrace-validation/rekey-commit-candidate/qualification.json.

The candidate listener now routes DTT_VM_REKEY through the existing traced-
child cleanup and re-exec path, so a fresh daemon reaches the startup
coordinator without relying on an external service restart. This path
compiles for amd64, arm64 and RISC-V, but has not run under a restored VM.
A queued ordinary dttransport event after the priority rekey notice still
causes startup to fail closed; no evidence authorizes discarding it.

The daemon receipt helper also retries a temporarily busy guest TX ring
against the same fixed deadline. This allows multiple journal receipts
while the driver worker reaps completed descriptors. The sanitized test
covers both a later successful write and deadline expiry; all 36 native
port suites pass after the change.

The candidate now records a SHA-256 digest in version-4 inbound delivery
journals. Startup can verify a terminal journal against an exact source
receipt even when its spool file was already removed; all other phases
require a readable spool that hashes to the durable digest. Version-3 journals without readable ELF
bytes continue to fail closed. A terminal version-4 inbound journal may
match a host terminal receipt with a zero digest and a revoked bit of zero
or one; this establishes cleanup state only and does not replay delivery.
The host, guest driver, and daemon validate that terminal receipt on the
old-incarnation ticketed query path. Startup drains driver-validated stale
receipts from earlier crashed attempts within its fixed 50-second deadline,
rejects malformed or unrelated events, and retries a temporarily unavailable
receipt within the same fixed deadline. All 36 native component suites pass,
and the changed host, guest module, and daemon transport sources compile
for amd64, arm64, and RISC-V. The test host still lacks the HyperTrace
device nodes, so no live restored VM has exercised this path; restore
remains gated with EOPNOTSUPP and this candidate is not yet promoted.

The rekey listener now kills traced children through the process
descriptors retained at launch, then waits for each descriptor to report
exit before re-exec with one shared ten-second deadline. A missing
descriptor, failed signal, or timed-out child stops the daemon instead
of risking a signal to a recycled PID or committing the destination
while an old child can still run. The source-extracted cleanup test
checks descriptor ownership, missing-descriptor failure, ESRCH, signal
failure, and deadline expiry. All 37 native suites pass, and the changed daemon
transport compiles for amd64, arm64, and RISC-V. Live child teardown
under a restored VM remains unqualified.

The destination restore transaction now contains the missing queued
delivery bind after ticket CLAIM and durable KILL binding, before daemon
admission or vCPU release. It retries the idempotent bind once if the
reply is uncertain. The changed snapshot coordinator compiles for amd64.
The earlier preflight EOPNOTSUPP remains in force because the VirtIO DTrace
device still does not install its planned queue during restore; this new
call is staged for that complete transaction and has not executed in a
live restore.

The VirtIO DTrace private restore handler now decodes the saved control
queue while the destination device is paused, plans it under the new VM
identity, and swaps the complete queue under the device lock. The first
control is NODE_REKEY and remains held until an exact handoff ticket is
attached; guest readiness and pending rekey state are reset. A source-
extracted device fixture exercises unpaused and malformed-image rejection,
atomic queue replacement, destination identity, and ticket wait. All 37
native suites pass, and the changed device source compiles for amd64,
arm64, and RISC-V. The earlier restore preflight and SAVE gates still
return EOPNOTSUPP; no live VM restore has qualified this handler.

The destination queue installer also rejects null metadata before
dereferencing it and refuses an existing rekey ACK, receipt reply, ticket
wait, or malformed inbound upload. These failure paths leave the old queue
and snapshot cursor untouched. The source-extracted fixture covers the
null metadata, stale inbound metadata, and pending-ticket cases; all 37
native suites pass, and the device source compiles for amd64, arm64, and
RISC-V. Live restore qualification is still outstanding.

The full restore coordinator no longer reruns ordinary device preflight
after its HyperTrace-aware preflight has completed. The transaction uses
the prevalidated device-restore loop; the public vm_restore_devices entry
retains its own preflight for independent callers. Previously the second
ordinary pass would reject the captured VirtIO DTrace image after the
destination restore transaction had already begun, even if the earlier
explicit EOPNOTSUPP gate were removed. The changed snapshot coordinator
compiles for amd64. The earlier gate remains, pending full transaction
qualification.

Packet provenance still has a trust-boundary gap: bhyve validates that a
guest-supplied mbuf ID is canonical, then passes it to tagged TAP, where
the kernel also accepts any canonical ID. A simple TX check that the ID's
host field equals the sending VM's ID would block a forged foreign origin,
but it would also discard a legitimate relayed packet whose mbuf retained
the first guest's origin ID. Mature cross-guest attribution needs a
host-verifiable origin or relay proof that survives forwarding, with the
added per-packet work measured before promotion. No such proof is present
in the current zero-padded ID wire format.

The isolated 256-byte mbuf sidecar now exposes disabled-by-default
`sdt:mbufid:ensure:{reuse,alloc,alloc-failure}` probes. They distinguish
an existing persistent tag from a new allocation and a failed allocation,
without adding counters or storage to every mbuf. The changed uipc_mbuf.c
compiles with -Werror for amd64, arm64, and RISC-V; each target object
contains all three probe definitions. This creates a measurement point for
live packet workloads, but does not establish acceptable throughput or
complete host-authenticated cross-guest provenance.

The `mbufid-sidecar` base DTrace script is now listed in share/dtrace's
installed SCRIPTS and reports reuse, allocation, and allocation-failure
counts over a workload interval without printing per-packet data. `make -V
SCRIPTS` includes it. The script still needs a loaded DTrace provider for
runtime validation; this host has no DTrace device, and neither counters nor
object compilation substitute for a throughput comparison.

The destination restore transaction now hashes the exact captured VirtIO
DTrace private image after preflight and compares it before device restore,
after device restore, and before the claimed ticket binds queued deliveries.
A changed image fails the transaction while vCPUs remain stopped instead of
using a different queue or receipt set under the preflight decision. The
source-extracted digest fixture covers byte mutation, length mutation,
recovery, and invalid input; all 38 native suites pass. The changed
snapshot coordinator compiles for amd64. The image remains a borrowed
read-only mapping, and live restore and adversarial concurrent file mutation
have not been exercised.

The isolated packet sidecar candidate now has a TAP-to-VM incarnation
binding ioctl backed by a read-only, capability-limited VMM attestation
descriptor. bhyve presents it before enabling packet tags, including on
restore paths that re-enable backend tagging. TAP clears the binding on
tag disable and close. The changed TAP driver and bhyve packet-backend
objects compile with -Werror on amd64, arm64, and RISC-V, and the
candidate amd64 bhyve links. This is
only the setup foundation for authenticated packet provenance: V1 packet
IDs are still canonical but forgeable, and the binding is not enforced on
packet I/O. The already-tagged restore path now verifies its cached binding
against a fresh VMM attestation and fails on an incarnation mismatch; it
cannot rebind that live framing mode. A distinct V2 wire format, kernel
stamp verification, relay and
restore tests, and live throughput measurement remain required. The bhyve
link initially consumed a stale pci_virtio_dtrace object from the main
worktree in a reused object directory; rebuilding that object from the
candidate source resolved the link, and the final link passed. The
current-tree native-port runner passed all 38 suites after the binding
change, including its packet-provenance and vtnet cases. Those fixtures
do not execute the new TAP ioctl inside a live kernel.

The candidate now defines a separate 48-byte V2 packet record and a
distinct control command/magic. Its first 32 bytes retain the V1 ID and
the remaining fields reserve a source-incarnation epoch and host stamp;
shape helpers distinguish an empty record, an unstamped local claim, and
a stamped claim without treating shape as authentication. The guest driver
and bhyve have cross-architecture size assertions, with if_vtnet.o
compiling for amd64, arm64, and RISC-V. The V2 command is deliberately
unhandled: there is no stamping key, TAP V2 mode, guest negotiation, or
verified relay path yet. V1 remains the only active wire protocol.

The V2 sidecar has now been implemented as a persistent optional mbuf tag;
upgrading a V1 tag preserves its ID and an allocation failure leaves the
V1 tag intact. Disabled-by-default SDT probes distinguish V2 reuse,
allocation, allocation failure, and upgrade, and the installed
mbufid-sidecar script reports them separately from V1 outcomes. A per-boot
kernel SipHash-2-4 key signs the 40-byte ID-and-epoch prefix with a
domain separator, with a constant-time stamp comparison. The HyperTrace
VM allocator publishes and revokes its incarnation epoch in an atomic
table so a future TAP V2 path can reject stale bindings without taking
DTrace configuration locks per packet. The changed mbuf code and
HyperTrace module build for amd64, arm64, and RISC-V. Native source-extracted
tests pass for stamp tampering, relay preservation, host-origin creation,
V1-to-V2 upgrade and OOM preservation, and VM allocator publication and
revocation. V2 remains inactive: TAP has no V2 mode or packet-policy
checks, guest/bhyve have no V2 negotiation, and there are no live traffic
or throughput results.

The kernel TAP endpoint now has a separate TAPSTAGGINGV2 mode. It requires a
bound VM incarnation still present in HyperTrace's atomic epoch table,
stamps fresh claims only for that VMID, verifies stamped relays with the
kernel key, and rejects malformed, altered, or stale records. A tagged
read either preserves a verified V2 tag or creates a newly stamped host
origin; an unstamped V1 tag cannot be promoted to a foreign-origin proof.
Mode changes refuse an in-flight V2 read/write, and close waits for those
operations before clearing the binding. The TAP object compiles with
-Werror on amd64, arm64, and RISC-V. Source-extracted TAP ingress tests use
the actual SipHash implementation and cover foreign fresh claims, a valid
relay from another VM, tampered stamps, revocation, and tag allocation
failure. All 41 native port suites pass. Bhyve and the guest still
negotiate only V1, so this new mode has no ordinary live users or throughput
qualification. Cross-host migration of stamped packets also lacks a
secure key or provenance handoff.

The bhyve TAP backend now exposes a separate V2 tag-enable operation and
tracks which framing mode it owns. Its disable path selects the matching
TAP ioctl, the Capsicum allowlist admits only that specific new command,
and the bounded peek buffer fits the 48-byte prefix. The changed backend
object compiles for amd64, arm64, and RISC-V and the amd64 bhyve links.
The source-extracted backend fixture verifies V2 enable/disable ioctl
selection and unsupported-backend rejection. VirtIO-net still requests
only V1; V2 cannot yet be activated through an ordinary guest control
command or restored from a VM snapshot.

The isolated candidate now negotiates authenticated V2 framing end to end:
the guest tries the distinct V2 control command before V1, preserves a
stamped 48-byte record across forwarding, and generates a fresh local claim
instead of promoting an unauthenticated V1 ID. Bhyve validates V2 record
shape in both directions, keeps the complete 48-byte prefix, and selects
the matching TAP backend mode. Snapshot format 4 records the V2 state and
rebinds TAP to the destination VM on restore; a nonempty staged V2 packet
is rejected because its stamp may belong to an older VM incarnation.
Kernel verification now checks the authenticated source epoch against the
live VM registry, so a cryptographically intact record from a revoked VM
is rejected. The guest and mbuf objects compile for amd64, arm64, and
RISC-V; bhyve links on amd64, and its changed backend objects compile on
arm64 and RISC-V. The 42 native port suites pass, including V2 guest ID
selection, bhyve shape rejection, TAP relay/revocation, and real SipHash
tests. This is still experimental: the current host lacks /dev/vmm,
/dev/dtrace/dtrace, and /dev/dttransport, so no live VM traffic, snapshot
resume, cross-host migration, or throughput result has been qualified.
Per-boot keys deliberately reject old stamps after a host change. The
guest now stores its current VirtIO-DTrace incarnation epoch in the optional
V2 mbuf tag, eight bytes beyond the wire record, and refreshes a copied
stamped tag as a fresh local claim after NODE_REKEY. This avoids enlarging
ordinary mbufs or changing the 48-byte V2 framing. Source-extracted tests
cover stale foreign relay tags, V1 upgrade, allocation failure, identity
assignment, rekey, and identity clear. Live snapshot/resume and migration
must still verify the guest rekey timing and packet behavior before
promotion; packets transmitted before rekey can fail closed at TAP.
The current source-hashed component evidence is under
/usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/packet-v2-incarnation-final-native-port/qualification.json.
